window.COMMERCE_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"commerce-occupations","old":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"Commerce consists of trade and the activities that assist trade. Trade is buying and selling goods and services. The aids to trade include banking, insurance, transport, warehousing, communication and advertising. Trade is the centre of commerce: the aids make exchange easier, safer and more regular. Commerce connects production with consumption; it does not mean manufacturing alone.\n\nAn occupation is a lawful activity through which a person earns a living. Industrial occupations obtain raw materials, change their form or construct things. Farming, mining, manufacturing and building are examples. Commercial occupations distribute goods and provide aids to trade. Retailers, wholesalers, bankers and transport operators perform commercial work. Service occupations provide personal or professional assistance. A teacher, doctor or lawyer gives a direct service to the person who needs it.\n\nDirect services satisfy wants directly. Commercial services assist the process of exchange. A lawyer advising a client is usually classified as giving a direct professional service in school Commerce. Do not classify every paid activity as trade merely because money changes hands. The question may be asking about the nature of the work, not its payment.\n\nCommerce developed as people produced surpluses and exchanged them. Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods. It is limited by the need for a double coincidence of wants: each trader must want what the other offers. Money removes much of that difficulty. Better roads, communication, security, banking and storage support larger markets and greater specialization.\n\nWorked example: a farmer grows maize, a mill turns it into flour, and a shop sells the flour. Growing is primary production, milling is secondary production, and the shop performs trade. The bank financing the shop supplies an aid to trade. Together these activities help consumers obtain a useful product.\n\nPublic services such as policing and law-making benefit society collectively and are traditionally called indirect services. Private professional assistance to an individual is a direct service.\n\nAdvertising expands awareness and demand, supporting trade; sustainable growth also depends on production, distribution, finance and wider conditions.\n\nCommerce can generate government revenue through lawful taxes and charges, helping finance public services and development.\n\nTrade creates interdependence between nations and can encourage cooperation and friendly relationships. It does not guarantee that every international disagreement disappears.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Commerce is wider than trade but is not the same as all production.\n• Trade is the buying-and-selling part; manufacturing changes the form of goods.\n• Commercial activity includes services, not only physical goods.\n• The reward earned does not determine the occupational category. Examine what the worker actually does."},"new":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"COMMERCE, TRADE AND OCCUPATIONS — HOW GOODS REACH THE CONSUMER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA farmer can produce a thousand bags of rice, yet families in another state may still be unable to buy them. Production alone does not guarantee that goods reach the people who need them. Commerce solves the problems of exchange, movement, storage, payment, information and risk. In JAMB, the key is to identify precisely what each business activity does.\n\n1. MEANING AND SCOPE OF COMMERCE\nCommerce is the branch of business activity concerned with the exchange and distribution of goods and services, together with the activities that facilitate exchange. Its two major divisions are TRADE and AIDS TO TRADE.\n\nTrade is buying and selling goods and services. A trader normally acquires goods for resale or connects buyers and sellers. A shopkeeper selling exercise books performs trade; the printer manufacturing them performs production.\n\nAids to trade are services that make trade possible, convenient or less risky:\n• Transport moves goods between places and creates place utility.\n• Warehousing stores goods until they are needed and creates time utility.\n• Banking provides payment services, savings facilities and credit.\n• Insurance provides financial protection against specified risks, subject to policy terms.\n• Advertising informs or persuades potential buyers.\n• Communication transfers orders, quotations, complaints and other commercial information.\n\nEXAMPLE — FROM KANO TO PORT HARCOURT\nA tomato farmer harvests tomatoes in Kano. A merchant buys them for resale (trade). A refrigerated vehicle carries them to Port Harcourt (transport). A bank transfers the purchase price (banking). A phone call confirms delivery arrangements (communication). An insurer may cover specified transit losses (insurance). A shop displays a price promotion (advertising). Each activity has a different function, even though they support the same transaction.\n\n2. COMMERCE, INDUSTRY AND BUSINESS: THE DIFFERENCE\nBusiness is the broad activity of producing or supplying goods and services, usually to earn income or meet an organisational objective. Industry is primarily concerned with extracting, processing, manufacturing or constructing goods. Commerce is concerned with exchange and distribution.\n\nA bakery making bread is engaged in secondary production. The same bakery selling bread directly to customers also undertakes trade. A business can therefore perform more than one function.\n\nProduction creates or increases usefulness (utility):\n• Form utility: changing raw materials into a more useful product, as when timber becomes furniture.\n• Place utility: making a product available where consumers want it, through transport.\n• Time utility: making a product available when needed, through storage.\n• Possession utility: transferring ownership or the right to use a product through exchange.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not say that commerce manufactures goods. Manufacturing is industrial production; commerce helps bring goods and services to the market.\n\n3. TRADE: HOME AND FOREIGN\nHome (domestic or internal) trade takes place within a country. It includes wholesale trade, which commonly involves purchasing in bulk and selling to retailers or other business users, and retail trade, which involves selling in smaller quantities to final consumers.\n\nForeign (international or external) trade crosses national borders:\n• Import trade: buying goods or services from abroad.\n• Export trade: selling goods or services to buyers abroad.\n• Entrepôt trade: importing goods and subsequently re-exporting them, usually with little or no substantial processing.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian dealer buying shoes from another Nigerian dealer conducts home trade. Buying shoes from Italy is import trade from Nigeria's perspective. Selling Nigerian cocoa to a buyer in the Netherlands is export trade.\n\nA wholesaler does not always sell only to retailers, and a retailer does not always operate from a physical shop. Classify the transaction by the function performed.\n\n4. OCCUPATION: MEANING AND MAJOR CLASSES\nAn occupation is a regular activity through which a person earns a livelihood. For school Commerce, occupations are commonly grouped into industrial, commercial and service occupations.\n\nINDUSTRIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve obtaining materials from nature, transforming them or constructing structures.\n• Extractive or primary: farming, fishing, mining, forestry.\n• Manufacturing or processing: milling rice, making soap, producing cement.\n• Construction: building bridges, houses and roads.\n\nCOMMERCIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve buying and selling or providing services that assist exchange: wholesalers, retailers, bankers, insurance workers, transporters, warehouse operators and advertising professionals.\n\nSERVICE OCCUPATIONS\nThese supply services rather than primarily producing or distributing physical goods. Direct or personal services are provided to identifiable clients, such as medical treatment, teaching, hairdressing or legal advice. Indirect or public services serve the wider community, such as public security and certain government administrative functions.\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Banking and transport are services in the broad economic sense, but school Commerce often groups them specifically under commercial occupations because they are aids to trade. A doctor treating a patient is ordinarily classified as providing a direct service, not as a trader merely because the patient pays.\n\n5. DIVISION OF LABOUR AND INTERDEPENDENCE\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular task or economic activity. As workers and firms specialise, they depend on others for goods and services they do not produce themselves.\n\nConsider a school uniform: a cotton grower provides raw material, a textile factory makes cloth, a tailor sews the uniform, a distributor transports it, and a shop sells it. No single occupation necessarily performs every step.\n\nSpecialisation can increase productivity and skill, but it also creates interdependence. If transport stops, a retailer may run out of stock even when the factory is still producing. Commerce connects specialised producers to consumers.\n\n6. HOW COMMERCE DEVELOPED\nIn subsistence economies, families produced much of what they consumed. As specialisation and surplus production increased, people exchanged goods.\n\nBARTER is the direct exchange of goods or services for other goods or services without money. Its difficulties include:\n• Double coincidence of wants: each party must want what the other offers.\n• Lack of a common measure of value: comparing unlike goods is difficult.\n• Indivisibility: a cow cannot always be divided conveniently to pay for a small item.\n• Difficulty storing some goods as wealth: perishable goods spoil.\n• Difficulty arranging deferred payments: agreeing what to repay later can be problematic.\n\nMoney developed as a generally accepted medium of exchange. Its principal functions are medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money makes exchange easier but does not eliminate scarcity or guarantee stable purchasing power.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nAda has a bag of beans and wants shoes. Tunde sells shoes but wants a bicycle, not beans. They cannot barter directly because their wants do not coincide. If Ada sells the beans for money, she can use the proceeds to buy shoes without needing the shoe seller to want beans.\n\n7. IMPORTANCE OF COMMERCE TO AN ECONOMY\nCommerce:\n• Connects producers with customers and widens markets.\n• Makes specialised production more practical.\n• Creates jobs in distribution, banking, insurance and related services.\n• Reduces some exchange risks and transaction difficulties.\n• Makes goods available at appropriate places and times.\n• Encourages investment and can increase business income.\n• Facilitates domestic and international trade.\n• Generates tax and other lawful government revenue.\n• May strengthen economic links between regions and countries.\n\nThese are potential contributions, not guarantees. Poor roads, insecurity, high transaction costs or weak institutions can prevent commerce from functioning effectively.\n\n8. COMPARISON GUIDE\nTrade versus commerce: trade is buying and selling; commerce includes trade plus aids to trade.\nIndustry versus commerce: industry obtains or transforms goods; commerce facilitates their exchange and distribution.\nWholesaler versus retailer: wholesalers generally sell in bulk to resellers or organisations; retailers generally sell to final consumers.\nDirect versus indirect services: direct services benefit identifiable clients; indirect/public services benefit the community collectively.\nBarter versus monetary exchange: barter swaps items directly; monetary exchange uses money as the intermediary.\n\n9. JAMB-STYLE APPLICATIONS\nQ1. A firm keeps bags of rice after harvest so they can be sold during the dry season. Which aid to trade is involved?\nAnswer: Warehousing. The main benefit is time utility.\n\nQ2. A furniture factory changes timber into tables. Is this commerce?\nAnswer: The transformation is secondary production. Selling the finished tables is trade.\n\nQ3. A lawyer advises a customer about a tenancy agreement. Which occupation is illustrated?\nAnswer: Direct professional service, even though the lawyer receives payment.\n\nQ4. A Nigerian company buys goods abroad and sells them to a third country without substantial processing. What is the trade called?\nAnswer: Entrepôt or re-export trade.\n\nQ5. Why does barter require a double coincidence of wants?\nAnswer: Each participant must simultaneously desire the item the other participant offers.\n\nQ6. What is the difference between place and time utility?\nAnswer: Transport creates place utility by changing location; warehousing creates time utility by changing when goods are available.\n\n10. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Commerce is broader than trade, but it is not synonymous with every kind of business activity.\n• Manufacturing is not an aid to trade; transport and banking are.\n• A paid occupation is not automatically trade.\n• A service can belong to a commercial occupation when its role is to assist trade.\n• Foreign trade is classified from the viewpoint of the country concerned: one country's export is another country's import.\n• Entrepôt trade involves re-export, not ordinary local retailing.\n• Warehousing does not create form utility; manufacturing commonly does.\n• Money reduces barter difficulties but does not remove the need for resources.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOMMERCE = TRADE + AIDS TO TRADE.\nAsk three questions about any activity: Is something being MADE, EXCHANGED, or HELPING EXCHANGE? That usually identifies industry, trade, or an aid to trade."}},{"id":"production-factors","old":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"Production is the creation of goods and services that satisfy human wants. It also creates or adds utility, meaning usefulness. A product is not fully useful to its consumer merely because it has left a factory. Transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility by keeping goods until they are needed.\n\nPrimary production obtains products from nature. Farming, fishing, forestry and mining are examples. Secondary production changes raw materials into more useful forms or constructs fixed structures. Manufacturing turns cotton into cloth; construction provides roads and buildings. Manufacturing can assemble factory components; construction assembles manufactured materials into usable works. A stem saying only “assembling products” needs context to distinguish these categories. Tertiary production provides services. Trade, banking, transport and direct professional services fall within the service sector.\n\nThe factors of production are land, labour, capital and enterprise. Land includes natural resources, not just soil. Its supply is relatively fixed from the economy's viewpoint, although particular land can be improved or reclaimed. Its reward is rent. Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production; its reward is wages or salaries. Workers can move between places or occupations, although training and family ties may restrict mobility.\n\nCapital means human-made resources used for further production. Machines, tools and factory buildings are capital goods. Money finances their purchase, but money is not itself a productive machine. The reward for using borrowed capital is interest. The entrepreneur organizes the other factors, makes business decisions and bears business risk. Profit is the reward for successful enterprise, while losses are possible.\n\nDivision of labour breaks production into separate tasks performed by different workers. Specialization means concentrating on a particular activity, product or area of work. Division of labour is one form of specialization. It can increase skill, save time and permit the use of machinery. However, repetitive work can cause boredom, and a failure at one stage can delay the whole process.\n\nWorked example: one worker cuts material, another sews and another checks finished school uniforms. This is division of labour. The machines are capital, the workers supply labour, and the owner who organizes production acts as entrepreneur.\n\nCapital goods are human-made production aids; intermediate goods are inputs used up or transformed in making other goods. In the economic long run, all factors can vary. Aptitude means natural ability for a particular occupation.\n\nFabrication forms or assembles materials into products; conversion changes their form. Use the particular transformation described when both terms appear among options.\n\nSpecialization can reduce craftsmanship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as manufacturing.\n• Capital goods assist further production; consumer goods satisfy wants directly.\n• The entrepreneur's main role is coordination and risk-bearing, not supplying every factor personally."},"new":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"PRODUCTION, FACTORS AND SPECIALISATION — HOW BUSINESSES CREATE VALUE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBefore goods can be bought and sold, somebody must make them available. Production, factors of production and division of labour explain how that happens. JAMB often presents a practical situation—a quarry, a tailoring workshop or a transport firm—and asks students to classify its activities, identify the relevant factor or explain the benefit or limitation of specialisation.\n\n1. MEANING OF PRODUCTION\nProduction is the creation or addition of utility through goods and services that satisfy human wants. Utility means usefulness or want-satisfying power. Production is not restricted to factory work: farming produces crops, a teacher provides education, and a transporter makes goods available in another place.\n\nFour useful forms of utility:\n• Form utility: processing or transforming materials, such as turning cassava into garri.\n• Place utility: moving goods to where they are wanted, such as transporting tomatoes from Jos to Lagos.\n• Time utility: storing goods until they are needed, such as keeping grain after harvest.\n• Possession utility: making ownership or the right to use goods available through exchange, such as selling a bicycle to a customer.\n\nBe careful: these describe different ways usefulness is added. A retailer may contribute possession utility without changing a product's physical form.\n\n2. STAGES OF PRODUCTION\nPRIMARY PRODUCTION obtains resources from nature. Examples include crop farming, fishing, forestry, mining and quarrying. Its output often supplies raw materials to other industries.\n\nSECONDARY PRODUCTION transforms raw materials or creates structures.\n• Manufacturing: cotton into cloth; cocoa beans into chocolate; metal into cooking pots.\n• Construction: building roads, bridges, houses and dams.\nManufacturing may include assembly. Construction is distinguished by producing structures or works, often fixed at their locations.\n\nTERTIARY PRODUCTION supplies services. It includes commercial services such as banking, transport, insurance, retailing and communication, as well as direct services such as medical treatment and education.\n\nPRODUCTION CHAIN — FOLLOW A BAG OF RICE\nFARMER (primary: grows paddy)\n  ↓\nRICE MILL (secondary: removes husk and processes rice)\n  ↓\nTRANSPORTER (tertiary: moves bags to market)\n  ↓\nWHOLESALER → RETAILER (tertiary trade)\n  ↓\nHOUSEHOLD (final consumer)\n\nThis is a simplified flow diagram, not a rule that every product must pass through every intermediary. A farmer can sell directly to consumers.\n\nJAMB APPLICATION: A firm that mines limestone performs primary production. A cement factory processing limestone performs secondary production. A lorry carrying cement to a dealer provides tertiary services.\n\n3. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION: THE FOUR INPUTS\nFactors of production are resources used to produce goods and services. The conventional school-Commerce classification is LAND, LABOUR, CAPITAL and ENTERPRISE.\n\nLAND\nLand means all natural resources used in production, not merely the ground beneath a building. It includes farmland, rivers, minerals, forests and natural fishing grounds.\nCharacteristics:\n• It is a gift of nature rather than a human-made capital good.\n• Its total natural supply is relatively fixed, though usable land can be improved, reclaimed or reallocated.\n• Its quality and location differ.\n• It is geographically immobile, although its use may change.\nReward: RENT, particularly payment for the use of land or natural resources.\n\nLABOUR\nLabour is human physical or mental effort used in producing goods and services. A mason, accountant, teacher and machine operator all supply labour.\nCharacteristics:\n• Labour is inseparable from the worker.\n• Skill, training, health and experience affect productivity.\n• Labour may move between occupations or places, but mobility is restricted by qualifications, language, family circumstances and other barriers.\n• Labour is perishable in the sense that an unused hour of labour cannot be stored for later.\nReward: WAGES or SALARIES.\n\nCAPITAL\nCapital consists of human-made resources used to produce other goods and services: machines, tools, production buildings, vehicles and equipment.\n• Fixed capital: durable productive assets used repeatedly, such as factory machinery.\n• Working or circulating capital: resources used up or regularly replaced in the production cycle, such as raw materials and supplies. In business finance, working capital also commonly means current assets minus current liabilities; do not confuse the two contexts.\n• Physical capital: productive equipment and structures.\n• Financial capital: funds available to acquire productive assets and finance operations.\n\nReward: INTEREST is conventionally associated with capital in factor-reward questions, especially lending. An owner may also earn other returns depending on how capital is employed.\n\nMoney is not itself a machine. It enables a business to buy machines and materials. Likewise, a bag of cement used to build a house is an intermediate input, while a cement mixer repeatedly used on projects is a capital good.\n\nENTERPRISE OR ENTREPRENEURSHIP\nEnterprise is the function of organising land, labour and capital, making decisions, innovating and bearing business risk.\nAn entrepreneur may:\n• Identify a market opportunity.\n• Combine resources and arrange finance.\n• Choose products, prices and production methods.\n• Hire workers and supervise operations.\n• Bear uncertainty about sales, costs and competition.\nReward: PROFIT when the venture succeeds; LOSS is possible when it fails.\n\nFACTORS AND REWARDS — MEMORY TABLE\nLand → Rent\nLabour → Wages or salaries\nCapital → Interest\nEnterprise → Profit (or possible loss)\n\nThese are conventional examination associations. In actual businesses, an individual may supply several factors and receive different types of income.\n\n4. FIXED CAPITAL, CONSUMER GOODS AND INTERMEDIATE GOODS\nA good's classification depends on HOW IT IS USED.\n• Consumer good: bought for direct satisfaction of wants, such as a family's refrigerator.\n• Capital good: used to produce or distribute goods and services, such as a refrigerator in a restaurant kitchen.\n• Intermediate good: used up or transformed in making another product, such as flour used by a bakery.\n\nA vehicle used for commercial deliveries is a capital good for the transport business; a family car used privately is primarily a consumer durable. The same physical object can therefore have different economic classifications.\n\n5. SPECIALISATION AND DIVISION OF LABOUR\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular job, skill, product or branch of production. It can occur among individuals, firms, regions and countries.\n\nDivision of labour means splitting a production process into distinct tasks assigned to different workers or groups. It is a form of specialisation, but not all specialisation is division of labour. A country specialising in cocoa exports does not necessarily divide a single factory process among workers.\n\nTAILORING WORKSHOP — TASK FLOW DIAGRAM\nMEASURE CUSTOMER\n  ↓\nCUT FABRIC\n  ↓\nSEW GARMENT\n  ↓\nATTACH BUTTONS / ZIP\n  ↓\nPRESS AND INSPECT\n  ↓\nDELIVER TO CUSTOMER\n\nIf different workers specialise in these stages, the workshop practises division of labour. If one tailor completes every stage, the process is not divided among workers, even if that tailor specialises in making uniforms.\n\n6. ADVANTAGES OF DIVISION OF LABOUR\n• Repetition can improve skill and speed.\n• Workers save time otherwise spent switching between tasks.\n• Specialised machines may be used efficiently.\n• Training for a narrow task may be faster.\n• Large-scale production can become easier.\n• Output per worker may rise, lowering unit cost.\n• Standardisation and quality checks can improve consistency.\n\nThese gains depend on good coordination, reliable materials and sufficient demand. Division of labour does not automatically increase quality or wages.\n\n7. DISADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS\n• Repetitive tasks may cause boredom and reduce motivation.\n• Workers may lose the ability to make an entire product.\n• A stoppage in one stage can interrupt the whole production line.\n• Highly specialised workers may struggle to change jobs.\n• Excessive dependence on machines or other workers can create vulnerability.\n• Work may become monotonous or reduce individual craftsmanship.\n\nSpecialisation is more effective when the market is large enough to support sustained demand, capital and equipment are available, workers can be trained, and transport and communication are reliable.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery with 30 workers can assign teams to mixing, baking, packaging and sales. A village bakery with only one worker and very few customers may find such a division impractical.\n\n8. SCALE OF PRODUCTION\nSmall-scale production generally uses fewer resources and serves a limited market. It can be flexible and close to customers but may face higher unit costs and limited finance.\n\nLarge-scale production uses greater productive capacity and may achieve economies of scale: reductions in average cost as output increases.\nInternal economies arise within a growing firm, such as bulk purchasing, specialised machinery or management expertise.\nExternal economies arise from advantages available to firms in an industry or location, such as better shared infrastructure or a pool of skilled workers.\n\nGrowth can also create DISECONOMIES OF SCALE, including coordination difficulties, communication delays and bureaucratic waste.\n\nWORKED COST EXAMPLE\nA workshop spends ₦120,000 producing 100 units.\nAverage cost = ₦120,000 ÷ 100 = ₦1,200 per unit.\nAfter better organisation, it spends ₦200,000 producing 200 units.\nAverage cost = ₦200,000 ÷ 200 = ₦1,000 per unit.\nAverage cost falls by ₦200 per unit. This illustrates a possible economy of scale, though lower average cost alone does not identify its precise cause.\n\n9. PRODUCTIVITY AND EFFICIENCY\nProductivity measures output relative to an input, often labour time or number of workers.\n\nLabour productivity = Total output ÷ Labour input.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA workshop produces 480 school bags with 12 workers over a comparable period.\nOutput per worker = 480 ÷ 12 = 40 bags.\nAfter training and improved tools, 12 workers produce 600 bags.\nNew output per worker = 600 ÷ 12 = 50 bags.\nIncrease = 10 bags per worker, or (10 ÷ 40) × 100 = 25%.\n\nProductivity is not the same as profitability. A factory can produce more units per worker but still lose money if costs rise sharply or customers refuse to buy its products.\n\n10. FACTOR MOBILITY AND LOCATION\nOccupational mobility is the ability of a factor—especially labour—to move from one job or use to another. Geographical mobility is movement between places. Training can improve occupational mobility; housing costs and family responsibilities can limit geographical mobility.\n\nLand cannot physically move from Enugu to Abuja, but land use can change. Capital equipment can often be relocated, although moving a large installed factory may be expensive. Entrepreneurs can transfer their skills and investment across activities.\n\n11. EXAMINATION DISTINCTIONS\nProduction versus manufacturing: production includes goods and services; manufacturing is one branch of secondary production.\nPrimary versus secondary: primary obtains resources from nature; secondary transforms materials or constructs works.\nCapital versus money: physical capital helps produce; money finances acquisition and payments.\nCapital good versus intermediate good: capital goods serve repeatedly; intermediate goods are used up or transformed.\nSpecialisation versus division of labour: specialisation is concentration; division of labour separates stages of one process.\nProductivity versus profit: productivity compares output to inputs; profit compares revenue to costs.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE WITH EXPLANATIONS\nQ1. A firm extracts granite from a quarry. What stage of production is this?\nAnswer: Primary production. It obtains a natural resource.\n\nQ2. A company builds a bridge. What stage of production?\nAnswer: Secondary production, specifically construction.\n\nQ3. Which factor of production receives profit as its conventional reward?\nAnswer: Enterprise. The entrepreneur organises resources and bears risk.\n\nQ4. A business buys a delivery van to distribute products. How is the van classified?\nAnswer: A capital good, because it supports ongoing business operations.\n\nQ5. A bakery uses flour to make bread. Is the flour a capital good or intermediate good?\nAnswer: Intermediate good, because it is transformed into the final product.\n\nQ6. A factory assigns cutting, stitching and inspection to different workers. What arrangement is shown?\nAnswer: Division of labour.\n\nQ7. Why can division of labour reduce unit costs?\nAnswer: Specialisation may improve speed, reduce switching time and permit efficient use of equipment.\n\nQ8. What is a major risk of extreme division of labour?\nAnswer: A breakdown at one stage can interrupt other stages; repetitive work can also cause boredom.\n\nQ9. Twelve workers produce 360 units in a day. What is output per worker?\nAnswer: 360 ÷ 12 = 30 units per worker per day.\n\nQ10. A machine is used for five years in production. Is it normally fixed or circulating capital?\nAnswer: Fixed capital, because it is a durable productive asset.\n\n13. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Tertiary production includes trade and professional services, not only transportation.\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as factory manufacturing.\n• The reward of land is rent; do not confuse it with the purchase price of land.\n• Money is financial capital in business usage but is not itself physical productive machinery.\n• The entrepreneur need not personally own every factor of production.\n• Division of labour is a form of specialisation, not an exact synonym for all specialisation.\n• Higher output does not necessarily mean higher productivity if inputs increased even faster.\n• Higher productivity does not guarantee profit.\n• An item may be a consumer good in one use and a capital good in another.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPRODUCTION: Primary → Secondary → Tertiary.\nFACTORS: Land → Rent; Labour → Wages; Capital → Interest; Enterprise → Profit.\nDIVISION OF LABOUR: Different people perform different stages of one process."}},{"id":"enterprise-location","old":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities."},"new":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently tests the difference between a sensible location factor and an absolute rule, and between horizontal, vertical and conglomerate combinations.\n\n1. STARTING A BUSINESS\nIdentify demand, test feasibility, calculate expected costs and revenues, secure skills and finance, comply with registration and permits, then plan operations. Registration alone does not establish commercial viability.\n\n2. LOCATION IS INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC\nA heavy cement works may locate near limestone to cut bulky-input transport costs. A neighbourhood pharmacy prioritizes customers, accessibility and licensing. A data-service firm may prioritize electricity, connectivity and skills. Compare input weight, market proximity, infrastructure, labour, security, environmental rules and future expansion. No single factor always wins.\n\n3. BUSINESS GROWTH\nInternal growth expands existing operations. Horizontal integration combines firms at the same production/distribution stage (two competing supermarkets). Vertical integration joins linked stages (flour mill and bakery). Conglomerate integration joins unrelated industries (bakery and insurance company).\n\n4. COOPERATION IS NOT ALWAYS A MERGER\nJoint venture: parties collaborate on a specific venture, often sharing risk and resources. Consortium: organisations cooperate on a large undertaking while typically retaining separate identities. Cartel: independent competitors coordinate competition-sensitive matters such as prices, output or markets. Mere cooperation does not by itself prove a cartel; the anti-competitive coordination matters.\n\n5. HOLDING COMPANY AND RETRENCHMENT\nA holding company controls another through sufficient voting power; it need not own every share. Retrenchment cuts activities or costs, while diversification enters new lines. Consolidation brings operations together; it is not automatically diversification.\n\n6. EXAM SCENARIOS\nTwo competing banks combine → horizontal. A cocoa processor acquires a cocoa farm → vertical. A bank buys a furniture company → conglomerate. Several builders bid together for one airport project → consortium or joint venture, depending on their agreement; not automatically cartel.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• 'Near raw materials' is not universally the best location.\n• Vertical = different linked stages; horizontal = same stage.\n• A consortium is not defined merely as any voluntary association.\n• A joint venture does not require permanent merger.\n• A cartel needs evidence of coordination that restricts competition."}},{"id":"sole-trade-partnership","old":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company."},"new":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nExams contrast ownership, management, liability and the legal documents used by small businesses. Keep those four questions separate.\n\n1. SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP\nOne owner controls the business, takes residual profit and normally bears unlimited personal liability. Advantages: easy formation, quick decisions, confidentiality and direct incentive. Disadvantages: limited finance, heavy workload, exposure to loss and continuity risk.\n\n2. PARTNERSHIP BASICS\nPartners carry on a business in common with a view to profit. They may combine skills and capital. In an ordinary general partnership, partners may have extensive personal liability and mutual agency; a partner can bind the firm in the ordinary course of its business, subject to applicable law and circumstances. Limited partnership arrangements differ.\n\n3. TYPES OF PARTNER\nActive partner participates in management. Sleeping/dormant partner contributes but stays out of daily management; this does not automatically erase liability. Nominal partner permits their name to be associated with the firm, typically without the ordinary financial participation of a full partner; liability to third parties can arise from holding out. A minor's position depends on the governing law—never assume a minor can freely contract as a full adult partner.\n\n4. PARTNERSHIP DEED\nThe deed records agreed capital contributions, profit sharing, drawings, duties, dispute handling and admission or retirement. It is an agreement between partners, not a company prospectus or share certificate. Absence of a written deed does not always mean no partnership exists.\n\n5. WORKED EXAMPLE\nAmaka runs the shop daily; Bello invests and does not manage. Amaka is active, Bello is sleeping. If Bello's name appears on the door, that fact alone does not make him nominal: his actual investment and rights still matter.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Who usually makes decisions alone in a sole trade? A: The sole proprietor.\nQ: Does a sleeping partner necessarily have limited liability? A: No.\nQ: Which document states agreed profit-sharing ratios? A: Partnership deed.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• One owner ≠ one employee: a sole trader may employ many people.\n• Sleeping ≠ nominal; management inactivity does not define nominal status.\n• A partnership deed does not incorporate the firm.\n• Sharing profits is relevant evidence but does not alone settle every legal question about partnership."}},{"id":"companies-liquidation","old":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims."},"new":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nThis topic combines company identity, capital terminology, shareholders versus creditors and liquidation priorities. Precise vocabulary matters more than memorizing a single list.\n\n1. INCORPORATION AND COMPANY TYPES\nIncorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. A private company and a public company differ in how they may offer securities and in legal requirements. Being public does not automatically mean stock-exchange listing. The Corporate Affairs Commission handles incorporation; an exchange handles listing under its rules.\n\n2. OWNERS AND CREDITORS\nOrdinary shareholders hold residual ownership interests and usually receive variable dividends when declared. Preference shares may carry dividend or capital priorities, subject to their terms. Debenture holders are creditors entitled to contractual repayment/interest; they do not become shareholders merely by lending.\n\n3. CAPITAL LANGUAGE — DO NOT SUBSTITUTE TERMS\nAuthorised/nominal capital is the traditional maximum share capital stated in the constitution in older textbooks; modern Nigerian company law has changed this framework. Issued capital represents shares actually issued. Unissued capital is the portion not issued. Called-up capital is the amount requested on issued shares; paid-up capital is what shareholders have actually paid. Reserve capital traditionally refers to a portion of uncalled capital set aside for winding up, NOT simply the unissued portion.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA historical exam says 5 million shares of 50 kobo each are available, and 80% are issued. Traditional nominal value total = ₦2.5 million; issued nominal capital = ₦2 million; unissued nominal portion = ₦500,000. Calling ₦500,000 'reserve capital' without an additional reserve resolution is technically inaccurate.\n\n4. MEMORANDUM, ARTICLES AND ULTRA VIRES\nThe memorandum traditionally sets constitutional fundamentals; articles govern internal rules. Ultra vires historically meant beyond the company's stated powers. Modern legal effects differ from older textbook statements, so use the paper's historical context and avoid treating every older doctrine as current Nigerian law.\n\n5. WINDING UP\nLiquidation realizes assets, settles valid claims and distributes any remainder. A liquidator administers the process; an auditor examines financial statements. Liquidation does not always mean fraud or that a business never had value. Creditors and statutory priorities generally precede ordinary shareholders; secured interests and liquidation expenses affect the order.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Is a debenture holder a shareholder? A: No, a creditor.\nQ: Does a PLC automatically trade on an exchange? A: No.\nQ: Is unissued capital automatically reserve capital? A: No.\nQ: Who administers winding up? A: Liquidator.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• Incorporation ≠ listing.\n• Limited liability protects members within its legal scope; it does not cancel company debts.\n• Debenture interest ≠ share dividend.\n• Unissued, uncalled, called-up and paid-up capital are different.\n• Liquidator ≠ auditor; shareholders are not first in line for assets."}},{"id":"cooperatives-public-enterprises","old":{"title":"Cooperatives, public enterprises and privatization","body":"A cooperative society is a voluntary association formed to meet members' common economic needs through a jointly owned undertaking. Typical features include democratic control and service to members. Voting commonly follows one member, one vote rather than the number of shares held. A cooperative can earn a surplus, but member service is central to its purpose.\n\nA consumer or retail cooperative buys goods for members and reduces the margin otherwise earned by outside middlemen. A producers' cooperative helps members produce or market their output. A credit and thrift society encourages saving and provides loans to members under agreed rules. Credit is funded by members' savings and other permitted resources; it is not free money without repayment obligations.\n\nCooperatives can provide access to supplies, credit and a stronger bargaining position. Problems may include limited capital, weak management, unpaid loans and low participation. Equal voting is a control principle, not proof that all members make identical purchases or receive an identical patronage refund.\n\nA public enterprise is owned or controlled by government. It may provide essential services, infrastructure, strategic goods or employment, and can pursue social objectives alongside financial ones. Its problems can include political interference, weak accountability and dependence on subsidies. Public ownership does not by itself establish that an enterprise must operate inefficiently.\n\nPrivatization transfers government ownership or equity interests to private investors. Commercialization reorganizes a public enterprise to operate on commercial principles and improve financial self-sufficiency, without necessarily transferring government ownership. Deregulation reduces direct restrictions or controls and permits greater market competition. These are related policies but not identical actions.\n\nIn Nigerian historical questions, the Bureau of Public Enterprises implements privatization and commercialization programmes, while the National Council on Privatization provides high-level policy oversight. Do not confuse the implementing agency with the highest policy-making body. Earlier papers may mention predecessor arrangements, so the question's date matters.\n\nA core investor combines a substantial investment with the managerial or technical capacity expected for the enterprise; money alone is not the whole idea.\n\nWorked example: if government retains ownership but requires an enterprise to cover costs through revenue, that is commercialization. If it sells its shares to private investors, that is privatization. Removing entry restrictions on competitors is deregulation.\n\nA cooperative patronage refund commonly follows the volume of members’ purchases or dealings, not equal amounts for every voter. Thrift concerns regular saving.\n\nNITEL’s public ownership supports a public-enterprise classification; “Limited” and “public corporation” still require care about its actual legal form.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Privatization concerns ownership, not simply raising prices.\n• Commercialization does not automatically mean a sale.\n• Cooperative voting and company voting should not be assumed to follow the same rule."},"new":{"title":"Cooperatives, public enterprises and privatization","body":"COOPERATIVES, PUBLIC ENTERPRISES AND PRIVATISATION — WHO OWNS AND CONTROLS THE BUSINESS?\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA group of farmers may pool money to buy fertiliser, while government may establish an organisation to provide public infrastructure. Both are business organisations, but their ownership, objectives, control and sources of finance differ. JAMB tests these distinctions and often asks students to identify a cooperative type or distinguish privatisation from commercialisation.\n\n1. COOPERATIVE SOCIETY: MEANING\nA cooperative is a voluntary association of persons who join together to meet common economic or social needs through an enterprise they jointly own and democratically control. The purpose is primarily to serve members, although a cooperative must still manage its finances responsibly.\n\nTypical cooperative principles include voluntary and open membership, democratic member control, member economic participation, autonomy, education and training, cooperation among cooperatives and concern for the community. In many primary cooperatives, the voting principle is ONE MEMBER, ONE VOTE rather than one vote per share.\n\nEXAMPLE: Twenty market women contribute savings to establish a bulk-purchasing society. It buys rice directly from suppliers and distributes it to members on agreed terms. The society exists chiefly to improve members' access to goods, not to maximise profits for outside shareholders.\n\n2. TYPES OF COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES\nCONSUMERS' COOPERATIVE: Members combine purchasing power to buy goods in bulk, reduce distribution costs and supply members at reasonable prices. Example: civil servants jointly operating a grocery shop.\n\nPRODUCERS' COOPERATIVE: Small producers share equipment, inputs or production facilities. Example: cassava processors buying and operating a shared grating machine.\n\nMARKETING COOPERATIVE: Members pool produce for grading, storage, transport and sale, improving bargaining power. Example: cocoa farmers marketing their harvest collectively.\n\nCREDIT AND THRIFT / SAVINGS COOPERATIVE: Members save regularly and obtain loans under agreed conditions. Loans are not gifts; borrowers must meet repayment rules.\n\nMULTIPURPOSE COOPERATIVE: Combines several activities such as savings, lending, purchasing and marketing.\n\nHOUSING COOPERATIVE: Members organise to acquire land, finance or develop housing.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A society that chiefly lends money from pooled member savings is a credit and thrift cooperative, not a consumers' cooperative merely because its members are consumers.\n\n3. HOW A COOPERATIVE IS ORGANISED\nThe general meeting of members normally exercises ultimate democratic control within the law and the society's rules. An elected management committee oversees activities, while appointed staff may handle daily operations. The society's constitution or bye-laws set membership conditions, contributions, elections, meetings, use of surplus and dispute procedures.\n\nSOURCES OF FINANCE\n• Members' shares and subscriptions.\n• Regular savings and contributions.\n• Retained surplus from operations.\n• Permitted loans and grants.\n• Other lawful financing arrangements.\n\nA cooperative surplus may be retained for development, used to support members or distributed according to applicable rules, often considering patronage. Equal voting rights do not mean that every member must receive the same amount of surplus.\n\n4. ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS\nAdvantages:\n• Collective bargaining can secure better buying or selling terms.\n• Members may access affordable credit or shared equipment.\n• Democratic participation encourages member involvement.\n• Small producers can achieve some benefits of large-scale operation.\n• Shared risks and training may improve resilience.\n\nLimitations:\n• Capital may be insufficient for expansion.\n• Weak record-keeping or dishonest management can cause losses.\n• Loan defaults may undermine a credit society.\n• Disagreement among members can slow decisions.\n• Poor attendance and limited managerial expertise may reduce performance.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A cooperative buys 100 bags of fertiliser for ₦3,000,000. Average purchase cost is ₦30,000 per bag before transport and administration. If individual members would otherwise pay ₦34,000 each, the gross saving is ₦4,000 per bag, but the net benefit must allow for cooperative operating costs.\n\n5. PUBLIC ENTERPRISES\nA public enterprise is an organisation owned or controlled by government to provide goods or services, implement policy or operate in a strategic sector. It may be established under a specific law or organised as a government-controlled company, depending on the jurisdiction.\n\nPossible objectives include:\n• Providing essential infrastructure and services.\n• Serving areas or groups that private businesses might neglect.\n• Supporting economic development and employment.\n• Protecting strategic interests.\n• Generating revenue where commercially appropriate.\n\nPublic ownership does not automatically mean services are free, and a public enterprise does not necessarily operate at a loss.\n\n6. SOURCES OF FINANCE AND CONTROL OF PUBLIC ENTERPRISES\nPublic enterprises may receive government equity, budgetary allocations, internally generated revenue, loans, grants and other lawful funds. The exact arrangement depends on their legal form.\n\nGovernment may appoint governing boards, set policy objectives and require public reporting. Managers handle operations subject to the enterprise's governing rules.\n\nPotential strengths include large-scale financing, continuity of essential services and pursuit of public objectives. Common challenges include political interference, bureaucratic delays, weak incentives, poor maintenance, overstaffing and financial losses. These are possible problems, not inevitable features of every public organisation.\n\n7. PRIVATISATION, COMMERCIALISATION AND NATIONALISATION\nPRIVATISATION means transferring government ownership or controlling interest in an enterprise or asset to private owners. It may be full or partial.\n\nCOMMERCIALISATION means operating an enterprise on a more commercial basis, with greater emphasis on cost recovery, efficiency and revenue generation. Government ownership can remain unchanged.\n\nNATIONALISATION means transferring private ownership of an enterprise or industry to government.\n\nDEREGULATION means reducing or removing certain government restrictions on market entry, prices or operations. Deregulation is not identical to privatisation: private and public firms may operate in a deregulated market.\n\nCOMPARISON\nPrivatisation → change in ownership.\nCommercialisation → change in operating approach.\nNationalisation → ownership moves toward the state.\nDeregulation → change in regulatory restrictions.\n\n8. REASONS AND RISKS OF REFORM\nGovernments may privatise to raise proceeds, attract investment, reduce the burden of funding loss-making enterprises or encourage efficiency. Commercialisation may aim to improve service quality, accountability and financial sustainability.\n\nPossible risks include job losses, higher prices, reduced access for poorer consumers, private monopoly and inadequate oversight. Outcomes depend on competition, regulation, contracts and management.\n\nEXAMPLE: If government retains 100% ownership of a water company but directs it to cover more of its costs from customer payments, that is commercialisation rather than privatisation. If government sells a controlling shareholding to private investors, ownership has been privatised.\n\n9. COOPERATIVE VERSUS PUBLIC ENTERPRISE\nOwnership: cooperative → members; public enterprise → government.\nControl: cooperative → democratic member structures; public enterprise → public authorities and appointed governance bodies.\nMain focus: cooperative → member needs; public enterprise → public-policy and/or commercial objectives.\nFinance: cooperative → member contributions and permitted borrowing; public enterprise → public funds, operating revenue and permitted borrowing.\nSurplus: cooperative → retained or applied for members under rules; public enterprise → used according to its legal and financial arrangements.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. A society enables farmers to sell produce together at better prices. Which type? Answer: Marketing cooperative.\nQ2. Which principle commonly distinguishes a primary cooperative from a share company? Answer: One member, one vote.\nQ3. A group pools savings and grants repayable loans to members. Which cooperative? Answer: Credit and thrift.\nQ4. Government retains ownership but requires a public corporation to cover operating costs. What reform? Answer: Commercialisation.\nQ5. Government sells its controlling interest to private investors. What reform? Answer: Privatisation.\nQ6. Private firms are brought under state ownership. What is this called? Answer: Nationalisation.\nQ7. Why might a cooperative obtain cheaper supplies? Answer: Collective bulk purchasing and stronger bargaining power.\nQ8. Name one risk of poor cooperative management. Answer: Misuse of funds, weak records or loan defaults.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• A cooperative serves members but still needs sound financial management.\n• One member, one vote concerns governance, not equal financial contributions.\n• Credit and thrift loans must normally be repaid.\n• Public ownership does not mean every service is free.\n• Privatisation changes ownership; commercialisation need not.\n• Deregulation and privatisation are different policies.\n• Nationalisation moves ownership toward government, not away from it.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOOPERATIVE = MEMBERS OWN AND CONTROL.\nPUBLIC ENTERPRISE = GOVERNMENT OWNS OR CONTROLS.\nPRIVATISATION = SELL OWNERSHIP; COMMERCIALISATION = OPERATE COMMERCIALLY."}},{"id":"business-management","old":{"title":"Management functions and organizational structure","body":"Management is the process of planning, organizing, directing and controlling resources to achieve organizational objectives. Resources include people, money, materials, machines, information and business opportunities. Effective management achieves the intended objective; efficient management avoids unnecessary waste in doing so.\n\nPlanning decides what to achieve and how to achieve it. Organizing groups tasks, establishes departments and assigns authority. Staffing obtains, places and develops suitable workers. Directing guides people in carrying out their duties. Motivation encourages the willingness and effort needed for performance. Coordinating brings related activities into harmony. Controlling compares actual results with plans and arranges corrective action.\n\nAuthority is the right to give instructions and make decisions within an assigned role. Responsibility is the duty to carry out a task. Accountability is being answerable for performance. Delegation assigns duties and sufficient authority to a subordinate, but the manager remains answerable for the work of the unit. Delegation is not simply abandoning supervision.\n\nAn organizational chart shows positions and formal reporting relationships. A line structure has a direct chain of authority. A line-and-staff structure combines line managers with specialists who advise them. In a functional structure, specialist authority is organized around particular functions. A matrix structure combines functional and project relationships, which can create more than one reporting line.\n\nSpan of control is the number of subordinates directly supervised by a manager. A narrow span can permit close supervision but may increase the number of management levels. A wide span can reduce levels but may overload a supervisor. Unity of command means receiving instructions from one immediate superior for a particular responsibility; it should not be confused with having only one department.\n\nWorked example: the sales manager sets a monthly target, allocates territories and checks actual sales. Setting the target is planning; assigning territories is organizing; comparing sales with the target is controlling. Encouraging effort through recognition and suitable rewards concerns motivation.\n\nDelegation depends on a superior’s judgment about the task and subordinate, but accountability is not abandoned.\n\nA simple line structure can suit a small enterprise because direct authority makes decisions and responsibility clear. Suitability still depends on size, specialist needs and complexity; it is not a universal rule for every medium-sized firm.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Recruitment finds candidates; staffing is the wider personnel function.\n• Organizing is about jobs, departments and authority, not merely keeping an office tidy.\n• A staff adviser does not automatically possess every line manager's command authority.\n• Motivation affects willingness and intensity of effort; it is not identical to planning."},"new":{"title":"Management functions and organizational structure","body":"BUSINESS MANAGEMENT AND ORGANISATIONAL STRUCTURE — MAKING PEOPLE AND RESOURCES WORK TOGETHER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA business may have workers, machines and money yet fail because tasks are poorly planned or nobody knows who makes decisions. Management coordinates these resources. JAMB tests the functions of management, authority and responsibility, organisational charts, delegation, leadership and motivation.\n\n1. MEANING OF MANAGEMENT\nManagement is the process of planning, organising, staffing, directing, coordinating and controlling people and other resources to achieve organisational goals.\n\nEFFECTIVENESS means achieving the intended result. EFFICIENCY means achieving results with minimal unnecessary waste of time, money and materials.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop planned to deliver 100 orders by Friday. Delivering all 100 achieves the target (effectiveness). Doing so without avoidable overtime, damaged stock or unnecessary transport costs improves efficiency.\n\n2. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGEMENT\nPLANNING: Deciding objectives in advance and choosing how to achieve them. A school-uniform maker forecasts demand, budgets for cloth and sets a production schedule.\n\nORGANISING: Dividing work into tasks, grouping activities and allocating resources and authority. A firm establishes purchasing, production, sales and accounts units.\n\nSTAFFING: Recruiting, selecting, placing, training and developing suitable employees. A firm hires qualified machine operators.\n\nDIRECTING OR LEADING: Guiding, communicating with and supervising workers so they understand what to do.\n\nMOTIVATING: Encouraging effort through fair pay, recognition, growth opportunities and suitable working conditions.\n\nCOORDINATING: Ensuring that different units work together. Purchasing must supply cloth before production can sew uniforms.\n\nCONTROLLING: Setting standards, measuring actual performance, comparing results with targets and correcting deviations.\n\nMANAGEMENT CONTROL CYCLE\nSET TARGET → MEASURE RESULTS → COMPARE WITH TARGET → CORRECT PROBLEMS → REVIEW.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A bakery plans to produce 500 loaves daily but makes 400. Management checks whether the problem is labour absence, power supply, machine failure or poor planning before taking corrective action. Simply blaming staff is not effective control.\n\n3. LEVELS OF MANAGEMENT\nTOP MANAGEMENT sets broad policy and long-term direction. Examples: board and senior executives.\nMIDDLE MANAGEMENT translates policy into departmental plans and supervises units. Examples: production and sales managers.\nLOWER OR SUPERVISORY MANAGEMENT oversees day-to-day tasks. Examples: shift supervisors and team leaders.\n\nThe exact titles differ across businesses. The important distinction is the scope of decisions and responsibilities.\n\n4. AUTHORITY, RESPONSIBILITY AND ACCOUNTABILITY\nAUTHORITY is the legitimate right to make decisions, issue instructions and use resources within an assigned role.\nRESPONSIBILITY is the duty to perform assigned work.\nACCOUNTABILITY is the obligation to explain and answer for results.\n\nDELEGATION occurs when a manager assigns a task and appropriate authority to a subordinate. The subordinate becomes responsible for carrying it out, but the manager retains overall accountability for the unit's work.\n\nEXAMPLE: A branch manager authorises a cashier to approve refunds up to ₦10,000. The cashier has limited delegated authority; refunds beyond that limit require higher approval.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Delegation is not abdication. A manager does not cease to be accountable simply because a subordinate performs the task.\n\n5. ORGANISATIONAL STRUCTURE\nAn organisational structure shows how duties, reporting relationships and decision-making powers are arranged.\n\nA simple line structure follows a direct chain of command:\nMANAGING DIRECTOR\n  ↓\nBRANCH MANAGER\n  ↓\nSUPERVISOR\n  ↓\nSALES STAFF\n\nA functional structure groups specialists into departments such as finance, operations, human resources and marketing.\n\nLINE AUTHORITY is the direct authority of a manager over subordinates in the chain of command.\nSTAFF AUTHORITY is specialist advice or support, such as legal or human-resource advice. Staff specialists do not automatically command all line departments.\n\nSPAN OF CONTROL is the number of subordinates directly reporting to a manager. A wide span means many direct reports; a narrow span means fewer.\n\nCHAIN OF COMMAND is the formal route through which instructions and reports pass.\nUNITY OF COMMAND means that, under the classical principle, a worker should receive orders from one immediate superior to avoid conflicting instructions.\n\n6. CENTRALISATION AND DECENTRALISATION\nCENTRALISATION concentrates important decisions at higher levels of management.\nDECENTRALISATION distributes decision-making authority to lower levels or branches.\n\nCentralisation can support consistency and control but may delay local decisions. Decentralisation can speed responses and encourage initiative but requires effective supervision and coordination.\n\nEXAMPLE: If all discounts require approval from headquarters, pricing decisions are centralised. If branch managers may set discounts within limits, some authority is decentralised.\n\n7. LEADERSHIP STYLES\nAUTOCRATIC: The leader makes decisions with little subordinate participation. It can be fast in emergencies but may reduce employee involvement.\nDEMOCRATIC: The leader consults staff and encourages participation. It can improve commitment but may take longer.\nLAISSEZ-FAIRE: Workers receive considerable freedom. It may suit experienced specialists but can lead to weak coordination when roles are unclear.\n\nNo style is always best. Effectiveness depends on urgency, staff competence and the task.\n\n8. MOTIVATION AND COMMUNICATION\nMotivation is the willingness to exert effort toward organisational goals. Financial incentives include wages, bonuses and allowances. Non-financial incentives include recognition, promotion opportunities, training, job security and good working conditions.\n\nCOMMUNICATION is the exchange of information and understanding.\n• Downward communication: manager to subordinate.\n• Upward communication: subordinate to manager.\n• Horizontal communication: between colleagues or units at similar levels.\n• Formal communication: follows official channels.\n• Informal communication: occurs through unofficial social networks.\n\nBarriers include noise, unclear language, poor listening, delayed feedback and information overload.\n\n9. COORDINATION, SUPERVISION AND CONTROL\nCoordination links related tasks; supervision guides day-to-day work; control measures performance against standards.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A retail chain advertises a product without informing its warehouse. Customers arrive, but the product is unavailable. The immediate problem is poor coordination between marketing and stock control. The remedy includes shared inventory information and approval procedures.\n\n10. BUSINESS ETHICS AND MANAGEMENT\nManagers should consider honest accounting, customer safety, fair treatment of employees, compliance with law and responsible use of company assets. A profitable decision is not necessarily ethical or lawful.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which function compares actual performance with planned targets? Answer: Controlling.\nQ2. A manager assigns a task but remains answerable for the result. What process? Answer: Delegation.\nQ3. What is span of control? Answer: Number of direct subordinates reporting to a manager.\nQ4. A worker receives conflicting instructions from two supervisors. Which classical principle is affected? Answer: Unity of command.\nQ5. Branch managers may approve local purchases. What management arrangement? Answer: Decentralisation.\nQ6. A manager invites staff contributions before deciding. What leadership style? Answer: Democratic.\nQ7. What communication direction describes a worker reporting a fault to the manager? Answer: Upward.\nQ8. A firm meets sales targets but wastes excessive materials. Is it necessarily efficient? Answer: No. It may be effective but inefficient.\nQ9. Who normally develops broad long-term strategy? Answer: Top management.\nQ10. What function brings departments into harmony? Answer: Coordinating.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Planning precedes action; controlling checks results and corrects deviations.\n• Authority is a right; responsibility is a duty; accountability means answerability.\n• Delegation does not erase a manager's accountability.\n• Staff specialists advise; they do not automatically possess line command.\n• Span of control concerns direct reports, not total company employees.\n• Centralisation is about where decisions are made, not the number of offices.\n• Motivation includes non-financial incentives.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPLAN → ORGANISE → STAFF → DIRECT → COORDINATE → CONTROL.\nAUTHORITY = RIGHT; RESPONSIBILITY = DUTY; ACCOUNTABILITY = ANSWERABILITY."}},{"id":"business-finance","old":{"title":"Sources of finance and forms of capital","body":"Business finance is money obtained and used to establish, operate or expand a business. A suitable source depends on the purpose, amount, cost, repayment period, security required and effect on control. Long-term assets should not normally depend entirely on a loan that must be repaid immediately.\n\nInternal finance is generated within an existing business, such as retained profit and proceeds from selling unwanted business assets. External finance comes from outside the business, including new share subscriptions, loans, debentures and trade credit. Personal savings can finance a sole trader, but a shareholder's private savings are not retained earnings generated by an incorporated company.\n\nShort-term sources include trade credit, bank overdrafts and accrued tax payable. Tax accruals temporarily retain cash until lawful settlement; tax itself is not income or permission to pay late. An overdraft permits withdrawals beyond an agreed current-account balance, subject to the bank's limit and terms. A bank loan provides a specified sum with repayment conditions. Leasing obtains the use of an asset in return for rental payments without necessarily buying it. Retained earnings are internal, long-term funds, though they can finance working capital.\n\nFixed capital finances durable assets used repeatedly, such as buildings and machinery. Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. Current assets include stock, debtors, bank and cash when realizable within the operating cycle. Current liabilities are obligations due within that cycle or the relevant short-term period. Owners' equity is assets less liabilities; for a company it includes paid-up share capital and accumulated reserves, subject to other equity items.\n\nHistorical school questions distinguish authorized or nominal capital, issued capital, called-up capital and paid-up capital. Authorized capital was the maximum permitted share capital under the older constitutional framework. Issued capital is the amount offered and allotted; called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares; paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. Modern Nigerian company law uses an issued-share-capital framework, so do not treat an old authorized-capital rule as unchanged present law.\n\nWorked example: current assets of ₦300,000 and current liabilities of ₦180,000 give working capital of ₦120,000. A shareholder loan is a liability, not automatically equity merely because its lender owns shares.\n\nCapital employed is commonly calculated as total assets less current liabilities, or fixed assets plus working capital. Distinguish it from owners’ equity and check whether the question includes long-term borrowing.\n\nA mortgage loan or bond is secured against property. Lenders commonly require security worth more than the loan to allow for recovery risk.\n\nEquivalently, add owners’ equity and long-term debt.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Bank borrowing is not retained profit.\n• Working capital is not all assets or total cash alone.\n• A debenture holder is a lender; a shareholder is an owner."},"new":{"title":"Sources of finance and forms of capital","body":"BUSINESS FINANCE AND CAPITAL — CHOOSING THE RIGHT MONEY FOR THE RIGHT PURPOSE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA profitable business can fail because it lacks cash when bills fall due. A growing business can also become overburdened by borrowing. JAMB examines sources of finance, types of capital, time periods, ownership and borrowing, and the difference between liquidity and profitability.\n\n1. MEANING OF BUSINESS FINANCE\nBusiness finance concerns obtaining, managing and using funds to start, operate and expand an enterprise. Businesses need finance for premises, machinery, raw materials, wages, electricity, stock, advertising and emergencies.\n\nThe appropriate source depends on the amount required, purpose, repayment period, cost, available security, risk and whether owners want to retain control.\n\nMATCH THE TERM TO THE NEED\nShort-term need: paying for stock that will sell next month.\nMedium-term need: acquiring a delivery van.\nLong-term need: building a new factory.\n\nUsing an overdraft repayable on demand to fund a factory that will generate returns over ten years creates a maturity mismatch and may cause financial distress.\n\n2. INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL SOURCES\nINTERNAL FINANCE originates from resources already within the business:\n• Retained earnings: profit kept in the business rather than distributed.\n• Sale of surplus or unwanted assets.\n• Cash released through better inventory or receivables management.\n\nEXTERNAL FINANCE comes from outside:\n• Additional owner contributions or share issues.\n• Bank loans and overdrafts.\n• Trade credit from suppliers.\n• Debentures or other debt securities.\n• Leasing and hire purchase arrangements.\n• Permitted grants or investment from outside financiers.\n\nA sole trader's personal savings may finance the business, but those savings are not retained earnings earned by an existing company.\n\n3. FINANCE BY DURATION\nSHORT-TERM FINANCE is generally repayable within about one year.\nExamples: trade credit, bank overdraft, short-term loan and some accrued expenses.\n\nMEDIUM-TERM FINANCE commonly covers more than one year up to several years.\nExamples: term loans, leasing and hire purchase, depending on contract length.\n\nLONG-TERM FINANCE is intended for major lasting investment.\nExamples: equity shares, long-term debentures, long-term loans and retained earnings.\n\nThese are useful classroom categories; exact year boundaries may vary by textbook and financing agreement.\n\n4. OWNER'S CAPITAL VERSUS BORROWED CAPITAL\nEQUITY or OWNERSHIP FINANCE comes from owners who bear business risk and generally share in residual profits. A company can raise equity by issuing shares. Ordinary shareholders usually have voting rights subject to company law and share class.\n\nDEBT FINANCE creates a contractual obligation to repay principal and usually pay interest. Examples include loans and debentures. Lenders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide money.\n\nCOMPARISON\nEquity: ownership interest; dividends are not automatically guaranteed; no ordinary fixed maturity for share capital; may dilute existing owners' control.\nDebt: creditor relationship; interest and repayment obligations according to terms; may require security; excessive borrowing increases financial risk.\n\n5. BANK OVERDRAFT AND BANK LOAN\nOVERDRAFT: A bank allows a current-account holder to withdraw beyond the account balance up to an agreed limit. It is usually suited to short-term fluctuating cash needs, and charges apply under the agreement.\n\nBANK LOAN: A specified sum is advanced for an agreed period with repayment terms. Loans may be secured or unsecured.\n\nEXAMPLE: A retailer expecting customer payments in two weeks may use a short-term overdraft for stock. A transport business buying a vehicle may prefer a term loan with repayments spread over the asset's useful period.\n\n6. TRADE CREDIT, HIRE PURCHASE AND LEASING\nTRADE CREDIT: A supplier allows the buyer to receive goods now and pay later. It supports working capital but late payment can damage supplier relationships or attract penalties.\n\nHIRE PURCHASE: A customer obtains possession of goods and pays by instalments; ownership usually transfers after the required conditions and final payment are satisfied. The exact legal position depends on the agreement.\n\nLEASING: A business pays for the right to use an asset for an agreed period without necessarily acquiring ownership. Leasing can reduce the immediate cash needed to use equipment.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Possession is not the same as ownership. In a typical hire-purchase arrangement, the hirer uses the item before title passes.\n\n7. TYPES OF CAPITAL\nFIXED CAPITAL: Long-lived productive assets such as factory buildings, machines and commercial vehicles.\n\nWORKING CAPITAL: In financial accounting, current assets minus current liabilities. It helps assess resources available for day-to-day operations.\n\nCURRENT ASSETS may include cash, inventories and trade receivables.\nCURRENT LIABILITIES may include trade payables and short-term debts.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nCash ₦80,000 + Inventory ₦220,000 + Receivables ₦100,000 = Current assets ₦400,000.\nTrade payables ₦150,000 + short-term loan ₦50,000 = Current liabilities ₦200,000.\nWorking capital = ₦400,000 − ₦200,000 = ₦200,000.\n\nIn production economics, circulating capital may refer to raw materials and inputs consumed in the production process. Do not confuse this usage with the accounting formula for net working capital.\n\n8. AUTHORISED, ISSUED, CALLED-UP AND PAID-UP SHARE CAPITAL\nAUTHORISED SHARE CAPITAL is a traditional textbook term for the maximum nominal share capital a company was permitted to issue under its constitutional documents. Modern Nigerian company law has moved away from the old authorised-share-capital framework toward minimum issued share capital; examination questions may still use the traditional vocabulary.\n\nISSUED SHARE CAPITAL: Shares allotted or issued to shareholders.\nCALLED-UP CAPITAL: The amount the company has required shareholders to pay on issued shares.\nPAID-UP CAPITAL: The amount actually paid by shareholders.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA company issues 10,000 shares with nominal value ₦2 each.\nNominal issued capital = 10,000 × ₦2 = ₦20,000.\nIf the company calls ₦1.50 per share, called-up capital = ₦15,000.\nIf shareholders have paid ₦1.20 per share, paid-up capital = ₦12,000.\nUnpaid calls = ₦15,000 − ₦12,000 = ₦3,000.\n\n9. LIQUIDITY, SOLVENCY AND PROFITABILITY\nLIQUIDITY means ability to meet short-term obligations when due.\nSOLVENCY concerns ability to meet overall obligations over the longer term.\nPROFITABILITY measures earnings relative to sales, investment or resources.\n\nA business may report profit while waiting months for customers to pay. It can then struggle to pay suppliers despite being profitable on paper.\n\nCURRENT RATIO = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities.\nUsing the earlier figures: ₦400,000 ÷ ₦200,000 = 2:1.\n\nA current ratio of 2:1 is not universally ideal; the appropriate level depends on the industry and quality of assets.\n\n10. COST OF FINANCE AND FINANCIAL RISK\nBorrowing can allow expansion without surrendering ownership, but interest must usually be paid even when sales disappoint. Equity may avoid fixed interest obligations but can dilute control and require sharing future returns.\n\nA prudent manager considers:\n• Effective borrowing cost and fees.\n• Timing of repayments.\n• Expected cash inflows.\n• Asset life.\n• Security or collateral requirements.\n• Sensitivity to changing sales and interest rates.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Profit retained for business expansion is what source? Answer: Internal finance, specifically retained earnings.\nQ2. A bank permits withdrawals above an agreed current-account balance. What facility? Answer: Overdraft.\nQ3. Goods are supplied now with payment due in 30 days. What source? Answer: Trade credit.\nQ4. What is the principal difference between shareholders and debenture holders? Answer: Owners versus creditors.\nQ5. Current assets ₦600,000 and current liabilities ₦250,000. Working capital? Answer: ₦350,000.\nQ6. Using those figures, current ratio? Answer: 600,000 ÷ 250,000 = 2.4:1.\nQ7. Which arrangement usually gives possession before eventual ownership on completion of instalments? Answer: Hire purchase.\nQ8. Is an accounting profit the same as cash available in the bank? Answer: No.\nQ9. Which source may dilute existing shareholders' ownership? Answer: New equity shares.\nQ10. What financing risk arises when a long-term asset is funded entirely by an overdraft? Answer: Short-term repayment may fall due before the asset generates sufficient cash.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Working capital = current assets MINUS current liabilities, not total fixed assets.\n• An overdraft differs from a term loan.\n• Interest belongs to borrowing; dividends are returns on shares when declared.\n• Hire purchase possession does not necessarily mean immediate ownership.\n• Profit and liquidity are different.\n• Old authorised-share-capital terminology must be distinguished from current Nigerian legal requirements.\n• Retained earnings belong to an existing business; personal savings are a separate source.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSHORT-TERM NEED → SHORT-TERM FINANCE.\nLONG-TERM ASSET → SUITABLE LONG-TERM FUNDING.\nWORKING CAPITAL = CURRENT ASSETS − CURRENT LIABILITIES."}},{"id":"trade-retailing-wholesaling","old":{"title":"Home trade, retailers and wholesalers","body":"Home trade is buying and selling within a country. It includes wholesale and retail trade. Wholesale trade normally involves buying in large quantities and selling to retailers or other business users. Retail trade sells goods to final consumers, usually in smaller quantities. The customer's purpose matters as well as the size of the purchase.\n\nWholesalers break bulk, store goods, provide market information, bear some trading risks and may give credit to retailers. They help producers reach numerous buyers without managing every small transaction. Retailers assemble goods conveniently for consumers, explain products, provide local access and may offer credit or after-sales service. Both wholesalers and retailers can break bulk at different stages.\n\nA merchant wholesaler takes title to the goods traded and bears the risks of ownership. An agent generally arranges sales for another person and earns commission without necessarily owning the goods. A broker brings parties together. A factor may sell goods entrusted to the factor and often has possession of them. Do not assume that every intermediary is both owner and agent.\n\nSmall retail outlets include market stalls, kiosks, hawkers and unit shops. A multiple or chain store has branches under common ownership and control. A department store has several departments under one roof, each dealing in a product range. A supermarket emphasizes self-service, while a shopping centre can contain separately owned businesses. Mail-order and online retailing permit purchases without visiting a conventional shop.\n\nMiddlemen may be bypassed when large retailers buy directly, producers sell online or goods are too perishable to pass through many stages. However, eliminating a wholesaler does not eliminate storage, transport, financing or distribution. Someone else must perform the necessary functions and bear their costs.\n\nWorked example: a drinks manufacturer supplies a wholesaler with 500 crates; the wholesaler supplies ten shops with smaller lots; each shop sells bottles to families. This is producer–wholesaler–retailer–consumer distribution. A supermarket buying directly removes one intermediary but still needs delivery and stock management.\n\nHire purchase lets a consumer use goods while paying instalments, with ownership passing according to the agreement, usually after the final payment. This is different from ordinary cash buying; failure to meet terms can lead to repossession under the applicable rules.\n\nManufacturers determine actual product characteristics through materials and production control. Sales regulations impose quality requirements; they do not manufacture the goods.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A chain store is not simply one shop with many departments.\n• A merchant wholesaler owns goods; an agent is not defined by ownership.\n• Raising prices artificially is not a productive service justifying a wholesaler.\n• A shorter channel is not always cheaper once all necessary functions are considered."},"new":{"title":"Home trade, retailers and wholesalers","body":"HOME TRADE, WHOLESALERS AND RETAILERS — HOW GOODS MOVE THROUGH THE MARKET\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA bag of flour can travel from a manufacturer to a wholesaler, then a retailer and finally a household. Sometimes the manufacturer sells directly online instead. JAMB tests the functions of intermediaries, channels of distribution, types of retailing and circumstances in which middlemen can be eliminated.\n\n1. HOME TRADE\nHome or internal trade is buying and selling within the boundaries of a country. It is commonly divided into wholesale and retail trade.\n\nWHOLESALE TRADE generally involves buying in large quantities from producers or other suppliers and selling to retailers, institutions or other business buyers.\n\nRETAIL TRADE generally involves selling goods or services to final consumers for personal or household use, usually in smaller quantities.\n\nThe classification depends largely on the customer's role and the purpose of the transaction, not only on how many items are bought.\n\nEXAMPLE: A restaurant purchasing 50 bags of rice for its kitchen may buy through wholesale channels. A household buying one bag from a neighbourhood shop is engaging in retail trade.\n\n2. CHANNELS OF DISTRIBUTION\nA channel of distribution is the route through which goods pass from producers to users.\n\nCOMMON CHANNELS\nProducer → Consumer.\nProducer → Retailer → Consumer.\nProducer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\nProducer → Agent → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\n\nA longer channel is not automatically worse. It may be useful when producers need wide geographic coverage or when many small retailers require regular supply.\n\nDIRECT DISTRIBUTION occurs when producers sell straight to consumers. INDIRECT DISTRIBUTION involves one or more intermediaries.\n\n3. FUNCTIONS OF A WHOLESALER\nA wholesaler may:\n• Buy goods in bulk from manufacturers.\n• Break bulk into quantities suitable for retailers.\n• Store goods and smooth seasonal supply.\n• Finance some distribution by buying ahead of retail sales.\n• Provide trade credit to retailers.\n• Bear ownership and storage risks where goods are purchased outright.\n• Supply market information to producers.\n• Sort, grade, package or assemble goods from different sources.\n• Arrange transportation and delivery.\n\nEXAMPLE: A beverage wholesaler buys 2,000 cartons from a factory and supplies 20 shops with 100 cartons each. The wholesaler breaks bulk and saves the manufacturer from handling many small orders.\n\n4. FUNCTIONS OF A RETAILER\nRetailers:\n• Make goods accessible near consumers.\n• Sell in convenient quantities.\n• Assemble assortments from different producers.\n• Explain products and advise buyers.\n• Display goods and advertise locally.\n• Offer selected credit, delivery or after-sales service.\n• Provide consumer feedback to suppliers.\n• Hold stock for immediate purchase.\n\nA retailer may also break bulk. It is incorrect to say that only wholesalers do so.\n\n5. TYPES OF WHOLESALERS AND INTERMEDIARIES\nMERCHANT WHOLESALER: Buys goods, takes ownership and resells at a margin.\n\nAGENT: Acts on behalf of a principal and may receive commission. An agent does not necessarily take ownership of the goods.\n\nBROKER: Brings buyers and sellers together and helps arrange a transaction, often for commission.\n\nFACTOR: Traditionally a commercial agent entrusted with possession of goods for sale and sometimes authorised to sell in the agent's own name. The exact powers depend on the arrangement.\n\nMANUFACTURERS' AGENT: Represents producers in securing sales.\n\nJAMB TRAP: An agent arranging a sale is not necessarily the owner of the goods. Ownership and agency are separate questions.\n\n6. TYPES OF RETAIL OUTLETS\nSMALL-SCALE RETAILING:\n• Market stall or kiosk.\n• Hawking or mobile vending.\n• Neighbourhood shop.\n• Speciality shop serving a narrow product category.\n\nLARGE-SCALE OR ORGANISED RETAILING:\n• Supermarket: self-service store, often with food and household items.\n• Department store: different departments selling varied product groups.\n• Chain or multiple shops: branches under common ownership.\n• Shopping centre: a complex housing different independent or chain retailers.\n• Hypermarket: very large store combining supermarket and general merchandise features.\n\nNON-STORE RETAILING:\n• Online and mobile commerce.\n• Mail-order selling.\n• Vending machines.\n• Door-to-door or direct selling.\n\nA shopping centre is not itself necessarily a department store: one contains several businesses; the other is usually one retailer divided into departments.\n\n7. ELIMINATION OF THE MIDDLEMAN\nDisintermediation means removing one or more intermediaries from a distribution channel.\n\nWhy it may happen:\n• Producers operate their own stores or websites.\n• Large retailers buy directly from factories.\n• Digital platforms connect producers and consumers.\n• Goods are perishable and need rapid delivery.\n• Buyers place sufficiently large orders.\n\nPossible advantages: fewer margins, closer customer contact and faster feedback.\nPossible disadvantages: producers must handle storage, transport, customer service and small transactions themselves.\n\nEXAMPLE: A poultry farm sells eggs directly to households using its own delivery van. It eliminates a wholesaler, but now bears delivery and order-processing costs.\n\n8. FACTORS AFFECTING CHANNEL CHOICE\n• Nature of goods: perishability, bulk and fragility.\n• Market size and geographic spread.\n• Availability and cost of intermediaries.\n• Producer's financial and storage capacity.\n• Desired control over pricing and customer experience.\n• Frequency and size of orders.\n• Competition and delivery speed.\n\nPerishable vegetables may favour shorter, faster channels. Durable packaged goods may pass through multiple intermediaries.\n\n9. PRICING AND TRADE MARGINS\nA trade margin is the difference between selling price and purchase cost before accounting for other operating expenses.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA retailer buys a carton for ₦18,000 and sells it for ₦22,000.\nGross margin amount = ₦22,000 − ₦18,000 = ₦4,000.\nGross margin on selling price = ₦4,000 ÷ ₦22,000 × 100 ≈ 18.18%.\nMarkup on cost = ₦4,000 ÷ ₦18,000 × 100 ≈ 22.22%.\n\nMargin and markup are not identical because they use different denominators. The ₦4,000 is not necessarily net profit; rent, transport and other expenses may remain.\n\n10. MODERN RETAIL AND CONSUMER PROTECTION\nRetailers increasingly combine physical shops, online ordering, electronic payments and delivery services. Regardless of the channel, honest descriptions, accurate pricing, safe products and appropriate handling of customer complaints matter.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Who usually buys in bulk and sells to retailers? Answer: Wholesaler.\nQ2. Who commonly sells to final consumers? Answer: Retailer.\nQ3. What does breaking bulk mean? Answer: Dividing large quantities into smaller saleable lots.\nQ4. A person introduces a buyer to a seller for commission without taking ownership. What role? Answer: Broker or agent, depending on the stated authority.\nQ5. A producer sells directly through its website. What happens to the channel? Answer: It becomes more direct; an intermediary may be eliminated.\nQ6. What retail outlet typically uses self-service for groceries and household goods? Answer: Supermarket.\nQ7. A single company owns 30 similar stores. What arrangement? Answer: Chain or multiple shops.\nQ8. A trader buys for ₦8,000 and sells for ₦10,000. Gross margin amount? Answer: ₦2,000.\nQ9. Is that ₦2,000 automatically net profit? Answer: No, operating expenses must still be considered.\nQ10. Why may a producer retain wholesalers? Answer: Bulk purchasing, storage, wider market coverage and reduced handling of small orders.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Wholesale and retail are classified by their commercial function, not solely quantity.\n• Both wholesalers and retailers can break bulk.\n• Agents need not own the goods they sell for principals.\n• A shopping centre differs from a department store.\n• Removing intermediaries does not remove distribution costs.\n• Markup uses cost as denominator; gross margin percentage uses selling price.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nWHOLESALER → BULK, STORAGE, RETAILER SUPPLY.\nRETAILER → FINAL CONSUMER, CONVENIENCE, ASSORTMENT.\nCHANNEL = ROUTE FROM PRODUCER TO USER."}},{"id":"trade-documents-payments","old":{"title":"Buying procedures, trade documents and credit instruments","body":"A normal buying procedure begins with an enquiry about goods and terms. The seller may reply with a quotation, price list or catalogue. The buyer sends an order specifying goods required. An invoice states the goods supplied, quantities, prices, discounts and amount payable. A delivery note accompanies the goods and helps the receiver check delivery; it is not proof that the invoice has been paid.\n\nA pro forma invoice gives details and prices before a completed sale, often to support an advance payment or an import arrangement. It is not an ordinary demand for payment for goods already delivered. A statement of account summarizes transactions and the balance owing over a period. A receipt acknowledges payment. An internal requisition requests goods or purchasing action within an organization.\n\nA credit note reduces the buyer's debt, for example when goods are returned or an overcharge is corrected. A debit note can request or record an additional charge, according to the transaction. Always check whose books and whose document the question describes. E and OE means errors and omissions excepted: accidental mistakes may be corrected; it is not a licence for fraud.\n\nA cheque is an order by an account holder directing a bank to pay a stated sum. A bill of exchange is an unconditional written order directing another person to pay a specified amount at an agreed time. A promissory note is a written promise by its maker to pay. Acceptance indicates the drawee's agreement to pay a bill. Discounting a bill means obtaining money before maturity, less a charge, from a willing financial institution.\n\nCredit permits goods or funds to be obtained now with payment later. It can increase sales and enable production before cash is received. However, it creates collection costs, default risk and possible overborrowing. Cash transactions avoid some credit risk but may restrict purchasing power.\n\nWorked example: a shop receives an invoice for ₦80,000, returns damaged goods worth ₦5,000 and receives a credit note. The invoice balance becomes ₦75,000 before any other payment or adjustment.\n\nA negotiable instrument can transfer payment rights by the legally required delivery or endorsement, subject to its form and rules. Cash with order, CWO, requires payment when ordering; cash on delivery requires it when goods arrive.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An invoice records an amount charged; a receipt records payment.\n• An order requests goods; a requisition is generally an internal request.\n• A promise to pay and an order to pay are different credit instruments."},"new":{"title":"Buying procedures, trade documents and credit instruments","body":"TRADE DOCUMENTS, BUYING PROCEDURES AND PAYMENT INSTRUMENTS — FOLLOW THE PAPER TRAIL\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently asks students to identify the document used at a particular stage of a sale. An invoice, quotation, receipt and statement of account may contain similar details, but they serve different purposes. The safest method is to follow the transaction in chronological order and ask what each document proves.\n\n1. THE NORMAL BUYING AND SELLING PROCEDURE\nA typical transaction may proceed as follows:\nENQUIRY → QUOTATION → ORDER → DELIVERY → INVOICE → PAYMENT → RECEIPT.\n\nThis is a teaching sequence, not a rigid legal requirement. Some sellers issue invoices before delivery, request advance payment or use electronic order confirmations.\n\nENQUIRY: Buyer asks about price, availability, quality or delivery terms.\nQUOTATION: Seller communicates a proposed price and conditions for specified goods or services.\nORDER: Buyer specifies the goods or services requested, quantity and terms.\nDELIVERY NOTE: Accompanies delivered goods and helps the buyer check what arrived.\nINVOICE: Seller's bill showing goods supplied, quantities, prices, discounts, charges and amount payable.\nRECEIPT: Acknowledges that payment has been received.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A school asks a stationery supplier for the price of 100 exercise books (enquiry). The supplier responds with prices and delivery terms (quotation). The school confirms its purchase (order). The books arrive with a delivery note. The supplier issues an invoice. After payment, the supplier issues a receipt.\n\n2. PRE-SALE DOCUMENTS\nLETTER OF ENQUIRY: Formal request for information about products and terms.\n\nPRICE LIST: General list of products and prices; unlike a tailored quotation, it may not address one buyer's specific request.\n\nCATALOGUE: Describes available products, often with specifications and illustrations.\n\nQUOTATION: Proposed price and terms for a particular supply. Its legal effect depends on wording and circumstances; a quotation is not always a binding contract by itself.\n\nTENDER: A formal offer submitted in response to an invitation to bid, commonly for substantial supplies or projects.\n\nPRO FORMA INVOICE: A preliminary document resembling an invoice, issued before a completed sale or shipment to indicate expected goods, prices and terms. It may support advance payment, import procedures or financing.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A pro forma invoice is not ordinarily proof that goods have already been supplied.\n\n3. DOCUMENTS DURING AND AFTER DELIVERY\nPURCHASE ORDER: Buyer's formal instruction to supply specified goods.\n\nDELIVERY NOTE: Details goods delivered; signed acknowledgement may confirm receipt of items, but does not itself prove payment.\n\nINVOICE: Records the seller's claim for the price of goods or services supplied under the transaction.\n\nSTATEMENT OF ACCOUNT: Periodic summary of transactions, payments, credits and outstanding balance between seller and buyer.\n\nRECEIPT: Evidence that payment has been received.\n\nGOODS RECEIVED NOTE: Internal record confirming goods received and checked by a business.\n\nREQUISITION: Internal request for stock or purchasing action; it is not the same as an order sent to an outside supplier.\n\n4. CREDIT NOTE AND DEBIT NOTE\nCREDIT NOTE: Commonly issued by a seller to reduce the amount owed by a buyer, such as when goods are returned, a price is reduced or an overcharge is corrected.\n\nDEBIT NOTE: Can notify a party of an additional charge or adjustment, depending on which party issues it and the accounting context. Some textbook examples use a debit note from a buyer to notify a supplier of returned goods. Always read who issued the document and why.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nInvoice total = ₦75,000.\nBuyer returns damaged goods valued at ₦10,000.\nSeller issues credit note = ₦10,000.\nAdjusted amount due = ₦75,000 − ₦10,000 = ₦65,000.\n\nThe credit note is not the same as a receipt. It reduces a debt; a receipt acknowledges payment.\n\n5. E. & O.E. AND TERMS OF SALE\nE. & O.E. stands for Errors and Omissions Excepted. It indicates that accidental errors or omissions in a document may be corrected. It is not permission to deceive buyers or ignore contractual obligations.\n\nCASH SALE: Payment occurs at or around purchase.\nCREDIT SALE: Buyer is allowed to pay later under agreed terms.\nC.O.D.: Cash on delivery, meaning payment is made when goods are delivered.\nC.I.F.: Cost, Insurance and Freight, a trade term commonly used in international shipping; responsibilities are governed by the applicable Incoterms rules and agreement.\nF.O.B.: Free on Board, another international trade term specifying responsibilities at the named port under applicable rules.\n\nDo not confuse a document's purpose with the timing of payment.\n\n6. TRADE DISCOUNTS AND CASH DISCOUNTS\nTRADE DISCOUNT is a reduction from a listed price, commonly offered to traders or for quantity purchases. It is usually deducted in calculating the invoice price.\n\nCASH DISCOUNT encourages prompt payment under specified terms.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nList price = ₦100,000.\nTrade discount 10% = ₦10,000.\nNet invoice price = ₦90,000.\nIf a further 5% cash discount is allowed for prompt payment, it is calculated on ₦90,000:\nCash discount = ₦4,500.\nAmount paid = ₦85,500.\n\nDo not add 10% and 5% and simply deduct 15% from the original price: successive discounts use different bases.\n\n7. PAYMENT INSTRUMENTS\nCASH: Notes and coins accepted for immediate payment.\n\nCHEQUE: A written order directing a bank to pay a stated sum from the drawer's account to the payee or bearer, subject to applicable rules.\n\nBANK TRANSFER: Electronic movement of funds between accounts.\n\nBANK DRAFT: A payment instrument issued by a bank, generally involving the bank's undertaking under its terms.\n\nDIRECT DEBIT: Authorised collection of payment from a payer's account, often for recurring bills.\n\nSTANDING ORDER: Instruction from an account holder to the bank to make regular payments of a specified amount or under specified arrangements.\n\nDEBIT CARD: Payment generally draws on available funds in the linked account, subject to account arrangements.\nCREDIT CARD: Payment is made using a credit facility and repaid under agreed terms.\n\n8. PARTIES TO A CHEQUE\nDRAWER: Person who writes and signs the cheque.\nDRAWEE: Bank directed to pay.\nPAYEE: Person entitled to receive payment.\n\nEXAMPLE: Ada writes a cheque from her account at Bank A to pay Chidi. Ada is the drawer; Bank A is the drawee; Chidi is the payee.\n\n9. CROSSING AND ENDORSEMENT OF CHEQUES\nA crossed cheque has markings that generally direct payment through a bank account rather than cashing over the counter, subject to applicable rules.\n\nA general crossing commonly uses two parallel lines; a special crossing names a bank. Words such as 'account payee' further restrict how the cheque should be collected.\n\nEndorsement is signing or otherwise properly transferring certain negotiable instruments. Not every crossed or account-payee cheque can be freely transferred.\n\n10. BILLS OF EXCHANGE AND PROMISSORY NOTES\nBILL OF EXCHANGE: A written unconditional order by one party directing another to pay a certain sum to a specified person or bearer on demand or at a determinable future time, subject to legal requirements.\n\nPROMISSORY NOTE: A written unconditional promise by the maker to pay a certain sum to a specified person or bearer under the relevant law.\n\nKEY DISTINCTION: A bill orders someone else to pay; a promissory note contains the maker's promise to pay.\n\nParties commonly described:\nBill: drawer, drawee, payee; the drawee may become acceptor.\nPromissory note: maker and payee.\n\n11. TRADE CREDIT AND PAYMENT RISK\nCredit sales can increase sales but create risk of late payment or default. Businesses may check customer creditworthiness, set credit limits, agree due dates, issue statements and follow up overdue accounts.\n\nElectronic payments reduce the need for cash handling but require accurate account details, transaction verification and fraud controls.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which document is a preliminary statement of expected charges before a completed sale? Answer: Pro forma invoice.\nQ2. Which document acknowledges payment? Answer: Receipt.\nQ3. Which document normally reduces the amount a buyer owes after returning goods? Answer: Credit note from the seller.\nQ4. Which document summarises account transactions over a period? Answer: Statement of account.\nQ5. Which document usually accompanies goods for checking at delivery? Answer: Delivery note.\nQ6. Who is the drawee of a cheque? Answer: The bank directed to pay.\nQ7. What does E. & O.E. mean? Answer: Errors and Omissions Excepted.\nQ8. What is the key difference between a bill of exchange and promissory note? Answer: An order to pay versus a promise to pay.\nQ9. A seller quotes ₦40,000 less 10% trade discount. Net price? Answer: ₦36,000.\nQ10. Does an invoice prove the buyer has paid? Answer: No. A receipt or verified payment record provides evidence of payment.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Quotation comes before purchase; invoice records a bill for a transaction.\n• Pro forma invoice is preliminary, not an ordinary post-delivery invoice.\n• Delivery note proves neither full payment nor ownership by itself.\n• Credit note reduces debt; receipt acknowledges payment.\n• Statement of account is a summary, not a single-transaction invoice.\n• Drawer writes the cheque; drawee is the bank; payee receives payment.\n• Bill of exchange = order; promissory note = promise.\n• Successive discounts are calculated in sequence.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nASK → QUOTE → ORDER → DELIVER → BILL → PAY → RECEIPT.\nCREDIT NOTE = REDUCE THE BILL.\nRECEIPT = PAYMENT ACKNOWLEDGED."}},{"id":"discounts-profit-ratios","old":{"title":"Discounts, profit, working capital and ratios","body":"A trade discount is a reduction from a list price, commonly allowed because of a buyer's trade status or distribution role. A quantity discount encourages a sufficiently large purchase. A cash discount rewards prompt payment. These discounts have different purposes and must not automatically be combined into one percentage.\n\nApply successive discounts to the balance remaining after the previous discount unless the question expressly states another basis. A 10% trade discount followed by a 5% cash discount is not normally a 15% reduction from the original amount. Terms such as 2/10, net 30 mean a 2% cash discount for payment within 10 days; otherwise the net invoice amount falls due within 30 days. Explain an unclear printed term rather than inventing missing conditions.\n\nWorked example: goods have a list value of ₦100,000. A 10% trade discount is ₦10,000, leaving ₦90,000. A 5% prompt-payment discount on ₦90,000 is ₦4,500. The buyer pays ₦85,500. The total reduction is ₦14,500, not ₦15,000. If a quantity discount is also allowed, identify exactly when and on what amount it applies.\n\nGross profit is sales less cost of goods sold. Cost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Net profit is gross profit plus other income, less operating expenses. Turnover usually means total sales over a period in school Commerce; do not confuse it with profit or the stock-turnover ratio.\n\nMark-up expresses gross profit as a percentage of cost. Margin expresses gross profit as a percentage of selling price. If goods cost ₦80 and sell for ₦100, gross profit is ₦20, mark-up is 25% and margin is 20%. The same profit therefore produces different percentages because the denominators differ.\n\nThe current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities. The acid-test ratio usually excludes stock from current assets before division. Working capital is the difference between current assets and current liabilities, not a ratio. Owners' equity equals total assets less external liabilities.\n\nThe stock-turnover rate is cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Average stock is opening stock plus closing stock, divided by two. A conflicting printed gross-profit figure should be disclosed rather than silently substituted.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Add and classify balance-sheet figures before calculating a ratio.\n• A percentage discount amount is not the final amount paid.\n• Trade discount and cash discount are not interchangeable.\n• Show the calculation if a booklet answer conflicts with the stated numbers."},"new":{"title":"Discounts, profit, working capital and ratios","body":"A trade discount is a reduction from a list price, commonly allowed because of a buyer's trade status or distribution role. A quantity discount encourages a sufficiently large purchase. A cash discount rewards prompt payment. These discounts have different purposes and must not automatically be combined into one percentage.\n\nApply successive discounts to the balance remaining after the previous discount unless the question expressly states another basis. A 10% trade discount followed by a 5% cash discount is not normally a 15% reduction from the original amount. Terms such as 2/10, net 30 mean a 2% cash discount for payment within 10 days; otherwise the net invoice amount falls due within 30 days. Explain an unclear printed term rather than inventing missing conditions.\n\nWorked example: goods have a list value of ₦100,000. A 10% trade discount is ₦10,000, leaving ₦90,000. A 5% prompt-payment discount on ₦90,000 is ₦4,500. The buyer pays ₦85,500. The total reduction is ₦14,500, not ₦15,000. If a quantity discount is also allowed, identify exactly when and on what amount it applies.\n\nGross profit is sales less cost of goods sold. Cost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Net profit is gross profit plus other income, less operating expenses. Turnover usually means total sales over a period in school Commerce; do not confuse it with profit or the stock-turnover ratio.\n\nMark-up expresses gross profit as a percentage of cost. Margin expresses gross profit as a percentage of selling price. If goods cost ₦80 and sell for ₦100, gross profit is ₦20, mark-up is 25% and margin is 20%. The same profit therefore produces different percentages because the denominators differ.\n\nThe current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities. The acid-test ratio usually excludes stock from current assets before division. Working capital is the difference between current assets and current liabilities, not a ratio. Owners' equity equals total assets less external liabilities.\n\nThe stock-turnover rate is cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Average stock is opening stock plus closing stock, divided by two. A conflicting printed gross-profit figure should be disclosed rather than silently substituted.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Add and classify balance-sheet figures before calculating a ratio.\n• A percentage discount amount is not the final amount paid.\n• Trade discount and cash discount are not interchangeable.\n• Show the calculation if a booklet answer conflicts with the stated numbers."}},{"id":"banking-money","old":{"title":"Money, banking and bank accounts","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. Its main functions are medium of exchange, measure of value or unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money facilitates specialization because a seller need not find someone who both wants the seller's product and offers the exact product desired.\n\nGood money should be generally acceptable, portable, durable, divisible, recognizable and reasonably stable in value. Scarcity relative to demand helps protect value, but scarcity alone does not make an object money. Legal tender is money a creditor is required by law to accept in the relevant circumstances; not every useful payment instrument is itself legal tender.\n\nCommercial banks accept deposits, provide payment services and lend to customers. A current account supports frequent business transactions and cheque or equivalent payment facilities, subject to the bank's arrangements. A savings account encourages saving and commonly earns interest. A fixed-deposit account places money for an agreed period, normally earning an agreed return subject to withdrawal conditions.\n\nA central bank manages currency and monetary policy, serves as banker to government and banks, holds relevant reserves and acts as lender of last resort under its legal framework. It is not an ordinary retail shop offering every member of the public a current account. Public-debt management and government financing arrangements must be read in their institutional and historical context.\n\nSpecialized institutions include development banks, mortgage institutions and microfinance banks. Development finance supports investment and economic development; mortgage finance concerns property-related borrowing. A bureau de change conducts permitted foreign-exchange transactions. Its name does not make it the authority that creates a country's monetary policy.\n\nElectronic banking includes transfers, cards, automated teller machines and online account services. A standing order instructs a bank to make specified recurring payments. A bank giro or credit transfer moves funds from one account to another. Security requires protecting passwords, verifying recipients and recognizing fraudulent messages.\n\nWorked example: a trader paid by bank transfer receives a bank-account credit. This is a payment mechanism, not proof that the trader has received a new bank loan.\n\nBanking supplies intangible financial services rather than manufacturing physical merchandise. Financial instruments can be traded, but they represent claims, obligations or payment instructions.\n\nA cashier’s cheque is drawn by a bank on itself. A bank draft can be drawn on another bank; terminology varies. Current-account fees include bank charges or historical commission on turnover.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An overdraft is borrowing, not the account holder's savings.\n• Bank rate terminology in historical papers concerns central-bank lending terms.\n• Money's exchange function differs from simply measuring a product's value."},"new":{"title":"Money, banking and bank accounts","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. Its main functions are medium of exchange, measure of value or unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money facilitates specialization because a seller need not find someone who both wants the seller's product and offers the exact product desired.\n\nGood money should be generally acceptable, portable, durable, divisible, recognizable and reasonably stable in value. Scarcity relative to demand helps protect value, but scarcity alone does not make an object money. Legal tender is money a creditor is required by law to accept in the relevant circumstances; not every useful payment instrument is itself legal tender.\n\nCommercial banks accept deposits, provide payment services and lend to customers. A current account supports frequent business transactions and cheque or equivalent payment facilities, subject to the bank's arrangements. A savings account encourages saving and commonly earns interest. A fixed-deposit account places money for an agreed period, normally earning an agreed return subject to withdrawal conditions.\n\nA central bank manages currency and monetary policy, serves as banker to government and banks, holds relevant reserves and acts as lender of last resort under its legal framework. It is not an ordinary retail shop offering every member of the public a current account. Public-debt management and government financing arrangements must be read in their institutional and historical context.\n\nSpecialized institutions include development banks, mortgage institutions and microfinance banks. Development finance supports investment and economic development; mortgage finance concerns property-related borrowing. A bureau de change conducts permitted foreign-exchange transactions. Its name does not make it the authority that creates a country's monetary policy.\n\nElectronic banking includes transfers, cards, automated teller machines and online account services. A standing order instructs a bank to make specified recurring payments. A bank giro or credit transfer moves funds from one account to another. Security requires protecting passwords, verifying recipients and recognizing fraudulent messages.\n\nWorked example: a trader paid by bank transfer receives a bank-account credit. This is a payment mechanism, not proof that the trader has received a new bank loan.\n\nBanking supplies intangible financial services rather than manufacturing physical merchandise. Financial instruments can be traded, but they represent claims, obligations or payment instructions.\n\nA cashier’s cheque is drawn by a bank on itself. A bank draft can be drawn on another bank; terminology varies. Current-account fees include bank charges or historical commission on turnover.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An overdraft is borrowing, not the account holder's savings.\n• Bank rate terminology in historical papers concerns central-bank lending terms.\n• Money's exchange function differs from simply measuring a product's value."}},{"id":"capital-market","old":{"title":"Capital markets, securities and stock exchange","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance through instruments such as shares, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments. A stock exchange is an organized market with rules for trading admitted securities. It does not create every company's capital or guarantee that every investor will make a profit.\n\nThe primary market concerns newly issued securities. Methods can include a public offer, rights issue or private placement. A prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation. The secondary market permits investors to trade existing securities. The company does not normally receive the purchase price each time one investor sells an existing share to another.\n\nA share represents an ownership interest in a company. A bond or debenture represents borrowing on stated terms. Some debts are secured against assets; others are unsecured. Convertible debt may be exchanged for shares under its terms. Bearer securities are associated with possession of the instrument, while registered holdings identify the owner in a register. Historical texts may describe inscribed holdings recorded without individual paper certificates.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying or selling securities and earns fees or commission. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. A bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects prices to fall. These describe market expectations or strategies, not companies permanently assigned to one category. Speculation can increase liquidity but does not guarantee price stability.\n\nIn Nigerian historical questions, the Nigerian Stock Exchange provided the organized securities market, while the Securities and Exchange Commission regulated capital-market activities and securities offers. The exchange was reorganized into the Nigerian Exchange structure in 2021. Use the name applicable to the paper's date. Incorporation by the Corporate Affairs Commission is separate from exchange listing.\n\nWorked example: a company sells new shares to raise money for a factory: primary-market finance. Later, Musa sells those shares to Ngozi through a broker: a secondary-market transaction. Both support investment, but only the new issue directly raises fresh funds for the company.\n\nFounders’ or deferred shares traditionally receive their dividend only after specified prior claims or conditions are met. The exact rights depend on their issue terms. A company group can consist of a holding company and its subsidiaries; incorporation alone does not establish exchange listing.\n\nExchange lists report prices and trades. Dividends and appreciation support share value; profitable sales realize capital gains. The historical Second-tier Securities Market helped smaller companies raise capital and attracted investment.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A shareholder is an owner; a bondholder is a creditor.\n• A prospectus is not a bill of exchange.\n• The regulator and the exchange have different functions.\n• Securities may be held electronically; older certificate terminology needs its historical context."},"new":{"title":"Capital markets, securities and stock exchange","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance through instruments such as shares, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments. A stock exchange is an organized market with rules for trading admitted securities. It does not create every company's capital or guarantee that every investor will make a profit.\n\nThe primary market concerns newly issued securities. Methods can include a public offer, rights issue or private placement. A prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation. The secondary market permits investors to trade existing securities. The company does not normally receive the purchase price each time one investor sells an existing share to another.\n\nA share represents an ownership interest in a company. A bond or debenture represents borrowing on stated terms. Some debts are secured against assets; others are unsecured. Convertible debt may be exchanged for shares under its terms. Bearer securities are associated with possession of the instrument, while registered holdings identify the owner in a register. Historical texts may describe inscribed holdings recorded without individual paper certificates.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying or selling securities and earns fees or commission. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. A bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects prices to fall. These describe market expectations or strategies, not companies permanently assigned to one category. Speculation can increase liquidity but does not guarantee price stability.\n\nIn Nigerian historical questions, the Nigerian Stock Exchange provided the organized securities market, while the Securities and Exchange Commission regulated capital-market activities and securities offers. The exchange was reorganized into the Nigerian Exchange structure in 2021. Use the name applicable to the paper's date. Incorporation by the Corporate Affairs Commission is separate from exchange listing.\n\nWorked example: a company sells new shares to raise money for a factory: primary-market finance. Later, Musa sells those shares to Ngozi through a broker: a secondary-market transaction. Both support investment, but only the new issue directly raises fresh funds for the company.\n\nFounders’ or deferred shares traditionally receive their dividend only after specified prior claims or conditions are met. The exact rights depend on their issue terms. A company group can consist of a holding company and its subsidiaries; incorporation alone does not establish exchange listing.\n\nExchange lists report prices and trades. Dividends and appreciation support share value; profitable sales realize capital gains. The historical Second-tier Securities Market helped smaller companies raise capital and attracted investment.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A shareholder is an owner; a bondholder is a creditor.\n• A prospectus is not a bill of exchange.\n• The regulator and the exchange have different functions.\n• Securities may be held electronically; older certificate terminology needs its historical context."}},{"id":"insurance-principles","old":{"title":"Insurance, assurance and risk principles","body":"Insurance is an arrangement in which an insurer undertakes, in return for a premium, to provide agreed compensation or benefits if a specified event occurs. A premium is the amount paid for cover. Pooling means combining many contributions in a common fund from which covered claims are met. The insurer assesses risk rather than promising payment for every imaginable loss.\n\nInsurable interest is a legally recognized financial or other relevant relationship to the subject insured. Utmost good faith requires honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, not permit profit from that loss. Contribution concerns sharing a covered loss between insurers where overlapping indemnity cover applies. Subrogation permits the insurer, after paying, to exercise relevant recovery rights. Proximate cause identifies the effective cause of a loss for deciding whether it falls within cover.\n\nLife assurance pays a stated benefit on death or specified survival, not ordinary property indemnity. Whole-life cover concerns death. An endowment pays on survival to maturity or earlier death under its terms. Surrender value is the amount available on permitted early termination, not necessarily the maturity benefit.\n\nProperty and liability covers include fire, motor, burglary, marine and fidelity insurance. Fidelity cover protects against specified employee dishonesty. Marine hull insurance concerns the vessel; cargo insurance concerns goods; freight insurance concerns freight earnings or charges at risk. Policy terms determine the precise cover. A cover note gives temporary evidence of cover while formal documents are prepared.\n\nPure risks involve loss or no loss; speculative risks can produce gain as well as loss. Gambling and ordinary business speculation are generally not insured like accidental property loss. Reinsurance is insurance obtained by an insurer to spread its own exposure.\n\nWorked example: a warehouse insured for a covered fire loss of ₦400,000 does not automatically receive ₦1 million merely because that is the policy limit. Indemnity, limits, excesses and other terms affect payment.\n\nGeneral average involves an intentional, reasonable sacrifice or expense for the common safety of a marine adventure. Particular average concerns an accidental partial loss borne under the relevant cover, without that shared sacrifice. Do not confuse either with a time or voyage policy.\n\nA floating marine policy covers a series of shipments under agreed declarations. An ex gratia payment is made without admitting a legal obligation. Property type and construction affect fire risk and premiums.\n\nDeath is certain, its timing uncertain; life-policyholders are assured.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Insurance does not prevent every accident.\n• A premium is not compensation paid to the claimant.\n• Assurance and insurance distinctions depend on the type of benefit, not a claim that human life has a fixed resale value."},"new":{"title":"Insurance, assurance and risk principles","body":"Insurance is an arrangement in which an insurer undertakes, in return for a premium, to provide agreed compensation or benefits if a specified event occurs. A premium is the amount paid for cover. Pooling means combining many contributions in a common fund from which covered claims are met. The insurer assesses risk rather than promising payment for every imaginable loss.\n\nInsurable interest is a legally recognized financial or other relevant relationship to the subject insured. Utmost good faith requires honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, not permit profit from that loss. Contribution concerns sharing a covered loss between insurers where overlapping indemnity cover applies. Subrogation permits the insurer, after paying, to exercise relevant recovery rights. Proximate cause identifies the effective cause of a loss for deciding whether it falls within cover.\n\nLife assurance pays a stated benefit on death or specified survival, not ordinary property indemnity. Whole-life cover concerns death. An endowment pays on survival to maturity or earlier death under its terms. Surrender value is the amount available on permitted early termination, not necessarily the maturity benefit.\n\nProperty and liability covers include fire, motor, burglary, marine and fidelity insurance. Fidelity cover protects against specified employee dishonesty. Marine hull insurance concerns the vessel; cargo insurance concerns goods; freight insurance concerns freight earnings or charges at risk. Policy terms determine the precise cover. A cover note gives temporary evidence of cover while formal documents are prepared.\n\nPure risks involve loss or no loss; speculative risks can produce gain as well as loss. Gambling and ordinary business speculation are generally not insured like accidental property loss. Reinsurance is insurance obtained by an insurer to spread its own exposure.\n\nWorked example: a warehouse insured for a covered fire loss of ₦400,000 does not automatically receive ₦1 million merely because that is the policy limit. Indemnity, limits, excesses and other terms affect payment.\n\nGeneral average involves an intentional, reasonable sacrifice or expense for the common safety of a marine adventure. Particular average concerns an accidental partial loss borne under the relevant cover, without that shared sacrifice. Do not confuse either with a time or voyage policy.\n\nA floating marine policy covers a series of shipments under agreed declarations. An ex gratia payment is made without admitting a legal obligation. Property type and construction affect fire risk and premiums.\n\nDeath is certain, its timing uncertain; life-policyholders are assured.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Insurance does not prevent every accident.\n• A premium is not compensation paid to the claimant.\n• Assurance and insurance distinctions depend on the type of benefit, not a claim that human life has a fixed resale value."}},{"id":"transport-logistics","old":{"title":"Transport, ports and distribution logistics","body":"Transport is the movement of people and goods from one place to another. It creates place utility, widens markets and connects producers with suppliers and customers. A suitable mode depends on cost, speed, distance, reliability, safety, accessibility, the nature of the goods and the quantity involved. The fastest mode is not automatically the cheapest or most suitable.\n\nRoad transport is flexible and can provide door-to-door service. It is useful for short journeys and local distribution but can be affected by traffic, accidents and poor roads. Rail can carry bulky loads over land economically where routes exist, but it lacks road transport's route flexibility. Air is fast and useful for urgent, valuable or perishable cargo, though it is relatively costly and subject to capacity limits.\n\nWater transport is important for bulky international cargo. Ocean liners follow regular routes and schedules; tramp shipping operates according to available cargo and charter arrangements rather than a fixed advertised timetable. Ferries generally provide shorter passenger or vehicle crossings. Pipelines continuously carry suitable liquids or gases and can efficiently move crude oil, but they require substantial fixed investment and do not carry general merchandise.\n\nA seaport provides facilities such as berths, loading and unloading arrangements, storage and access for ships and cargo. A ports authority administers port facilities and operations within its mandate. Customs administers duties and related border controls; the two bodies are not interchangeable. Clearing agents assist customs clearance, while forwarding agents arrange movement and documentation.\n\nA consignment note records goods entrusted to a carrier. A manifest lists cargo carried. A bill of lading acknowledges receipt or shipment, evidences the carriage contract and, in appropriate negotiable form, can act as a document of title. Do not assume that every transport document transfers ownership.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer may use a ship for a container of machines, a port warehouse while clearance is arranged and a truck for final delivery. These stages complement rather than replace one another.\n\nA carrier undertakes carriage of goods or people; the consignor sends the consignment. Airport and seaport facilities can both include cargo handling, storage and security.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A scheduled ocean service is a liner, not a tramp.\n• A port's loading function does not mean the ports authority is the customs authority.\n• Water transport is not always appropriate for an urgent medical consignment.\n• A pipeline transports particular fluids, not every industrial product."},"new":{"title":"Transport, ports and distribution logistics","body":"Transport is the movement of people and goods from one place to another. It creates place utility, widens markets and connects producers with suppliers and customers. A suitable mode depends on cost, speed, distance, reliability, safety, accessibility, the nature of the goods and the quantity involved. The fastest mode is not automatically the cheapest or most suitable.\n\nRoad transport is flexible and can provide door-to-door service. It is useful for short journeys and local distribution but can be affected by traffic, accidents and poor roads. Rail can carry bulky loads over land economically where routes exist, but it lacks road transport's route flexibility. Air is fast and useful for urgent, valuable or perishable cargo, though it is relatively costly and subject to capacity limits.\n\nWater transport is important for bulky international cargo. Ocean liners follow regular routes and schedules; tramp shipping operates according to available cargo and charter arrangements rather than a fixed advertised timetable. Ferries generally provide shorter passenger or vehicle crossings. Pipelines continuously carry suitable liquids or gases and can efficiently move crude oil, but they require substantial fixed investment and do not carry general merchandise.\n\nA seaport provides facilities such as berths, loading and unloading arrangements, storage and access for ships and cargo. A ports authority administers port facilities and operations within its mandate. Customs administers duties and related border controls; the two bodies are not interchangeable. Clearing agents assist customs clearance, while forwarding agents arrange movement and documentation.\n\nA consignment note records goods entrusted to a carrier. A manifest lists cargo carried. A bill of lading acknowledges receipt or shipment, evidences the carriage contract and, in appropriate negotiable form, can act as a document of title. Do not assume that every transport document transfers ownership.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer may use a ship for a container of machines, a port warehouse while clearance is arranged and a truck for final delivery. These stages complement rather than replace one another.\n\nA carrier undertakes carriage of goods or people; the consignor sends the consignment. Airport and seaport facilities can both include cargo handling, storage and security.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A scheduled ocean service is a liner, not a tramp.\n• A port's loading function does not mean the ports authority is the customs authority.\n• Water transport is not always appropriate for an urgent medical consignment.\n• A pipeline transports particular fluids, not every industrial product."}},{"id":"warehousing","old":{"title":"Warehousing, stockholding and time utility","body":"Warehousing is the storage and protection of goods until they are required for sale or use. It bridges the time gap between production and consumption and therefore creates time utility. A warehouse does not necessarily increase the physical quantity or improve the original quality of goods; its value includes preserving availability and condition.\n\nStorage permits seasonal products to be supplied beyond the harvest period. It helps businesses maintain regular production, assemble goods for distribution and meet unexpected demand. Warehousing can also allow grading, packing and preparation for sale. Holding stock ties up finance and creates risks such as theft, fire, damage, obsolescence and deterioration, so more stock is not always better.\n\nA private warehouse is operated for its owner's goods. A public warehouse offers storage services to different users for charges. A bonded warehouse holds goods under customs control, commonly before relevant import duties are paid. Duties are not abolished simply because goods enter a bonded warehouse. A refrigerated or cold-storage facility keeps temperature-sensitive products under suitable conditions.\n\nLocation depends on the goods and the business purpose. A distribution warehouse often benefits from nearness to customers and reliable roads. Storage for imported goods may be near a port; a factory store may be near production. Safety, space, transport access, utilities, land costs, labour and the nature of surrounding activities also matter. No single location rule fits every warehouse.\n\nStock records show quantities received, issued and remaining. Regular stocktaking checks physical stock against records. Reorder arrangements help prevent shortages, but businesses must consider delivery time and changing demand. FIFO describes issuing or accounting for earlier receipts first; it is not proof that goods can never expire or suffer damage.\n\nWorked example: rice bought after harvest is stored safely and sold throughout the year. The storage service makes the rice available when consumers need it. If demand rises unexpectedly, existing stock can reduce temporary shortages, but a warehouse cannot create unlimited supplies.\n\nA state warehouse is government-owned or controlled. Public warehousing describes storage available to customers, not necessarily state ownership. Manufacturers and wholesalers can keep private warehouses for their own stocks.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Warehousing is a productive service because it creates utility.\n• Bonded storage postpones or controls duty treatment; it does not automatically cancel duty.\n• A warehouse near raw materials is not necessarily ideal for serving final consumers.\n• Price stabilization is a possible result of regular supply, not a guarantee that prices never change."},"new":{"title":"Warehousing, stockholding and time utility","body":"Warehousing is the storage and protection of goods until they are required for sale or use. It bridges the time gap between production and consumption and therefore creates time utility. A warehouse does not necessarily increase the physical quantity or improve the original quality of goods; its value includes preserving availability and condition.\n\nStorage permits seasonal products to be supplied beyond the harvest period. It helps businesses maintain regular production, assemble goods for distribution and meet unexpected demand. Warehousing can also allow grading, packing and preparation for sale. Holding stock ties up finance and creates risks such as theft, fire, damage, obsolescence and deterioration, so more stock is not always better.\n\nA private warehouse is operated for its owner's goods. A public warehouse offers storage services to different users for charges. A bonded warehouse holds goods under customs control, commonly before relevant import duties are paid. Duties are not abolished simply because goods enter a bonded warehouse. A refrigerated or cold-storage facility keeps temperature-sensitive products under suitable conditions.\n\nLocation depends on the goods and the business purpose. A distribution warehouse often benefits from nearness to customers and reliable roads. Storage for imported goods may be near a port; a factory store may be near production. Safety, space, transport access, utilities, land costs, labour and the nature of surrounding activities also matter. No single location rule fits every warehouse.\n\nStock records show quantities received, issued and remaining. Regular stocktaking checks physical stock against records. Reorder arrangements help prevent shortages, but businesses must consider delivery time and changing demand. FIFO describes issuing or accounting for earlier receipts first; it is not proof that goods can never expire or suffer damage.\n\nWorked example: rice bought after harvest is stored safely and sold throughout the year. The storage service makes the rice available when consumers need it. If demand rises unexpectedly, existing stock can reduce temporary shortages, but a warehouse cannot create unlimited supplies.\n\nA state warehouse is government-owned or controlled. Public warehousing describes storage available to customers, not necessarily state ownership. Manufacturers and wholesalers can keep private warehouses for their own stocks.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Warehousing is a productive service because it creates utility.\n• Bonded storage postpones or controls duty treatment; it does not automatically cancel duty.\n• A warehouse near raw materials is not necessarily ideal for serving final consumers.\n• Price stabilization is a possible result of regular supply, not a guarantee that prices never change."}},{"id":"communication-ict","old":{"title":"Business communication and computer fundamentals","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. A simple process involves a sender, encoding, a message, a channel, a receiver, decoding and feedback. Noise is anything that interferes with understanding, including a poor connection, unclear language or distraction. Sending a message is not proof that its intended meaning has been understood.\n\nOral communication allows quick explanation and feedback. Written communication provides a record but may take longer to prepare or interpret. Visual communication uses pictures, charts or signs; non-verbal communication includes gestures and expression. The best channel depends on urgency, confidentiality, accuracy, cost, distance and the need for a record.\n\nPostal and courier services move letters and parcels. A teleprinter was used for telex communication, while a fax transmitted an image of a document. These appear in historical papers even though email and online messaging now perform much routine communication. A registered item may require a collection notice and proof of identity; it is not collected merely because someone knows the sender.\n\nA computer accepts data, processes it under instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment; software is the set of programs. System software includes operating-system functions, while application software performs user tasks. The processor interprets and executes instructions. Input devices supply data; output devices present results. RAM is temporary working memory, unlike permanent file storage.\n\nComputers can improve speed, consistency, storage and information retrieval. They still depend on suitable data and instructions: incorrect input can produce incorrect output. Security, reliable electricity, training, maintenance and backup are important. An internet connection links external networks; an intranet is a private internal network using related technologies. Email is a service, not the entire internet.\n\nHistorical language questions may associate FORTRAN with scientific computation and COBOL with business data processing. Treat this as a traditional language classification, not a claim that no modern language can perform either task.\n\nWorked example: a shop enters sales with a keyboard, the program calculates totals, data is stored, and a printer produces receipts. These are input, processing, storage and output functions.\n\nTraditional size classifications include microcomputers, minicomputers, mainframes and supercomputers; a desktop is a common microcomputer. Analogue, digital and hybrid refer to data-handling methods. Mixing a size category with a method is not a consistent classification. A workstation is designed for an individual user’s demanding tasks.\n\nRegistered or recorded-delivery services provide particular tracking or receipt evidence under their terms; a certificate of posting alone is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n\nExpress prioritizes speed.\n\nCommon traps:\n• RAM is not the same as a hard drive.\n• Software is not the physical processor.\n• Communication moves information; transport moves people and goods."},"new":{"title":"Business communication and computer fundamentals","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. A simple process involves a sender, encoding, a message, a channel, a receiver, decoding and feedback. Noise is anything that interferes with understanding, including a poor connection, unclear language or distraction. Sending a message is not proof that its intended meaning has been understood.\n\nOral communication allows quick explanation and feedback. Written communication provides a record but may take longer to prepare or interpret. Visual communication uses pictures, charts or signs; non-verbal communication includes gestures and expression. The best channel depends on urgency, confidentiality, accuracy, cost, distance and the need for a record.\n\nPostal and courier services move letters and parcels. A teleprinter was used for telex communication, while a fax transmitted an image of a document. These appear in historical papers even though email and online messaging now perform much routine communication. A registered item may require a collection notice and proof of identity; it is not collected merely because someone knows the sender.\n\nA computer accepts data, processes it under instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment; software is the set of programs. System software includes operating-system functions, while application software performs user tasks. The processor interprets and executes instructions. Input devices supply data; output devices present results. RAM is temporary working memory, unlike permanent file storage.\n\nComputers can improve speed, consistency, storage and information retrieval. They still depend on suitable data and instructions: incorrect input can produce incorrect output. Security, reliable electricity, training, maintenance and backup are important. An internet connection links external networks; an intranet is a private internal network using related technologies. Email is a service, not the entire internet.\n\nHistorical language questions may associate FORTRAN with scientific computation and COBOL with business data processing. Treat this as a traditional language classification, not a claim that no modern language can perform either task.\n\nWorked example: a shop enters sales with a keyboard, the program calculates totals, data is stored, and a printer produces receipts. These are input, processing, storage and output functions.\n\nTraditional size classifications include microcomputers, minicomputers, mainframes and supercomputers; a desktop is a common microcomputer. Analogue, digital and hybrid refer to data-handling methods. Mixing a size category with a method is not a consistent classification. A workstation is designed for an individual user’s demanding tasks.\n\nRegistered or recorded-delivery services provide particular tracking or receipt evidence under their terms; a certificate of posting alone is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n\nExpress prioritizes speed.\n\nCommon traps:\n• RAM is not the same as a hard drive.\n• Software is not the physical processor.\n• Communication moves information; transport moves people and goods."}},{"id":"marketing-advertising","old":{"title":"Marketing, promotion and customer satisfaction","body":"Marketing identifies customers' needs and organizes activities that satisfy them through a suitable offer. It includes product planning, pricing, distribution, promotion and customer service. Selling is the part concerned with persuading a customer and completing a transaction. Marketing begins before a finished product is offered for sale and continues after delivery.\n\nThe marketing concept emphasizes customer orientation, coordinated business effort and achieving objectives through customer satisfaction. The production concept emphasizes availability and efficient output; the product concept emphasizes quality or features; the selling concept emphasizes strong sales effort. A firm practising marketing does not assume that customers must buy whatever it has already produced.\n\nThe marketing mix is commonly summarized as product, price, place and promotion. Product includes design, quality, branding, packaging and related service. Price concerns the amount and terms charged. Place concerns distribution and access. Promotion communicates the offer and can include advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a broad market into groups with relevant shared characteristics. A business can select a target segment and position its product as offering a distinctive benefit. An underserved group can provide an opportunity, but selecting a segment and establishing a position are not identical to physically moving a shop.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor through media such as radio, television, print or online channels. Informative advertising explains a product; persuasive advertising encourages preference or purchase. Sales promotion offers shorter-term incentives such as samples, demonstrations, discounts or competitions. Personal selling uses direct interaction with a buyer. Public relations develops understanding and goodwill between an organization and its publics.\n\nWorked example: a detergent maker develops small affordable packs for students, sets a suitable price, supplies nearby shops and advertises on local radio. The pack is product, the charge is price, the shops are place and the advertisement is promotion. Giving trial samples is sales promotion rather than a new distribution channel.\n\nMarket research studies a particular market; marketing research covers wider marketing decisions. A market structure describes the participants and competitive relationships. Pricing must consider demand, cost, competition and objectives; one factor is not universally decisive.\n\nCompetitive advertising stresses a brand against rival brands. Useful advice and free installation can build goodwill; installation after purchase is also an after-sales service. These categories can overlap without making all publicity the same as advertising.\n\nCommon traps:\n• The customer, not the firm's production convenience, is central to marketing orientation.\n• Advertising informs and persuades; transporting stock is distribution.\n• A promotion objective can differ from the medium used.\n• The product element includes planning and development, not only the selling price."},"new":{"title":"Marketing, promotion and customer satisfaction","body":"Marketing identifies customers' needs and organizes activities that satisfy them through a suitable offer. It includes product planning, pricing, distribution, promotion and customer service. Selling is the part concerned with persuading a customer and completing a transaction. Marketing begins before a finished product is offered for sale and continues after delivery.\n\nThe marketing concept emphasizes customer orientation, coordinated business effort and achieving objectives through customer satisfaction. The production concept emphasizes availability and efficient output; the product concept emphasizes quality or features; the selling concept emphasizes strong sales effort. A firm practising marketing does not assume that customers must buy whatever it has already produced.\n\nThe marketing mix is commonly summarized as product, price, place and promotion. Product includes design, quality, branding, packaging and related service. Price concerns the amount and terms charged. Place concerns distribution and access. Promotion communicates the offer and can include advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a broad market into groups with relevant shared characteristics. A business can select a target segment and position its product as offering a distinctive benefit. An underserved group can provide an opportunity, but selecting a segment and establishing a position are not identical to physically moving a shop.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor through media such as radio, television, print or online channels. Informative advertising explains a product; persuasive advertising encourages preference or purchase. Sales promotion offers shorter-term incentives such as samples, demonstrations, discounts or competitions. Personal selling uses direct interaction with a buyer. Public relations develops understanding and goodwill between an organization and its publics.\n\nWorked example: a detergent maker develops small affordable packs for students, sets a suitable price, supplies nearby shops and advertises on local radio. The pack is product, the charge is price, the shops are place and the advertisement is promotion. Giving trial samples is sales promotion rather than a new distribution channel.\n\nMarket research studies a particular market; marketing research covers wider marketing decisions. A market structure describes the participants and competitive relationships. Pricing must consider demand, cost, competition and objectives; one factor is not universally decisive.\n\nCompetitive advertising stresses a brand against rival brands. Useful advice and free installation can build goodwill; installation after purchase is also an after-sales service. These categories can overlap without making all publicity the same as advertising.\n\nCommon traps:\n• The customer, not the firm's production convenience, is central to marketing orientation.\n• Advertising informs and persuades; transporting stock is distribution.\n• A promotion objective can differ from the medium used.\n• The product element includes planning and development, not only the selling price."}},{"id":"foreign-trade-documents","old":{"title":"Foreign trade, payment documents and trade balances","body":"Foreign trade is buying and selling between countries. Imports are goods or services bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. Entrepôt trade involves importing goods and re-exporting them. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage international exchange. Countries do not need identical resources or costs before they can trade.\n\nVisible trade concerns physical goods. Invisible trade concerns services and related non-merchandise transactions, such as transport and tourism receipts. Balance of trade is the difference between the value of merchandise exports and imports over a period. The balance of payments is the systematic record of economic transactions between residents and non-residents over a period; it is wider than imports and exports of goods alone.\n\nA buyer may send an indent to an overseas supplier or agent specifying goods required. A commercial invoice states the goods and price. A certificate of origin identifies the goods' country of origin. An insurance certificate evidences relevant cover. A bill of lading concerns receipt and carriage of sea cargo and may have a title function. A consular invoice involves certification under the importing country's requirements where applicable.\n\nDocumentary credit, commonly a letter-of-credit arrangement, involves a bank undertaking payment subject to compliant documents and stated terms. A documentary bill is presented with specified shipping documents. Neither term means that the bank guarantees the physical quality of the goods merely because documents are supplied. A letter of hypothecation gives relevant rights over goods used as security under its arrangement.\n\nFOB and CIF are historical examination terms as well as modern trade-rule labels. CIF includes cost, insurance and freight in the seller's quoted responsibilities. FOB concerns delivery on board under the applicable rule. Who pays a charge and when risk transfers must be distinguished; modern Incoterms do not make them the same event in every case.\n\nWorked example: merchandise exports of ₦90 million and imports of ₦110 million produce an adverse merchandise trade balance of ₦20 million. Tourism receipts could improve the wider external position but do not turn into merchandise exports.\n\nCommodity markets trade primary products and other standardized goods. They can link exporters and importers, but not every commodity transaction crosses a national border.\n\nForeign-exchange markets trade currencies; instruments such as travellers’ cheques can represent foreign-currency claims, unlike ordinary domestic shares.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade deficit is not automatically the whole balance-of-payments position.\n• A manifest lists cargo; an indent orders goods.\n• Trade barriers include tariffs, quotas, exchange restrictions and burdensome procedures, not only long distances."},"new":{"title":"Foreign trade, payment documents and trade balances","body":"Foreign trade is buying and selling between countries. Imports are goods or services bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. Entrepôt trade involves importing goods and re-exporting them. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage international exchange. Countries do not need identical resources or costs before they can trade.\n\nVisible trade concerns physical goods. Invisible trade concerns services and related non-merchandise transactions, such as transport and tourism receipts. Balance of trade is the difference between the value of merchandise exports and imports over a period. The balance of payments is the systematic record of economic transactions between residents and non-residents over a period; it is wider than imports and exports of goods alone.\n\nA buyer may send an indent to an overseas supplier or agent specifying goods required. A commercial invoice states the goods and price. A certificate of origin identifies the goods' country of origin. An insurance certificate evidences relevant cover. A bill of lading concerns receipt and carriage of sea cargo and may have a title function. A consular invoice involves certification under the importing country's requirements where applicable.\n\nDocumentary credit, commonly a letter-of-credit arrangement, involves a bank undertaking payment subject to compliant documents and stated terms. A documentary bill is presented with specified shipping documents. Neither term means that the bank guarantees the physical quality of the goods merely because documents are supplied. A letter of hypothecation gives relevant rights over goods used as security under its arrangement.\n\nFOB and CIF are historical examination terms as well as modern trade-rule labels. CIF includes cost, insurance and freight in the seller's quoted responsibilities. FOB concerns delivery on board under the applicable rule. Who pays a charge and when risk transfers must be distinguished; modern Incoterms do not make them the same event in every case.\n\nWorked example: merchandise exports of ₦90 million and imports of ₦110 million produce an adverse merchandise trade balance of ₦20 million. Tourism receipts could improve the wider external position but do not turn into merchandise exports.\n\nCommodity markets trade primary products and other standardized goods. They can link exporters and importers, but not every commodity transaction crosses a national border.\n\nForeign-exchange markets trade currencies; instruments such as travellers’ cheques can represent foreign-currency claims, unlike ordinary domestic shares.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade deficit is not automatically the whole balance-of-payments position.\n• A manifest lists cargo; an indent orders goods.\n• Trade barriers include tariffs, quotas, exchange restrictions and burdensome procedures, not only long distances."}},{"id":"economic-integration","old":{"title":"Economic cooperation and regional organizations","body":"Economic integration is cooperation among economies to reduce barriers and coordinate selected policies. A free-trade area removes agreed trade barriers among members while allowing separate external trade policies. A customs union adds a common external tariff. A common market permits freer movement of factors such as labour and capital as well as goods. A monetary union goes further in monetary arrangements; these stages should not be treated as identical.\n\nRegional cooperation can enlarge markets, permit specialization, support shared infrastructure and strengthen bargaining power. Difficulties include different development levels, overlapping memberships, political disagreements, weak transport links and obstacles to implementing agreements. Signing a treaty does not prove that every barrier immediately disappears.\n\nECOWAS was established to promote cooperation and integration among West African states. Historical questions should be answered with membership and institutional arrangements appropriate to their date. Membership has changed, including withdrawals that took effect in 2025, so an old list should not be presented as permanently current. Sovereign states and admission under the treaty framework are relevant; population or landmass alone does not define membership.\n\nRiver-basin organizations coordinate shared water resources and related development. The Lake Chad Basin Commission's founding members were Cameroon, Chad, Niger and Nigeria. Later membership expanded, so a question asking about founders differs from one asking about members generally. The Niger Basin Authority, historically described as the Niger Basin Commission, involves countries sharing interests in the Niger basin. Ghana and the Gambia should not be assumed to belong merely because they are West African countries.\n\nThe Mano River Union began as a subregional cooperation arrangement and later expanded. The European Union is a wider political and economic integration project with its own membership rules; not every European country is an EU member. Regional financial institutions and commercial banks can support trade without being the same thing as an intergovernmental river-basin organization.\n\nWorked example: two neighbouring states removing tariffs between themselves establish an element of free trade. If they also apply the same tariff to non-members, they are moving toward a customs union. Allowing workers to move freely adds a different level of integration.\n\nEcobank is a regional commercial banking initiative associated with promoting African trade. A commercial bank’s regional purpose does not make it identical to a government-owned river-basin commission.\n\nThe Lake Chad Basin Commission was established in 1964 and has its headquarters in N’Djamena, Chad.\n\nECOWAS free-movement arrangements facilitate eligible citizens’ movement, residence and establishment; implementation and current membership require date-specific qualifications.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Geographical proximity alone does not prove membership.\n• Check whether the paper asks for founders, members or present membership.\n• Do not substitute today's political changes silently into an older examination question."},"new":{"title":"Economic cooperation and regional organizations","body":"Economic integration is cooperation among economies to reduce barriers and coordinate selected policies. A free-trade area removes agreed trade barriers among members while allowing separate external trade policies. A customs union adds a common external tariff. A common market permits freer movement of factors such as labour and capital as well as goods. A monetary union goes further in monetary arrangements; these stages should not be treated as identical.\n\nRegional cooperation can enlarge markets, permit specialization, support shared infrastructure and strengthen bargaining power. Difficulties include different development levels, overlapping memberships, political disagreements, weak transport links and obstacles to implementing agreements. Signing a treaty does not prove that every barrier immediately disappears.\n\nECOWAS was established to promote cooperation and integration among West African states. Historical questions should be answered with membership and institutional arrangements appropriate to their date. Membership has changed, including withdrawals that took effect in 2025, so an old list should not be presented as permanently current. Sovereign states and admission under the treaty framework are relevant; population or landmass alone does not define membership.\n\nRiver-basin organizations coordinate shared water resources and related development. The Lake Chad Basin Commission's founding members were Cameroon, Chad, Niger and Nigeria. Later membership expanded, so a question asking about founders differs from one asking about members generally. The Niger Basin Authority, historically described as the Niger Basin Commission, involves countries sharing interests in the Niger basin. Ghana and the Gambia should not be assumed to belong merely because they are West African countries.\n\nThe Mano River Union began as a subregional cooperation arrangement and later expanded. The European Union is a wider political and economic integration project with its own membership rules; not every European country is an EU member. Regional financial institutions and commercial banks can support trade without being the same thing as an intergovernmental river-basin organization.\n\nWorked example: two neighbouring states removing tariffs between themselves establish an element of free trade. If they also apply the same tariff to non-members, they are moving toward a customs union. Allowing workers to move freely adds a different level of integration.\n\nEcobank is a regional commercial banking initiative associated with promoting African trade. A commercial bank’s regional purpose does not make it identical to a government-owned river-basin commission.\n\nThe Lake Chad Basin Commission was established in 1964 and has its headquarters in N’Djamena, Chad.\n\nECOWAS free-movement arrangements facilitate eligible citizens’ movement, residence and establishment; implementation and current membership require date-specific qualifications.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Geographical proximity alone does not prove membership.\n• Check whether the paper asks for founders, members or present membership.\n• Do not substitute today's political changes silently into an older examination question."}},{"id":"law-contract-agency","old":{"title":"Contracts, sale of goods and agency","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its usual requirements include offer, acceptance, consideration where required, intention to create legal relations, capacity and a lawful purpose. An agreement alone is not automatically enforceable. Consideration is something of value exchanged for a promise; it is not simply careful thinking before signing.\n\nAn offer states terms on which the maker is willing to be bound if accepted. Acceptance must correspond to the offer and meet the applicable communication rules. An invitation to treat invites offers rather than necessarily being an offer itself. A sale of goods transfers or agrees to transfer ownership of goods for a money price. Barter exchanges goods without the money-price element of a conventional sale.\n\nExisting goods are already owned or possessed by the seller. Future goods are to be manufactured or acquired after the contract. Specific goods are identified and agreed upon when the contract is made. These labels concern different characteristics; future does not mean that goods have already been specifically delivered.\n\nContracts may end through performance, agreement, breach, operation of law or frustration. Frustration involves an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different under the applicable law. A higher cost or mere disappointment does not automatically establish frustration. Fraud concerns deception and should not be assumed whenever goods are lost.\n\nAgency is a relationship in which an agent is authorized to act for a principal in dealings with another party. It can arise by express agreement, implication, ratification, necessity or other recognized rules. Ratification is later approval of an act originally performed without authority, where legal conditions are satisfied. Agency by necessity requires a genuine emergency and circumstances justifying action to protect the principal's interests.\n\nWorked example: a carrier with perishable goods faces an emergency, cannot reasonably obtain instructions and sells them to prevent a greater loss. This may illustrate agency by necessity, not automatic authority to sell every consignment for convenience. An agent who handles goods must still account properly to the principal.\n\nA del credere agent receives extra commission for guaranteeing payment by buyers under the agency agreement. A voidable contract remains effective unless the entitled party rescinds it; an unrelated stranger cannot normally cancel it at will. A credit sale normally transfers ownership according to the sale agreement before all instalments are paid; hire purchase commonly postpones title.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Title means ownership, not merely physical possession.\n• A prospectus is not a contract of partnership.\n• Theft or loss does not automatically prove fraud by the contracting parties.\n• Historical legal questions require their precise assumptions; do not turn a simplified example into universal legal advice."},"new":{"title":"Contracts, sale of goods and agency","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its usual requirements include offer, acceptance, consideration where required, intention to create legal relations, capacity and a lawful purpose. An agreement alone is not automatically enforceable. Consideration is something of value exchanged for a promise; it is not simply careful thinking before signing.\n\nAn offer states terms on which the maker is willing to be bound if accepted. Acceptance must correspond to the offer and meet the applicable communication rules. An invitation to treat invites offers rather than necessarily being an offer itself. A sale of goods transfers or agrees to transfer ownership of goods for a money price. Barter exchanges goods without the money-price element of a conventional sale.\n\nExisting goods are already owned or possessed by the seller. Future goods are to be manufactured or acquired after the contract. Specific goods are identified and agreed upon when the contract is made. These labels concern different characteristics; future does not mean that goods have already been specifically delivered.\n\nContracts may end through performance, agreement, breach, operation of law or frustration. Frustration involves an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different under the applicable law. A higher cost or mere disappointment does not automatically establish frustration. Fraud concerns deception and should not be assumed whenever goods are lost.\n\nAgency is a relationship in which an agent is authorized to act for a principal in dealings with another party. It can arise by express agreement, implication, ratification, necessity or other recognized rules. Ratification is later approval of an act originally performed without authority, where legal conditions are satisfied. Agency by necessity requires a genuine emergency and circumstances justifying action to protect the principal's interests.\n\nWorked example: a carrier with perishable goods faces an emergency, cannot reasonably obtain instructions and sells them to prevent a greater loss. This may illustrate agency by necessity, not automatic authority to sell every consignment for convenience. An agent who handles goods must still account properly to the principal.\n\nA del credere agent receives extra commission for guaranteeing payment by buyers under the agency agreement. A voidable contract remains effective unless the entitled party rescinds it; an unrelated stranger cannot normally cancel it at will. A credit sale normally transfers ownership according to the sale agreement before all instalments are paid; hire purchase commonly postpones title.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Title means ownership, not merely physical possession.\n• A prospectus is not a contract of partnership.\n• Theft or loss does not automatically prove fraud by the contracting parties.\n• Historical legal questions require their precise assumptions; do not turn a simplified example into universal legal advice."}},{"id":"consumer-business-environment","old":{"title":"Consumer protection, business environment and responsibility","body":"The business environment consists of internal and external conditions that affect business operations. Internal factors include employees, finance, management and organizational culture. External factors include customers, competitors, economic conditions, technology, politics, law and social expectations. Some school questions use environment narrowly for external influences, so read their wording rather than assuming every list uses the same definition.\n\nEconomic influences include income, inflation, interest rates, market structure and access to finance. Political and legal influences concern government stability, policy and regulations. Technological change affects production, information and distribution. Social and cultural factors include beliefs, habits and changing consumer needs. Businesses can respond to these influences but cannot directly control all of them.\n\nConsumer protection comprises measures that safeguard buyers against unsafe goods, deception and unfair practices. Common consumer rights include safety, information, choice, being heard and seeking redress. Redress means a remedy for a justified complaint, such as repair, replacement or refund under the relevant rules. A consumer does not have an automatic right to free samples, company profits or unlimited credit.\n\nStandards bodies set or enforce relevant product requirements, while professional bodies regulate or promote standards within their professions. In Nigerian questions, distinguish the Standards Organisation of Nigeria from the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria, consumer associations and chambers of commerce. Modern competition and consumer-protection arrangements differ from some older institutional names, so explain the historical context of past papers.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business decisions on workers, customers, communities and the environment. It includes safe products, fair dealing, responsible waste disposal and appropriate community support. Scholarships or support for community sport can illustrate social responsibility, even when they also improve goodwill. Pollution is not made acceptable merely because a business earns profit.\n\nWorked example: an oil spill contaminating farmland and a stream can cause both land and water pollution. Dumping waste carelessly can affect health, fishing and farming. Prevention and proper treatment are business responsibilities, not just advertising opportunities.\n\nMerchantable quality concerns suitability for the ordinary uses of goods sold under their description. Fitness for a buyer’s particular purpose is a related but distinct requirement. Indigenization increases indigenous ownership; it can reduce foreign ownership and associated outward capital remittances, but it is not naturalization of a person.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Consumer awareness is useful but does not replace actual product quality or safety.\n• A trade association protects members' interests; it is not automatically the statutory quality regulator.\n• Copyright protects original creative expression; patents concern qualifying inventions; trademarks identify commercial origin."},"new":{"title":"Consumer protection, business environment and responsibility","body":"The business environment consists of internal and external conditions that affect business operations. Internal factors include employees, finance, management and organizational culture. External factors include customers, competitors, economic conditions, technology, politics, law and social expectations. Some school questions use environment narrowly for external influences, so read their wording rather than assuming every list uses the same definition.\n\nEconomic influences include income, inflation, interest rates, market structure and access to finance. Political and legal influences concern government stability, policy and regulations. Technological change affects production, information and distribution. Social and cultural factors include beliefs, habits and changing consumer needs. Businesses can respond to these influences but cannot directly control all of them.\n\nConsumer protection comprises measures that safeguard buyers against unsafe goods, deception and unfair practices. Common consumer rights include safety, information, choice, being heard and seeking redress. Redress means a remedy for a justified complaint, such as repair, replacement or refund under the relevant rules. A consumer does not have an automatic right to free samples, company profits or unlimited credit.\n\nStandards bodies set or enforce relevant product requirements, while professional bodies regulate or promote standards within their professions. In Nigerian questions, distinguish the Standards Organisation of Nigeria from the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria, consumer associations and chambers of commerce. Modern competition and consumer-protection arrangements differ from some older institutional names, so explain the historical context of past papers.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business decisions on workers, customers, communities and the environment. It includes safe products, fair dealing, responsible waste disposal and appropriate community support. Scholarships or support for community sport can illustrate social responsibility, even when they also improve goodwill. Pollution is not made acceptable merely because a business earns profit.\n\nWorked example: an oil spill contaminating farmland and a stream can cause both land and water pollution. Dumping waste carelessly can affect health, fishing and farming. Prevention and proper treatment are business responsibilities, not just advertising opportunities.\n\nMerchantable quality concerns suitability for the ordinary uses of goods sold under their description. Fitness for a buyer’s particular purpose is a related but distinct requirement. Indigenization increases indigenous ownership; it can reduce foreign ownership and associated outward capital remittances, but it is not naturalization of a person.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Consumer awareness is useful but does not replace actual product quality or safety.\n• A trade association protects members' interests; it is not automatically the statutory quality regulator.\n• Copyright protects original creative expression; patents concern qualifying inventions; trademarks identify commercial origin."}},{"id":"tourism","old":{"title":"Tourism as an aid to trade","body":"Tourism involves travelling and staying outside one's usual environment for purposes such as leisure, business or other permitted short-term visits. It creates demand for accommodation, food, transport, entertainment and related services. Domestic tourism occurs within a person's country; international tourism crosses national borders. A business visitor can generate tourism expenditure even without taking a holiday.\n\nTourism assists commerce by bringing customers to service providers and creating markets for local goods. Hotels buy food and supplies; guides provide information; transport operators move visitors; craftspeople sell products. Foreign visitors' spending can bring foreign-exchange earnings, while residents travelling abroad create spending outside the domestic economy. These service transactions are commonly described as invisible trade in school Commerce.\n\nAttractions may be natural, historical, cultural or business-related. Beaches, landscapes, festivals, museums and conferences can draw visitors. However, an attraction alone does not guarantee a successful tourism industry. Safe access, reliable transport, accommodation, sanitation, communication, skilled workers and information are also needed. Conservation protects attractions from damage and helps communities benefit over time.\n\nGovernment and specialized agencies can develop standards, promote destinations, support infrastructure and provide relevant information. Private businesses supply many of the actual services. A tourism-promotion agency is not the same as a customs authority, airline or hotel. Institutional names and responsibilities can change, so a past-paper answer should be tied to the stated period rather than silently replaced with a new acronym.\n\nBenefits include employment, tax receipts, income for local businesses and incentives to preserve cultural or natural sites. Possible costs include seasonal jobs, congestion, pollution, pressure on local resources and leakage when substantial spending goes to foreign suppliers. Responsible planning should consider residents as well as visitors.\n\nWorked example: visitors attending a festival in Osun State pay local transport operators, buy meals and stay in guesthouses. Their spending supports several businesses, not only the festival organizer. If the visitors are Nigerian residents, the example is domestic tourism rather than an export receipt from foreign visitors.\n\nTourism planning begins by identifying and assessing attractions and resources. Historic buildings are cultural attractions. Cultural interaction can be a benefit, while communities may fear unwanted foreign influences. Social benefits differ from financial receipts.\n\nThe World Tourism Organization is headquartered in Madrid. Distinguish it from the World Trade Organization.\n\nInbound tourism concerns non-residents visiting the country considered; outbound tourism concerns residents visiting another country.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Tourism is a service activity, not merely the sale of souvenirs.\n• A foreign-exchange benefit applies particularly to international visitor spending.\n• Poor security, unreliable infrastructure and weak publicity are obstacles, not inherent advantages.\n• Tourism's economic contribution does not remove the need to protect local communities and the environment."},"new":{"title":"Tourism as an aid to trade","body":"Tourism involves travelling and staying outside one's usual environment for purposes such as leisure, business or other permitted short-term visits. It creates demand for accommodation, food, transport, entertainment and related services. Domestic tourism occurs within a person's country; international tourism crosses national borders. A business visitor can generate tourism expenditure even without taking a holiday.\n\nTourism assists commerce by bringing customers to service providers and creating markets for local goods. Hotels buy food and supplies; guides provide information; transport operators move visitors; craftspeople sell products. Foreign visitors' spending can bring foreign-exchange earnings, while residents travelling abroad create spending outside the domestic economy. These service transactions are commonly described as invisible trade in school Commerce.\n\nAttractions may be natural, historical, cultural or business-related. Beaches, landscapes, festivals, museums and conferences can draw visitors. However, an attraction alone does not guarantee a successful tourism industry. Safe access, reliable transport, accommodation, sanitation, communication, skilled workers and information are also needed. Conservation protects attractions from damage and helps communities benefit over time.\n\nGovernment and specialized agencies can develop standards, promote destinations, support infrastructure and provide relevant information. Private businesses supply many of the actual services. A tourism-promotion agency is not the same as a customs authority, airline or hotel. Institutional names and responsibilities can change, so a past-paper answer should be tied to the stated period rather than silently replaced with a new acronym.\n\nBenefits include employment, tax receipts, income for local businesses and incentives to preserve cultural or natural sites. Possible costs include seasonal jobs, congestion, pollution, pressure on local resources and leakage when substantial spending goes to foreign suppliers. Responsible planning should consider residents as well as visitors.\n\nWorked example: visitors attending a festival in Osun State pay local transport operators, buy meals and stay in guesthouses. Their spending supports several businesses, not only the festival organizer. If the visitors are Nigerian residents, the example is domestic tourism rather than an export receipt from foreign visitors.\n\nTourism planning begins by identifying and assessing attractions and resources. Historic buildings are cultural attractions. Cultural interaction can be a benefit, while communities may fear unwanted foreign influences. Social benefits differ from financial receipts.\n\nThe World Tourism Organization is headquartered in Madrid. Distinguish it from the World Trade Organization.\n\nInbound tourism concerns non-residents visiting the country considered; outbound tourism concerns residents visiting another country.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Tourism is a service activity, not merely the sale of souvenirs.\n• A foreign-exchange benefit applies particularly to international visitor spending.\n• Poor security, unreliable infrastructure and weak publicity are obstacles, not inherent advantages.\n• Tourism's economic contribution does not remove the need to protect local communities and the environment."}},{"id":"trade-associations","old":{"title":"Trade associations, chambers and professional bodies","body":"A trade association is a voluntary organization of businesses or persons in the same trade formed to promote their common interests. Members may exchange information, improve standards, make representations to government and discuss common commercial problems. A trade association is not formed primarily to buy every member's goods or distribute profits like an ordinary trading company.\n\nA chamber of commerce brings together businesses from different trades within an area to promote commerce generally. It may provide business information, encourage trade contacts, organize fairs and exhibitions, represent business concerns and assist with permitted documentation. Its broader membership distinguishes it from an association limited to one trade. A chamber does not replace the courts, customs authority or central bank.\n\nA manufacturers' association represents manufacturing interests. It can advise government on industrial policies, research common problems and disseminate information through publications and meetings. An employers' association represents employers in matters such as employment relations and negotiations. A trade union represents workers. Improving workers' welfare can be a consequence of several organizations' activities, but that does not erase the distinction between employer and employee representation.\n\nA professional body concerns people practising a particular profession. It may establish qualifications, professional conduct and disciplinary arrangements under its governing framework. The Nigerian Bar Association concerns the legal profession, while a statutory regulator's powers must be distinguished from a voluntary association's advocacy. A question about service-rendering professions is different from one about associations of miners, farmers or manufacturers.\n\nConsumer associations represent buyers' interests, provide information and press for fair treatment. They may report harmful practices or advise consumers, but they do not automatically possess every enforcement power of a government agency. Collective advocacy and statutory regulation can support one another without being identical.\n\nWorked example: several garment producers form an association to discuss textile tariffs and training. That is a trade or manufacturers' interest organization. If banks, shops, manufacturers and transport firms in the same city cooperate to promote local commerce, a chamber of commerce is the more suitable description.\n\nNACCIMA is the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture. It is an umbrella association for chambers; distinguish its exact name from plausible but incorrect alternatives.\n\nHistorical papers assign trade-association registration to the commerce ministry; check the institution’s period.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade association is not the same as a trade union.\n• Promoting members' interests does not mean guaranteeing every member a profit.\n• Organizing a trade fair can promote contact and publicity; it does not itself transfer title to every displayed product.\n• Match an organization's actual membership and functions to the question, not merely a familiar acronym."},"new":{"title":"Trade associations, chambers and professional bodies","body":"A trade association is a voluntary organization of businesses or persons in the same trade formed to promote their common interests. Members may exchange information, improve standards, make representations to government and discuss common commercial problems. A trade association is not formed primarily to buy every member's goods or distribute profits like an ordinary trading company.\n\nA chamber of commerce brings together businesses from different trades within an area to promote commerce generally. It may provide business information, encourage trade contacts, organize fairs and exhibitions, represent business concerns and assist with permitted documentation. Its broader membership distinguishes it from an association limited to one trade. A chamber does not replace the courts, customs authority or central bank.\n\nA manufacturers' association represents manufacturing interests. It can advise government on industrial policies, research common problems and disseminate information through publications and meetings. An employers' association represents employers in matters such as employment relations and negotiations. A trade union represents workers. Improving workers' welfare can be a consequence of several organizations' activities, but that does not erase the distinction between employer and employee representation.\n\nA professional body concerns people practising a particular profession. It may establish qualifications, professional conduct and disciplinary arrangements under its governing framework. The Nigerian Bar Association concerns the legal profession, while a statutory regulator's powers must be distinguished from a voluntary association's advocacy. A question about service-rendering professions is different from one about associations of miners, farmers or manufacturers.\n\nConsumer associations represent buyers' interests, provide information and press for fair treatment. They may report harmful practices or advise consumers, but they do not automatically possess every enforcement power of a government agency. Collective advocacy and statutory regulation can support one another without being identical.\n\nWorked example: several garment producers form an association to discuss textile tariffs and training. That is a trade or manufacturers' interest organization. If banks, shops, manufacturers and transport firms in the same city cooperate to promote local commerce, a chamber of commerce is the more suitable description.\n\nNACCIMA is the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture. It is an umbrella association for chambers; distinguish its exact name from plausible but incorrect alternatives.\n\nHistorical papers assign trade-association registration to the commerce ministry; check the institution’s period.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade association is not the same as a trade union.\n• Promoting members' interests does not mean guaranteeing every member a profit.\n• Organizing a trade fair can promote contact and publicity; it does not itself transfer title to every displayed product.\n• Match an organization's actual membership and functions to the question, not merely a familiar acronym."}},{"id":"business-computing","old":{"title":"Computer types, processing, storage and business software","body":"A computer is an electronic device that accepts data, processes it according to instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment. Software consists of programs that tell the equipment what to do. The keyboard is an input device; a monitor and printer are output devices. Input, processing and output units are hardware, not three kinds of software.\n\nThe central processing unit, or CPU, controls processing. Its control unit fetches and interprets instructions and coordinates the movement of data. The arithmetic and logic unit performs calculations and logical comparisons. Registers hold small amounts of data needed immediately by the processor. Calling the whole CPU the coordinating section is less precise than naming its control unit.\n\nDigital computers process discrete data represented by digits. Analogue computers represent continuously varying quantities. Hybrid systems combine analogue and digital features. These describe methods of handling data. Supercomputers, mainframes, minicomputers and microcomputers are traditional capacity or size classes. A list mixing analogue and hybrid with mainframe and microcomputer is not a consistent list of digital size classes. Modern weather forecasting commonly uses powerful digital computers; older school examples may describe analogue measurements combined with digital calculations.\n\nPrimary memory supports current processing. RAM normally loses its contents when power is removed. Secondary storage keeps files for later use. DASD means Direct Access Storage Device: a record can be reached without first reading every preceding record. “Assess” is a spelling error for “Access,” not a different storage principle. Permanent storage does not mean that a file can never be erased.\n\nA spreadsheet such as Microsoft Excel performs calculations and presents financial tables or charts. A database stores related records and supports searches and reports. A word processor prepares text documents. Choose the application from the actual task: calculations suggest a spreadsheet, while searching a structured customer list suggests a database.\n\nWorked example: an Abuja shop stores customer records in a database, calculates monthly sales in a spreadsheet and prints letters using a word processor. These tasks may exchange data, but the applications have different main purposes.\n\nA cybercafé pays for electricity, connectivity, equipment, maintenance and staff. These operating costs can be a major constraint; the leading constraint varies by place and period, so a paper’s ranking is contextual.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Software is not a physical input device.\n• Digital classification and capacity classification answer different questions.\n• The control unit coordinates; the arithmetic and logic unit calculates and compares.\n• An application's familiar name does not establish that it best fits every business task."},"new":{"title":"Computer types, processing, storage and business software","body":"A computer is an electronic device that accepts data, processes it according to instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment. Software consists of programs that tell the equipment what to do. The keyboard is an input device; a monitor and printer are output devices. Input, processing and output units are hardware, not three kinds of software.\n\nThe central processing unit, or CPU, controls processing. Its control unit fetches and interprets instructions and coordinates the movement of data. The arithmetic and logic unit performs calculations and logical comparisons. Registers hold small amounts of data needed immediately by the processor. Calling the whole CPU the coordinating section is less precise than naming its control unit.\n\nDigital computers process discrete data represented by digits. Analogue computers represent continuously varying quantities. Hybrid systems combine analogue and digital features. These describe methods of handling data. Supercomputers, mainframes, minicomputers and microcomputers are traditional capacity or size classes. A list mixing analogue and hybrid with mainframe and microcomputer is not a consistent list of digital size classes. Modern weather forecasting commonly uses powerful digital computers; older school examples may describe analogue measurements combined with digital calculations.\n\nPrimary memory supports current processing. RAM normally loses its contents when power is removed. Secondary storage keeps files for later use. DASD means Direct Access Storage Device: a record can be reached without first reading every preceding record. “Assess” is a spelling error for “Access,” not a different storage principle. Permanent storage does not mean that a file can never be erased.\n\nA spreadsheet such as Microsoft Excel performs calculations and presents financial tables or charts. A database stores related records and supports searches and reports. A word processor prepares text documents. Choose the application from the actual task: calculations suggest a spreadsheet, while searching a structured customer list suggests a database.\n\nWorked example: an Abuja shop stores customer records in a database, calculates monthly sales in a spreadsheet and prints letters using a word processor. These tasks may exchange data, but the applications have different main purposes.\n\nA cybercafé pays for electricity, connectivity, equipment, maintenance and staff. These operating costs can be a major constraint; the leading constraint varies by place and period, so a paper’s ranking is contextual.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Software is not a physical input device.\n• Digital classification and capacity classification answer different questions.\n• The control unit coordinates; the arithmetic and logic unit calculates and compares.\n• An application's familiar name does not establish that it best fits every business task."}},{"id":"office-communication","old":{"title":"Office procedures and the communication process","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. The sender forms an idea and encodes it into words, signs or another understandable form. The message travels through a channel or medium. The receiver decodes it and may provide feedback. Feedback tells the sender whether the message was received and understood; it is not the original message travelling forward.\n\nNoise is anything that interferes with communication. It includes loud sound, a weak telephone connection, unfamiliar words, unclear handwriting and distraction. Distance can increase communication difficulties, but suitable technology may overcome it. A communication diagram should be read by following the arrows, not by guessing a letter from its position on the page.\n\nDownward communication passes from a superior to a subordinate, such as a manager giving instructions. Upward communication passes from a subordinate to a superior, such as a sales report. Horizontal or lateral communication occurs between people at comparable organizational levels. A department sending information to another department does not always make it upward or downward.\n\nAn internal memorandum, commonly called a memo, carries official information within an organization. Circulars send the same information to several recipients. Telephones and face-to-face discussion permit quick oral exchange. Notice boards display information where intended readers can see it. Advertising normally addresses customers or the public, so it is not the ordinary channel for confidential departmental instructions.\n\nA procedure states the ordered steps for carrying out a task. A policy supplies a general guide to decisions. A programme coordinates activities and timing to achieve an objective. These are connected management tools, but a step-by-step office method is specifically a procedure.\n\nThe Internet connects networks worldwide. Email is one Internet service for exchanging electronic messages. GSM mobile communication allows users to communicate while moving between suitable network coverage areas. Historical questions may contrast telephone, radio, telegraph and television; identify whether the message is oral, written, visual, public or private before choosing.\n\nWorked example: a Kaduna manager sends branch supervisors a circular about stocktaking. Each supervisor explains the instructions to assistants and sends a completion report back. The instructions are downward; the report is upward; the reply also provides feedback.\n\nA telegram is a written message sent through telegraph services. Telex uses linked teleprinters to transmit typed messages.\n\nReliable communication can reduce travel costs and risks, connect participants and improve coordination of goods delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sending a message does not prove understanding.\n• Feedback and noise are different parts of the process.\n• An advertisement is not normally an internal memo.\n• Choose a medium for the stated audience and purpose, not simply because it reaches many people."},"new":{"title":"Office procedures and the communication process","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. The sender forms an idea and encodes it into words, signs or another understandable form. The message travels through a channel or medium. The receiver decodes it and may provide feedback. Feedback tells the sender whether the message was received and understood; it is not the original message travelling forward.\n\nNoise is anything that interferes with communication. It includes loud sound, a weak telephone connection, unfamiliar words, unclear handwriting and distraction. Distance can increase communication difficulties, but suitable technology may overcome it. A communication diagram should be read by following the arrows, not by guessing a letter from its position on the page.\n\nDownward communication passes from a superior to a subordinate, such as a manager giving instructions. Upward communication passes from a subordinate to a superior, such as a sales report. Horizontal or lateral communication occurs between people at comparable organizational levels. A department sending information to another department does not always make it upward or downward.\n\nAn internal memorandum, commonly called a memo, carries official information within an organization. Circulars send the same information to several recipients. Telephones and face-to-face discussion permit quick oral exchange. Notice boards display information where intended readers can see it. Advertising normally addresses customers or the public, so it is not the ordinary channel for confidential departmental instructions.\n\nA procedure states the ordered steps for carrying out a task. A policy supplies a general guide to decisions. A programme coordinates activities and timing to achieve an objective. These are connected management tools, but a step-by-step office method is specifically a procedure.\n\nThe Internet connects networks worldwide. Email is one Internet service for exchanging electronic messages. GSM mobile communication allows users to communicate while moving between suitable network coverage areas. Historical questions may contrast telephone, radio, telegraph and television; identify whether the message is oral, written, visual, public or private before choosing.\n\nWorked example: a Kaduna manager sends branch supervisors a circular about stocktaking. Each supervisor explains the instructions to assistants and sends a completion report back. The instructions are downward; the report is upward; the reply also provides feedback.\n\nA telegram is a written message sent through telegraph services. Telex uses linked teleprinters to transmit typed messages.\n\nReliable communication can reduce travel costs and risks, connect participants and improve coordination of goods delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sending a message does not prove understanding.\n• Feedback and noise are different parts of the process.\n• An advertisement is not normally an internal memo.\n• Choose a medium for the stated audience and purpose, not simply because it reaches many people."}},{"id":"securities-capital-calculations","old":{"title":"Share capital, securities and market participants","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. A debenture or bond records borrowing under stated terms. Ordinary shareholders generally bear greater business risk and receive dividends after prior claims. Debt normally requires interest and repayment according to its terms. Convertible debt combines borrowing with a right to convert into shares; not every ordinary debenture is automatically convertible.\n\nIn historical Nigerian Commerce terminology, authorized or nominal capital is the maximum share capital permitted by the company's constitutional documents. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares offered and allotted. Called-up capital is the amount shareholders have been required to pay. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. Unissued capital is the authorized portion not yet issued. Reserve capital traditionally means uncalled capital reserved for a winding-up call; it must not casually be equated with every unissued or unpaid amount. Modern company-law requirements differ from some historical terminology.\n\nA share premium is the amount received above a share's nominal value and is commonly classified as a capital reserve. Revenue reserves arise from retained trading profits. Owners' equity includes share capital and relevant reserves; it does not include an outside lender's loan merely because the money finances assets.\n\nWorked example: a company has 2,000,000 shares of 50 kobo nominal value and issues 1,000,000 at ₦2 each. Nominal issued capital is ₦500,000. Cash proceeds are ₦2,000,000. The ₦1,500,000 excess is share premium. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders, usually proportionately. A bonus issue capitalizes reserves without fresh subscription cash.\n\nA bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects them to fall. A stag subscribes to new issues hoping to sell soon at a profit. A broker deals for clients. A jobber or dealer buys and sells on the dealer's own account; the spread between buying and selling prices is traditionally called the jobber's turn. Speculation means trading in anticipation of price movements.\n\nAn irredeemable bond has no fixed redemption date under its terms, although interest remains payable. Treasury bills are short-term government securities usually described as low-risk or risk-free in simplified domestic-currency comparisons. Shares can offer some inflation protection through business growth, but their prices and dividends are not guaranteed.\n\nHistorical Nigerian Second-tier Securities Market questions concern eligibility for smaller indigenous firms and a three-year trading record. These past listing rules are not automatically current exchange requirements.\n\nRetained profits avoid flotation costs but still carry an opportunity cost; they are not economically free.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Nominal capital is not always cash proceeds.\n• A premium is not an ordinary trading profit.\n• A stag concerns new issues; a bull concerns expected price rises.\n• “Risk-free” in a classroom comparison does not mean absence of every economic risk."},"new":{"title":"Share capital, securities and market participants","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. A debenture or bond records borrowing under stated terms. Ordinary shareholders generally bear greater business risk and receive dividends after prior claims. Debt normally requires interest and repayment according to its terms. Convertible debt combines borrowing with a right to convert into shares; not every ordinary debenture is automatically convertible.\n\nIn historical Nigerian Commerce terminology, authorized or nominal capital is the maximum share capital permitted by the company's constitutional documents. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares offered and allotted. Called-up capital is the amount shareholders have been required to pay. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. Unissued capital is the authorized portion not yet issued. Reserve capital traditionally means uncalled capital reserved for a winding-up call; it must not casually be equated with every unissued or unpaid amount. Modern company-law requirements differ from some historical terminology.\n\nA share premium is the amount received above a share's nominal value and is commonly classified as a capital reserve. Revenue reserves arise from retained trading profits. Owners' equity includes share capital and relevant reserves; it does not include an outside lender's loan merely because the money finances assets.\n\nWorked example: a company has 2,000,000 shares of 50 kobo nominal value and issues 1,000,000 at ₦2 each. Nominal issued capital is ₦500,000. Cash proceeds are ₦2,000,000. The ₦1,500,000 excess is share premium. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders, usually proportionately. A bonus issue capitalizes reserves without fresh subscription cash.\n\nA bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects them to fall. A stag subscribes to new issues hoping to sell soon at a profit. A broker deals for clients. A jobber or dealer buys and sells on the dealer's own account; the spread between buying and selling prices is traditionally called the jobber's turn. Speculation means trading in anticipation of price movements.\n\nAn irredeemable bond has no fixed redemption date under its terms, although interest remains payable. Treasury bills are short-term government securities usually described as low-risk or risk-free in simplified domestic-currency comparisons. Shares can offer some inflation protection through business growth, but their prices and dividends are not guaranteed.\n\nHistorical Nigerian Second-tier Securities Market questions concern eligibility for smaller indigenous firms and a three-year trading record. These past listing rules are not automatically current exchange requirements.\n\nRetained profits avoid flotation costs but still carry an opportunity cost; they are not economically free.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Nominal capital is not always cash proceeds.\n• A premium is not an ordinary trading profit.\n• A stag concerns new issues; a bull concerns expected price rises.\n• “Risk-free” in a classroom comparison does not mean absence of every economic risk."}},{"id":"banking-policy-payments","old":{"title":"Monetary control, bank facilities and cheque payments","body":"A commercial bank accepts deposits, provides payment services and lends under agreed conditions. A central bank manages currency and monetary policy and serves as banker to government and banks. Development finance institutions support particular development needs; a mortgage institution concentrates on property finance. Historical institutional names should be read in the period of the paper.\n\nThe central bank can influence the quantity of money and bank credit. A lower cash reserve requirement can leave banks with more funds available for lending; a higher requirement tends to restrict that capacity. In open-market operations, buying securities injects money into the banking system, while selling securities withdraws it. Actual lending also depends on demand, risk and other policy conditions. Moral suasion persuades banks to follow guidance rather than directly imposing a numerical tax.\n\nA current account supports frequent business receipts and payments, including cheque facilities under bank rules. A domiciliary account holds permitted foreign currency. A savings account encourages saving and commonly pays interest. A line of credit allows borrowing up to an agreed limit. Discounting a bill means paying its holder before maturity after deducting the agreed discount. Trade credit comes from a supplier allowing later payment, not directly from a bank.\n\nA bank draft is a payment instrument drawn by a bank rather than an ordinary customer's personal cheque. A crossed cheque is directed through a bank account rather than ordinary over-the-counter cash payment. A general crossing alone does not necessarily mean that only the named original payee can obtain title. Account-payee instructions are more specific. “Not negotiable” means a transferee cannot obtain a better title than the transferor; it does not simply prohibit every transfer.\n\nWorked example: a trader receives a crossed cheque from a customer and lodges it with the bank for collection. This provides an account record instead of obtaining cash directly at the drawee bank's counter. If the trader needs regular payments to a supplier, a standing order may suit the recurring instruction.\n\nPaper currency issued under law is legal tender. Cheques and drafts are useful payment instruments, but a creditor is not generally required to accept them as legal tender merely because banks process them.\n\nHistorical school rules commonly treat a cheque unpresented for six months as stale. Bank interest on lending is income; the loan principal is not itself earned revenue.\n\nA bearer cheque is payable to the bearer under its terms; a crossing separately affects the collection route.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Buying and selling securities have opposite monetary effects.\n• Cash reserve ratio is not an ordinary customer's interest rate.\n• A current account is not identical to an overdraft.\n• Read the exact crossing before asserting who can receive payment."},"new":{"title":"Monetary control, bank facilities and cheque payments","body":"A commercial bank accepts deposits, provides payment services and lends under agreed conditions. A central bank manages currency and monetary policy and serves as banker to government and banks. Development finance institutions support particular development needs; a mortgage institution concentrates on property finance. Historical institutional names should be read in the period of the paper.\n\nThe central bank can influence the quantity of money and bank credit. A lower cash reserve requirement can leave banks with more funds available for lending; a higher requirement tends to restrict that capacity. In open-market operations, buying securities injects money into the banking system, while selling securities withdraws it. Actual lending also depends on demand, risk and other policy conditions. Moral suasion persuades banks to follow guidance rather than directly imposing a numerical tax.\n\nA current account supports frequent business receipts and payments, including cheque facilities under bank rules. A domiciliary account holds permitted foreign currency. A savings account encourages saving and commonly pays interest. A line of credit allows borrowing up to an agreed limit. Discounting a bill means paying its holder before maturity after deducting the agreed discount. Trade credit comes from a supplier allowing later payment, not directly from a bank.\n\nA bank draft is a payment instrument drawn by a bank rather than an ordinary customer's personal cheque. A crossed cheque is directed through a bank account rather than ordinary over-the-counter cash payment. A general crossing alone does not necessarily mean that only the named original payee can obtain title. Account-payee instructions are more specific. “Not negotiable” means a transferee cannot obtain a better title than the transferor; it does not simply prohibit every transfer.\n\nWorked example: a trader receives a crossed cheque from a customer and lodges it with the bank for collection. This provides an account record instead of obtaining cash directly at the drawee bank's counter. If the trader needs regular payments to a supplier, a standing order may suit the recurring instruction.\n\nPaper currency issued under law is legal tender. Cheques and drafts are useful payment instruments, but a creditor is not generally required to accept them as legal tender merely because banks process them.\n\nHistorical school rules commonly treat a cheque unpresented for six months as stale. Bank interest on lending is income; the loan principal is not itself earned revenue.\n\nA bearer cheque is payable to the bearer under its terms; a crossing separately affects the collection route.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Buying and selling securities have opposite monetary effects.\n• Cash reserve ratio is not an ordinary customer's interest rate.\n• A current account is not identical to an overdraft.\n• Read the exact crossing before asserting who can receive payment."}},{"id":"external-trade-terms","old":{"title":"Foreign-trade prices, documents and external accounts","body":"Foreign trade exchanges goods and services between residents of different countries. Visible exports and imports concern physical goods. Invisible transactions include services such as shipping, insurance, consultancy and tourism. An imported service does not become visible trade merely because the customer pays money or receives a printed receipt.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports and imports. The balance of payments records the wider set of transactions between residents and non-residents. Historical school accounts commonly distinguish current and capital items; modern presentations also identify a financial account. Selling foreign assets or using reserves can finance an external deficit, but it is not the same as increasing merchandise exports or permanently removing its underlying causes.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable and may help customs verify goods' values for assessing duty. An indent orders goods. A commercial invoice states goods and prices. A pro forma invoice is a preliminary invoice or quotation supplied before a final sale. A price list simply lists prices. Do not substitute a shipping note for every document with an invoice function.\n\nFOB means free on board under the applicable shipping rule. In the usual school example, the buyer arranges the main carriage and insurance after the seller's on-board delivery responsibility. C and F, traditionally cost and freight, includes freight in the quoted selling price but not the insurance obligation included in CIF. CIF means cost, insurance and freight. Payment of charges, risk and legal ownership are separate questions. Incoterms allocate delivery responsibilities and risk; they do not themselves decide transfer of property in every sale.\n\nA charter party is a contract for hiring a ship or its carrying capacity. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may function as a document of title. Documentary CIF sales commonly involve payment against shipping documents, but the parties' contract and applicable law determine precisely when title passes.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer compares an FOB quotation with a CIF quotation for the same goods. The FOB figure may look lower because main freight and insurance must still be arranged or paid separately. Compare the included obligations before deciding which offer is cheaper.\n\nCountertrade exchanges goods or services under an arrangement that substitutes partly or wholly for payment in foreign currency. Globalization increases international business links, but tariffs, sanctions and other barriers still exist.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Cost, risk and ownership do not always transfer together.\n• Customs duties and ports-authority charges are different.\n• Invisible imports are services, not imported goods that happen to be difficult to see.\n• Financing a payments deficit differs from correcting every cause of it."},"new":{"title":"Foreign-trade prices, documents and external accounts","body":"Foreign trade exchanges goods and services between residents of different countries. Visible exports and imports concern physical goods. Invisible transactions include services such as shipping, insurance, consultancy and tourism. An imported service does not become visible trade merely because the customer pays money or receives a printed receipt.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports and imports. The balance of payments records the wider set of transactions between residents and non-residents. Historical school accounts commonly distinguish current and capital items; modern presentations also identify a financial account. Selling foreign assets or using reserves can finance an external deficit, but it is not the same as increasing merchandise exports or permanently removing its underlying causes.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable and may help customs verify goods' values for assessing duty. An indent orders goods. A commercial invoice states goods and prices. A pro forma invoice is a preliminary invoice or quotation supplied before a final sale. A price list simply lists prices. Do not substitute a shipping note for every document with an invoice function.\n\nFOB means free on board under the applicable shipping rule. In the usual school example, the buyer arranges the main carriage and insurance after the seller's on-board delivery responsibility. C and F, traditionally cost and freight, includes freight in the quoted selling price but not the insurance obligation included in CIF. CIF means cost, insurance and freight. Payment of charges, risk and legal ownership are separate questions. Incoterms allocate delivery responsibilities and risk; they do not themselves decide transfer of property in every sale.\n\nA charter party is a contract for hiring a ship or its carrying capacity. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may function as a document of title. Documentary CIF sales commonly involve payment against shipping documents, but the parties' contract and applicable law determine precisely when title passes.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer compares an FOB quotation with a CIF quotation for the same goods. The FOB figure may look lower because main freight and insurance must still be arranged or paid separately. Compare the included obligations before deciding which offer is cheaper.\n\nCountertrade exchanges goods or services under an arrangement that substitutes partly or wholly for payment in foreign currency. Globalization increases international business links, but tariffs, sanctions and other barriers still exist.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Cost, risk and ownership do not always transfer together.\n• Customs duties and ports-authority charges are different.\n• Invisible imports are services, not imported goods that happen to be difficult to see.\n• Financing a payments deficit differs from correcting every cause of it."}},{"id":"business-legal-protection","old":{"title":"Business rights, intellectual property and sale obligations","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its requirements commonly include offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, lawful purpose and intention to create legal relations. An offer announces willingness to be bound on stated terms when accepted. A condition is an important contractual term; breach may justify rejecting goods and other remedies. A warranty is a subsidiary term whose breach normally gives a damages remedy without the same rejection right. Actual remedies depend on the applicable law and facts.\n\nHistorical sale-of-goods rules imply obligations concerning matters such as title, description, quality and fitness in relevant circumstances. Merchantable quality concerns suitability for ordinary uses, while fitness for a particular purpose depends on the relevant conditions. A buyer should not treat every disappointment as proof that every implied condition was broken. Nor does a seller escape all responsibility simply by asking the buyer to inspect.\n\nAn employee performs duties under the employment relationship. The employer's responsibilities include agreed remuneration, appropriate working arrangements and duties concerning safety. An employee properly incurring authorized expenses for the employer may be entitled to reimbursement or indemnification. Annual leave, job security and working facilities are important, but the best answer to a question depends on the particular right being tested.\n\nA trademark distinguishes one trader's goods or services from another's. A patent protects a qualifying invention under its legal conditions. Industrial design protection concerns the appearance of an article. Copyright protects qualifying original expression, not every unrecorded idea. Historical Nigerian trademark questions may use an initial seven-year registration term followed by renewal periods; do not apply that number indiscriminately to patents, designs or every jurisdiction.\n\nA certificate of incorporation establishes the company's incorporation and legal personality. Older questions may distinguish it from a certificate permitting commencement of certain business. A memorandum concerns the company's constitutional framework; a prospectus invites investment. The corporate name must meet registration requirements and must not improperly conflict with an existing protected name. A company is legally distinct from its shareholders.\n\nWorked example: an employee buys authorized delivery fuel while carrying out the employer's instructions and submits the receipt. Repaying that expense concerns indemnification; it is not the employee's dividend or a new ownership interest in the firm.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A patent, trademark and copyright protect different interests.\n• Incorporation and an invitation to buy shares are different events.\n• An employee's expense reimbursement is not profit sharing.\n• Historical legal terminology should not be taught as a complete statement of today's law."},"new":{"title":"Business rights, intellectual property and sale obligations","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its requirements commonly include offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, lawful purpose and intention to create legal relations. An offer announces willingness to be bound on stated terms when accepted. A condition is an important contractual term; breach may justify rejecting goods and other remedies. A warranty is a subsidiary term whose breach normally gives a damages remedy without the same rejection right. Actual remedies depend on the applicable law and facts.\n\nHistorical sale-of-goods rules imply obligations concerning matters such as title, description, quality and fitness in relevant circumstances. Merchantable quality concerns suitability for ordinary uses, while fitness for a particular purpose depends on the relevant conditions. A buyer should not treat every disappointment as proof that every implied condition was broken. Nor does a seller escape all responsibility simply by asking the buyer to inspect.\n\nAn employee performs duties under the employment relationship. The employer's responsibilities include agreed remuneration, appropriate working arrangements and duties concerning safety. An employee properly incurring authorized expenses for the employer may be entitled to reimbursement or indemnification. Annual leave, job security and working facilities are important, but the best answer to a question depends on the particular right being tested.\n\nA trademark distinguishes one trader's goods or services from another's. A patent protects a qualifying invention under its legal conditions. Industrial design protection concerns the appearance of an article. Copyright protects qualifying original expression, not every unrecorded idea. Historical Nigerian trademark questions may use an initial seven-year registration term followed by renewal periods; do not apply that number indiscriminately to patents, designs or every jurisdiction.\n\nA certificate of incorporation establishes the company's incorporation and legal personality. Older questions may distinguish it from a certificate permitting commencement of certain business. A memorandum concerns the company's constitutional framework; a prospectus invites investment. The corporate name must meet registration requirements and must not improperly conflict with an existing protected name. A company is legally distinct from its shareholders.\n\nWorked example: an employee buys authorized delivery fuel while carrying out the employer's instructions and submits the receipt. Repaying that expense concerns indemnification; it is not the employee's dividend or a new ownership interest in the firm.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A patent, trademark and copyright protect different interests.\n• Incorporation and an invitation to buy shares are different events.\n• An employee's expense reimbursement is not profit sharing.\n• Historical legal terminology should not be taught as a complete statement of today's law."}},{"id":"marketing-practice","old":{"title":"Marketing mix, packaging and personal selling","body":"Marketing identifies customer needs and organizes activities that satisfy them while achieving the firm's objectives. The marketing mix consists of controllable variables commonly grouped as the four Ps: product, price, place and promotion. Firms adjust these together rather than treating advertising alone as the whole marketing function.\n\nProduct includes the design, quality, brand, packaging and services attached to the offer. Packaging means developing and providing the container or wrapper for a product. Its graphics can identify the brand, explain use and attract attention; protection and convenience are also important. Branding identifies and distinguishes an offer. Changing the packet is not necessarily changing the physical product inside it.\n\nPrice includes the amount charged, discounts, credit terms and payment conditions. Place concerns channels and distribution arrangements that make the product available. Promotion communicates with customers and other relevant groups. The four Ps use place, not property, and promotion, not production. Producing a good is important, but it is not the missing fourth P in that standard list.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor. Personal selling involves direct interaction with a prospective buyer. It permits questions, explanation and demonstrations, which are useful for complex industrial equipment. Sales promotion uses incentives such as free samples, gifts, competitions or temporary price reductions. Public relations builds understanding and goodwill between the organization and its publics. Advertising and public relations both communicate, but differ in purpose and degree of control over particular messages.\n\nCustomer service includes assistance before, during and after a sale. Reliable advice, prompt responses, installation, repairs and fair complaint handling can retain customers. A single gift cannot compensate indefinitely for unsafe goods or poor service. Giving credit can increase sales, but it also introduces collection costs and default risk; increased profit is not guaranteed.\n\nWorked example: a Kano machinery seller demonstrates a sewing machine, answers a tailor's questions and explains servicing. That is personal selling supported by customer service. A free starter pack offered for one week is sales promotion. A newspaper announcement paid for by the seller is advertising. The shop's location and delivery service belong to place.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product demonstration can support more than one promotional activity; read the setting.\n• Promotion is wider than advertising.\n• Packaging combines protection and communication, not only decoration.\n• Higher sales from credit do not automatically mean higher net profit.\n• Customer service continues after the money has been collected."},"new":{"title":"Marketing mix, packaging and personal selling","body":"Marketing identifies customer needs and organizes activities that satisfy them while achieving the firm's objectives. The marketing mix consists of controllable variables commonly grouped as the four Ps: product, price, place and promotion. Firms adjust these together rather than treating advertising alone as the whole marketing function.\n\nProduct includes the design, quality, brand, packaging and services attached to the offer. Packaging means developing and providing the container or wrapper for a product. Its graphics can identify the brand, explain use and attract attention; protection and convenience are also important. Branding identifies and distinguishes an offer. Changing the packet is not necessarily changing the physical product inside it.\n\nPrice includes the amount charged, discounts, credit terms and payment conditions. Place concerns channels and distribution arrangements that make the product available. Promotion communicates with customers and other relevant groups. The four Ps use place, not property, and promotion, not production. Producing a good is important, but it is not the missing fourth P in that standard list.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor. Personal selling involves direct interaction with a prospective buyer. It permits questions, explanation and demonstrations, which are useful for complex industrial equipment. Sales promotion uses incentives such as free samples, gifts, competitions or temporary price reductions. Public relations builds understanding and goodwill between the organization and its publics. Advertising and public relations both communicate, but differ in purpose and degree of control over particular messages.\n\nCustomer service includes assistance before, during and after a sale. Reliable advice, prompt responses, installation, repairs and fair complaint handling can retain customers. A single gift cannot compensate indefinitely for unsafe goods or poor service. Giving credit can increase sales, but it also introduces collection costs and default risk; increased profit is not guaranteed.\n\nWorked example: a Kano machinery seller demonstrates a sewing machine, answers a tailor's questions and explains servicing. That is personal selling supported by customer service. A free starter pack offered for one week is sales promotion. A newspaper announcement paid for by the seller is advertising. The shop's location and delivery service belong to place.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product demonstration can support more than one promotional activity; read the setting.\n• Promotion is wider than advertising.\n• Packaging combines protection and communication, not only decoration.\n• Higher sales from credit do not automatically mean higher net profit.\n• Customer service continues after the money has been collected."}},{"id":"business-environment-institutions","old":{"title":"Business environment, product safety and public institutions","body":"The business environment consists of conditions that influence business decisions and performance. Internal conditions include staff, management, finance, resources and organizational culture. External conditions include customers, competitors, laws, economic trends, social patterns and technology. The macro-environment refers to broad outside forces rather than one firm's internal instructions.\n\nDemographic factors describe population characteristics such as age distribution, educational level, size and ethnic composition. Cultural factors concern values, beliefs, customs and accepted behaviour. Economic factors include income, inflation and interest rates. Technological factors include new production methods, communication systems and products. Technology may make an older product obsolete even when it still works physically. Obsolescence is loss of usefulness or market appeal, not necessarily complete mechanical failure.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business on employees, consumers, the community and the natural environment. Businesses should supply safe goods, provide accurate information and manage waste responsibly. They cannot transfer every duty for product quality to a regulator. Meeting a contract is an obligation; maintaining a safe and tidy operating environment also expresses responsibility to workers and neighbours.\n\nThe Standards Organisation of Nigeria concerns relevant standards and product conformity. NAFDAC means National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control; its familiar slogan is “Safeguarding the health of the nation.” The Manufacturers Association of Nigeria represents manufacturers' interests. These organizations have different functions and should not be selected merely because all their names concern products or business.\n\nConsumerism is an organized effort to protect consumers' interests and secure fair treatment. It concerns harmful products, misleading claims and other exploitative practices, not simply discouraging one form of consumption. Quality, safety, information, choice and redress must work together. A customer who knows a product is unsafe has not thereby received adequate protection from its hazards.\n\nPollution can affect air, water and land; noise can also harm health and welfare. Gas flaring releases pollutants, while untreated industrial and domestic wastes create other hazards. Claims about the single “most dangerous” or “main” source need a defined place, period and measure. A short examination phrase should not become an unsupported universal ranking.\n\nWorked example: a Port Harcourt producer installs waste treatment, labels products accurately and investigates complaints. These actions address environmental and consumer responsibilities. Advertising its concern without changing harmful conduct would not provide the same protection.\n\nThe historical Consumer Protection Council had a broad consumer-protection role; modern Nigerian competition and consumer-protection institutions have different names and statutory arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A demographic description is not an internal management policy.\n• A trade association is not automatically a statutory regulator.\n• New technology can cause obsolescence without physical damage.\n• Pollution categories can overlap in one incident."},"new":{"title":"Business environment, product safety and public institutions","body":"The business environment consists of conditions that influence business decisions and performance. Internal conditions include staff, management, finance, resources and organizational culture. External conditions include customers, competitors, laws, economic trends, social patterns and technology. The macro-environment refers to broad outside forces rather than one firm's internal instructions.\n\nDemographic factors describe population characteristics such as age distribution, educational level, size and ethnic composition. Cultural factors concern values, beliefs, customs and accepted behaviour. Economic factors include income, inflation and interest rates. Technological factors include new production methods, communication systems and products. Technology may make an older product obsolete even when it still works physically. Obsolescence is loss of usefulness or market appeal, not necessarily complete mechanical failure.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business on employees, consumers, the community and the natural environment. Businesses should supply safe goods, provide accurate information and manage waste responsibly. They cannot transfer every duty for product quality to a regulator. Meeting a contract is an obligation; maintaining a safe and tidy operating environment also expresses responsibility to workers and neighbours.\n\nThe Standards Organisation of Nigeria concerns relevant standards and product conformity. NAFDAC means National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control; its familiar slogan is “Safeguarding the health of the nation.” The Manufacturers Association of Nigeria represents manufacturers' interests. These organizations have different functions and should not be selected merely because all their names concern products or business.\n\nConsumerism is an organized effort to protect consumers' interests and secure fair treatment. It concerns harmful products, misleading claims and other exploitative practices, not simply discouraging one form of consumption. Quality, safety, information, choice and redress must work together. A customer who knows a product is unsafe has not thereby received adequate protection from its hazards.\n\nPollution can affect air, water and land; noise can also harm health and welfare. Gas flaring releases pollutants, while untreated industrial and domestic wastes create other hazards. Claims about the single “most dangerous” or “main” source need a defined place, period and measure. A short examination phrase should not become an unsupported universal ranking.\n\nWorked example: a Port Harcourt producer installs waste treatment, labels products accurately and investigates complaints. These actions address environmental and consumer responsibilities. Advertising its concern without changing harmful conduct would not provide the same protection.\n\nThe historical Consumer Protection Council had a broad consumer-protection role; modern Nigerian competition and consumer-protection institutions have different names and statutory arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A demographic description is not an internal management policy.\n• A trade association is not automatically a statutory regulator.\n• New technology can cause obsolescence without physical damage.\n• Pollution categories can overlap in one incident."}},{"id":"financial-statements-capital","old":{"title":"Financial statements, equity and capital calculations","body":"A statement of financial position, traditionally called a balance sheet, shows assets, liabilities and owners’ equity at a stated date. Assets are resources controlled by the business. Liabilities are obligations owed to other parties. Owners’ equity is the residual claim after liabilities are deducted from assets. The accounting equation is assets equal equity plus liabilities.\n\nFixed or non-current assets support operations over longer periods, such as machinery and buildings. Current assets include stock, debtors, bank and cash expected to be realized within the relevant cycle. Current liabilities include amounts due within that cycle or the normal short-term period. Classification concerns the nature and timing of an item, not simply whether its number is large.\n\nOwners’ equity includes contributed share capital and relevant reserves. Long-term loans are not owners’ equity. Older school questions sometimes call capital a liability of the business to its proprietor because the business and owner are treated separately. Modern statements normally distinguish equity from liabilities. Explain that accounting-entity idea rather than claiming that an owner's ordinary investment is a bank debt.\n\nNet working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. It may be negative. A table showing ₦5 million of current assets and ₦11 million of current liabilities gives negative ₦6 million, not positive ₦6 million. Do not remove the minus sign merely because all offered options are positive. Check the table's totals when a printed number appears malformed, and disclose any inference needed.\n\nCapital employed commonly means total assets less current liabilities, or equivalently owners’ equity plus long-term debt. Fixed assets plus net working capital gives the same result when classifications are consistent. Some older questions use capital employed loosely for all fixed and current assets, meaning gross capital. The explanation must distinguish that gross usage from the usual net measure instead of teaching two contradictory formulas without context.\n\nWorked example: a business has fixed assets of ₦180,000, investments of ₦100,000 and current assets of ₦60,000. Current liabilities are ₦40,000. Total assets are ₦340,000; working capital is ₦20,000; capital employed is ₦300,000. The total assets and capital employed are different figures.\n\nHistorical balance sheets may show fictitious assets, such as unwritten-off expenses. For adjusted productive capital, deduct these balances from assets and equity before applying the capital-employed formula. State the convention: retaining them gives a different unadjusted figure.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A reserve can belong to equity without being a separate pile of cash.\n• A current liability is subtracted when finding net working capital.\n• Capital employed is not automatically the first row labelled capital.\n• Missing or inconsistent source figures should be explained, not silently invented."},"new":{"title":"Financial statements, equity and capital calculations","body":"A statement of financial position, traditionally called a balance sheet, shows assets, liabilities and owners’ equity at a stated date. Assets are resources controlled by the business. Liabilities are obligations owed to other parties. Owners’ equity is the residual claim after liabilities are deducted from assets. The accounting equation is assets equal equity plus liabilities.\n\nFixed or non-current assets support operations over longer periods, such as machinery and buildings. Current assets include stock, debtors, bank and cash expected to be realized within the relevant cycle. Current liabilities include amounts due within that cycle or the normal short-term period. Classification concerns the nature and timing of an item, not simply whether its number is large.\n\nOwners’ equity includes contributed share capital and relevant reserves. Long-term loans are not owners’ equity. Older school questions sometimes call capital a liability of the business to its proprietor because the business and owner are treated separately. Modern statements normally distinguish equity from liabilities. Explain that accounting-entity idea rather than claiming that an owner's ordinary investment is a bank debt.\n\nNet working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. It may be negative. A table showing ₦5 million of current assets and ₦11 million of current liabilities gives negative ₦6 million, not positive ₦6 million. Do not remove the minus sign merely because all offered options are positive. Check the table's totals when a printed number appears malformed, and disclose any inference needed.\n\nCapital employed commonly means total assets less current liabilities, or equivalently owners’ equity plus long-term debt. Fixed assets plus net working capital gives the same result when classifications are consistent. Some older questions use capital employed loosely for all fixed and current assets, meaning gross capital. The explanation must distinguish that gross usage from the usual net measure instead of teaching two contradictory formulas without context.\n\nWorked example: a business has fixed assets of ₦180,000, investments of ₦100,000 and current assets of ₦60,000. Current liabilities are ₦40,000. Total assets are ₦340,000; working capital is ₦20,000; capital employed is ₦300,000. The total assets and capital employed are different figures.\n\nHistorical balance sheets may show fictitious assets, such as unwritten-off expenses. For adjusted productive capital, deduct these balances from assets and equity before applying the capital-employed formula. State the convention: retaining them gives a different unadjusted figure.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A reserve can belong to equity without being a separate pile of cash.\n• A current liability is subtracted when finding net working capital.\n• Capital employed is not automatically the first row labelled capital.\n• Missing or inconsistent source figures should be explained, not silently invented."}},{"id":"accounting-depreciation-inventory","old":{"title":"Depreciation, stock, drawings and profit calculations","body":"Accounting records should distinguish the nature of an item before calculations begin. Fixed assets, also called non-current assets, support business operations over longer periods. Current assets are expected to be realized or used within the operating cycle or relevant short-term period. Stock, debtors, prepayments and cash can be current assets; buildings, vehicles and fittings normally belong to fixed assets.\n\nDepreciation allocates a depreciable asset's cost over its useful life. It is not a cash payment into a repair fund and does not necessarily measure the exact fall in market value. Physical capital may lose service potential through use, age or obsolescence. Land itself is not normally depreciated in the same way as a machine, although land improvements can have limited lives.\n\nWhen a question specifies ten per cent depreciation of fixed assets, identify the assets included and the base to which that rate applies. Under a simple one-period cost-based calculation, subtract ten per cent from the relevant original figure. Do not depreciate cash or debtors merely because they appear on the same balance sheet. If the question asks for value before rather than after depreciation, the required answer changes.\n\nDrawings are money or goods taken by an owner for private use. They reduce the owner's capital and are not an ordinary business operating expense. Profit increases capital, while losses reduce it, subject to other capital movements. The proprietor and business are treated as separate accounting entities even when the business is not incorporated.\n\nCost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Closing stock is the unsold stock remaining at the end; it is not found by deducting selling expenses from sales. Carriage on sales is a distribution expense rather than part of the purchase cost of stock. Gross profit is sales less cost of goods sold.\n\nWorked example: a trader has opening stock of ₦1,800, purchases of ₦2,800 and closing stock of ₦350. Cost of goods sold is ₦4,250 before any other relevant adjustment. If carriage on sales is ₦500, that expense does not change the stated ₦350 closing stock.\n\nRate of stock turnover equals cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Rearranging, four turnovers and ₦49,600 average stock imply ₦198,400 cost of goods sold; sales revenue requires further margin information.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Drawings are not wages paid to employees.\n• Depreciation is not charged indiscriminately on every asset.\n• Working capital subtracts current liabilities from current assets.\n• Rate of stock turnover normally uses cost of goods sold, not sales revenue.\n• State whether an answer is before or after a requested depreciation adjustment."},"new":{"title":"Depreciation, stock, drawings and profit calculations","body":"Accounting records should distinguish the nature of an item before calculations begin. Fixed assets, also called non-current assets, support business operations over longer periods. Current assets are expected to be realized or used within the operating cycle or relevant short-term period. Stock, debtors, prepayments and cash can be current assets; buildings, vehicles and fittings normally belong to fixed assets.\n\nDepreciation allocates a depreciable asset's cost over its useful life. It is not a cash payment into a repair fund and does not necessarily measure the exact fall in market value. Physical capital may lose service potential through use, age or obsolescence. Land itself is not normally depreciated in the same way as a machine, although land improvements can have limited lives.\n\nWhen a question specifies ten per cent depreciation of fixed assets, identify the assets included and the base to which that rate applies. Under a simple one-period cost-based calculation, subtract ten per cent from the relevant original figure. Do not depreciate cash or debtors merely because they appear on the same balance sheet. If the question asks for value before rather than after depreciation, the required answer changes.\n\nDrawings are money or goods taken by an owner for private use. They reduce the owner's capital and are not an ordinary business operating expense. Profit increases capital, while losses reduce it, subject to other capital movements. The proprietor and business are treated as separate accounting entities even when the business is not incorporated.\n\nCost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Closing stock is the unsold stock remaining at the end; it is not found by deducting selling expenses from sales. Carriage on sales is a distribution expense rather than part of the purchase cost of stock. Gross profit is sales less cost of goods sold.\n\nWorked example: a trader has opening stock of ₦1,800, purchases of ₦2,800 and closing stock of ₦350. Cost of goods sold is ₦4,250 before any other relevant adjustment. If carriage on sales is ₦500, that expense does not change the stated ₦350 closing stock.\n\nRate of stock turnover equals cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Rearranging, four turnovers and ₦49,600 average stock imply ₦198,400 cost of goods sold; sales revenue requires further margin information.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Drawings are not wages paid to employees.\n• Depreciation is not charged indiscriminately on every asset.\n• Working capital subtracts current liabilities from current assets.\n• Rate of stock turnover normally uses cost of goods sold, not sales revenue.\n• State whether an answer is before or after a requested depreciation adjustment."}},{"id":"international-trade-economics","old":{"title":"Comparative advantage, trade barriers and external balances","body":"International trade involves exchange between residents of different countries. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage specialization and trade. Currency differences, transport costs, legal requirements and trade restrictions can make international transactions more difficult than a simple domestic sale.\n\nAbsolute advantage means producing more output from a given amount of resources, or using fewer resources for a given output. Comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost. Opportunity cost is the next-best alternative forgone. Comparative advantage explains why countries can benefit from specialization and exchange even when one country is more efficient in producing both goods. It is not the same as having the larger population or the strongest currency.\n\nA tariff is a tax on imports or exports under the applicable law. A quota limits quantity. Embargoes and sanctions restrict specified trade for policy reasons. Customs duties are artificial barriers because governments impose them; distance is a natural obstacle. Standards can protect safety but can also make compliance costly. Globalization increases international connections without removing every barrier.\n\nDumping commonly means selling an exported product below its normal value under the relevant trade-law test. Older school questions may describe sales below cost or at very low prices. Do not teach every foreign discount as legally established dumping: the comparison basis and evidence matter. Devaluation concerns a deliberate reduction in a currency's official value under the relevant exchange-rate arrangement, not a seller reducing a product's price.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports with merchandise imports. The balance of payments records wider transactions between residents and non-residents. Traditional school presentations may use current, capital and monetary-movement accounts; modern statistical accounts distinguish financial transactions more explicitly. Current transactions include goods and services, while capital and financial transactions concern different kinds of transfers and claims.\n\nWorked example: Country A gives up two bags of maize to produce one unit of cloth, while Country B gives up four bags. A has the lower opportunity cost in cloth. This comparison, rather than the money price alone, identifies comparative advantage in the simplified example.\n\nTerms of trade compare export prices with import prices, commonly as an export-price index divided by an import-price index, multiplied by 100. A rise means a given quantity of exports purchases more imports; this differs from the value-based balance of trade.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A balance-of-payments problem is broader than a merchandise deficit.\n• A customs duty is not a physical transport distance.\n• Comparative advantage concerns opportunity cost, not simply absolute output.\n• Currency changes can help some traders and hurt others; their effect is not identical in every transaction.\n• Historical accounting headings must be explained rather than silently replaced."},"new":{"title":"Comparative advantage, trade barriers and external balances","body":"International trade involves exchange between residents of different countries. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage specialization and trade. Currency differences, transport costs, legal requirements and trade restrictions can make international transactions more difficult than a simple domestic sale.\n\nAbsolute advantage means producing more output from a given amount of resources, or using fewer resources for a given output. Comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost. Opportunity cost is the next-best alternative forgone. Comparative advantage explains why countries can benefit from specialization and exchange even when one country is more efficient in producing both goods. It is not the same as having the larger population or the strongest currency.\n\nA tariff is a tax on imports or exports under the applicable law. A quota limits quantity. Embargoes and sanctions restrict specified trade for policy reasons. Customs duties are artificial barriers because governments impose them; distance is a natural obstacle. Standards can protect safety but can also make compliance costly. Globalization increases international connections without removing every barrier.\n\nDumping commonly means selling an exported product below its normal value under the relevant trade-law test. Older school questions may describe sales below cost or at very low prices. Do not teach every foreign discount as legally established dumping: the comparison basis and evidence matter. Devaluation concerns a deliberate reduction in a currency's official value under the relevant exchange-rate arrangement, not a seller reducing a product's price.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports with merchandise imports. The balance of payments records wider transactions between residents and non-residents. Traditional school presentations may use current, capital and monetary-movement accounts; modern statistical accounts distinguish financial transactions more explicitly. Current transactions include goods and services, while capital and financial transactions concern different kinds of transfers and claims.\n\nWorked example: Country A gives up two bags of maize to produce one unit of cloth, while Country B gives up four bags. A has the lower opportunity cost in cloth. This comparison, rather than the money price alone, identifies comparative advantage in the simplified example.\n\nTerms of trade compare export prices with import prices, commonly as an export-price index divided by an import-price index, multiplied by 100. A rise means a given quantity of exports purchases more imports; this differs from the value-based balance of trade.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A balance-of-payments problem is broader than a merchandise deficit.\n• A customs duty is not a physical transport distance.\n• Comparative advantage concerns opportunity cost, not simply absolute output.\n• Currency changes can help some traders and hurt others; their effect is not identical in every transaction.\n• Historical accounting headings must be explained rather than silently replaced."}},{"id":"shipping-orders-postal-documents","old":{"title":"Import orders, shipping receipts and delivery documents","body":"Trade documents serve different purposes at different stages. An enquiry asks about goods and terms. A quotation gives an offered price and conditions. An order states what the buyer wants to purchase. An invoice records goods supplied or charged for. A delivery note accompanies goods so that the receiver can check the consignment; it is not the same as a receipt for money paid.\n\nAn indent is an order for goods, commonly in foreign trade. A closed indent specifies the manufacturer or supplier from whom goods should be obtained. An open indent gives the agent discretion to select a suitable supplier within the order's instructions. “Open” does not mean that there are no limits on price, quality or accountability.\n\nA consignment note records goods handed to a carrier and the relevant delivery instructions. An advice note informs the buyer that goods have been dispatched and may arrive separately from them. A freight note concerns the carriage charge. An exporter should match the required document to its function rather than choosing whichever name sounds most international.\n\nA mate's receipt acknowledges cargo received on board or into the ship's custody under the relevant procedure. It can be exchanged for a bill of lading. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may operate as a document of title. It is not a customs invoice. A dock warrant acknowledges goods held by a dock or warehouse operator and can have a title function under its terms. A bill of sight permits provisional customs entry when full particulars are unavailable; it does not replace every storage receipt.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable. A certificate of origin states the country in which goods originate. These do not perform the same job as an indent ordering goods.\n\nFranco is a traditional delivered-price term under which the quotation includes the specified delivery to the buyer's destination. Compare its stated destination with FOB, where the buyer usually bears the main freight and insurance after the relevant on-board delivery. The parties' agreement controls the precise obligations.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian importer sends an open indent through an agent, receives an invoice and uses shipping documents to arrange collection. The agent's freedom to choose a manufacturer does not make the invoice a purchase order.\n\nPoste restante holds letters for travellers to collect at a post office; courier services deliver urgent documents through dedicated arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Delivery of goods and payment of money need different evidence.\n• A mate's receipt and a consular invoice are not interchangeable.\n• A storage receipt is not automatically proof of customs-duty payment.\n• Read the destination named in a delivered-price quotation."},"new":{"title":"Import orders, shipping receipts and delivery documents","body":"Trade documents serve different purposes at different stages. An enquiry asks about goods and terms. A quotation gives an offered price and conditions. An order states what the buyer wants to purchase. An invoice records goods supplied or charged for. A delivery note accompanies goods so that the receiver can check the consignment; it is not the same as a receipt for money paid.\n\nAn indent is an order for goods, commonly in foreign trade. A closed indent specifies the manufacturer or supplier from whom goods should be obtained. An open indent gives the agent discretion to select a suitable supplier within the order's instructions. “Open” does not mean that there are no limits on price, quality or accountability.\n\nA consignment note records goods handed to a carrier and the relevant delivery instructions. An advice note informs the buyer that goods have been dispatched and may arrive separately from them. A freight note concerns the carriage charge. An exporter should match the required document to its function rather than choosing whichever name sounds most international.\n\nA mate's receipt acknowledges cargo received on board or into the ship's custody under the relevant procedure. It can be exchanged for a bill of lading. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may operate as a document of title. It is not a customs invoice. A dock warrant acknowledges goods held by a dock or warehouse operator and can have a title function under its terms. A bill of sight permits provisional customs entry when full particulars are unavailable; it does not replace every storage receipt.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable. A certificate of origin states the country in which goods originate. These do not perform the same job as an indent ordering goods.\n\nFranco is a traditional delivered-price term under which the quotation includes the specified delivery to the buyer's destination. Compare its stated destination with FOB, where the buyer usually bears the main freight and insurance after the relevant on-board delivery. The parties' agreement controls the precise obligations.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian importer sends an open indent through an agent, receives an invoice and uses shipping documents to arrange collection. The agent's freedom to choose a manufacturer does not make the invoice a purchase order.\n\nPoste restante holds letters for travellers to collect at a post office; courier services deliver urgent documents through dedicated arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Delivery of goods and payment of money need different evidence.\n• A mate's receipt and a consular invoice are not interchangeable.\n• A storage receipt is not automatically proof of customs-duty payment.\n• Read the destination named in a delivered-price quotation."}},{"id":"employment-staffing-industrial-relations","old":{"title":"Staffing, worker relations and team leadership","body":"Staffing is the management function concerned with obtaining and developing suitable people for organizational work. Recruitment attracts candidates. Selection assesses applicants and chooses suitable people. Appointment engages the chosen person, while placement assigns that person to a position. Induction introduces the employee to the job and organization. Different textbooks group these stages slightly differently, so compare the actual activities rather than merely memorizing a three-word sequence.\n\nTraining develops knowledge and skills needed for work. A worker's education, training, experience and competence help distinguish skilled from unskilled work in classroom classifications. This does not mean that work described as unskilled requires no effort or learning. Remuneration includes wages, salaries and other agreed rewards for work. Fair pay, recognition and suitable conditions can help motivate employees.\n\nDirecting guides and encourages employees to carry out plans. Motivation is therefore commonly placed under directing, although staffing and reward systems also influence it. Planning establishes objectives; organizing assigns work and authority; controlling compares actual performance with standards and arranges correction. A manager should not call every action controlling simply because a superior is involved.\n\nA trade union represents workers' collective interests. An employers' association represents employers. A trade association represents members in a trade, and a chamber of commerce promotes business across different trades. Similar activities such as representation do not make these bodies identical. Classifying a named organization requires its actual membership and legal or institutional role.\n\nIndustrial relations concerns relationships between employers and workers, including negotiation and settlement of disputes. Collective bargaining seeks agreement on matters such as pay and conditions. Unresolved disputes may go through conciliation, arbitration or the appropriate industrial court under the applicable law. In historical Nigerian questions, the Industrial Arbitration Panel or similar arbitration terminology concerns labour disputes; it is not a general code-of-conduct bureau.\n\nEsprit de corps means team spirit: cooperation and a sense of shared belonging. Unity of command concerns one immediate reporting authority, while unity of direction concerns coordinated plans for related objectives. They support teamwork but are not alternative spellings of the same principle.\n\nWorked example: a supervisor explains a target, trains workers, recognizes good performance and helps settle a scheduling dispute. These actions combine direction, development and worker relations rather than replacing all management with recruitment.\n\nNURTW is formally a transport workers’ union; older papers may use trade association loosely.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Recruitment is wider than interviewing one applicant.\n• An appointment and the job placement are related but distinct stages.\n• Worker representation differs from a manufacturers' business association.\n• Narrow spans can underuse a capable supervisor; wide spans can overload one."},"new":{"title":"Staffing, worker relations and team leadership","body":"Staffing is the management function concerned with obtaining and developing suitable people for organizational work. Recruitment attracts candidates. Selection assesses applicants and chooses suitable people. Appointment engages the chosen person, while placement assigns that person to a position. Induction introduces the employee to the job and organization. Different textbooks group these stages slightly differently, so compare the actual activities rather than merely memorizing a three-word sequence.\n\nTraining develops knowledge and skills needed for work. A worker's education, training, experience and competence help distinguish skilled from unskilled work in classroom classifications. This does not mean that work described as unskilled requires no effort or learning. Remuneration includes wages, salaries and other agreed rewards for work. Fair pay, recognition and suitable conditions can help motivate employees.\n\nDirecting guides and encourages employees to carry out plans. Motivation is therefore commonly placed under directing, although staffing and reward systems also influence it. Planning establishes objectives; organizing assigns work and authority; controlling compares actual performance with standards and arranges correction. A manager should not call every action controlling simply because a superior is involved.\n\nA trade union represents workers' collective interests. An employers' association represents employers. A trade association represents members in a trade, and a chamber of commerce promotes business across different trades. Similar activities such as representation do not make these bodies identical. Classifying a named organization requires its actual membership and legal or institutional role.\n\nIndustrial relations concerns relationships between employers and workers, including negotiation and settlement of disputes. Collective bargaining seeks agreement on matters such as pay and conditions. Unresolved disputes may go through conciliation, arbitration or the appropriate industrial court under the applicable law. In historical Nigerian questions, the Industrial Arbitration Panel or similar arbitration terminology concerns labour disputes; it is not a general code-of-conduct bureau.\n\nEsprit de corps means team spirit: cooperation and a sense of shared belonging. Unity of command concerns one immediate reporting authority, while unity of direction concerns coordinated plans for related objectives. They support teamwork but are not alternative spellings of the same principle.\n\nWorked example: a supervisor explains a target, trains workers, recognizes good performance and helps settle a scheduling dispute. These actions combine direction, development and worker relations rather than replacing all management with recruitment.\n\nNURTW is formally a transport workers’ union; older papers may use trade association loosely.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Recruitment is wider than interviewing one applicant.\n• An appointment and the job placement are related but distinct stages.\n• Worker representation differs from a manufacturers' business association.\n• Narrow spans can underuse a capable supervisor; wide spans can overload one."}},{"id":"pricing-promotion-product-strategy","old":{"title":"Pricing strategies, product mix and advertising media","body":"A pricing policy guides how a business sets and changes prices. It should consider costs, demand, competition, objectives and the customers being served. Price is one element of the marketing mix, alongside product, place and promotion. A policy that suits one product and season may be unsuitable for another.\n\nPrice skimming sets a relatively high initial price to obtain returns from buyers willing to pay more, commonly before wider competition or lower-priced versions develop. Penetration pricing uses a relatively low initial price to attract a large market quickly. Neither policy guarantees profit. Target-return pricing seeks a stated return on investment; bidding quotes a price for a particular contract.\n\nVariable pricing changes prices according to relevant conditions, such as season, demand or customer group, within legal and fair-dealing limits. Seasonal demand can make it relevant to warm clothing. Product-line pricing sets related prices across a range of products. A question should provide enough context before claiming that one strategy is universally best.\n\nA product mix is the full range of goods and services a firm offers. Branding gives an offer an identity and distinguishes it from competitors. A brand name can be protected as a trademark when legal requirements are met. A label may show the brand, ingredients or instructions; packaging is the container or wrapper. These functions overlap, but naming the branded word is different from naming the entire packet.\n\nPromotion includes advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations. Informative advertising explains features or availability. Persuasive advertising encourages preference. Competitive advertising stresses a particular brand in relation to rivals. Publicity refers to public attention or information, often through media coverage rather than purchased advertisement space.\n\nBillboards and posters are outdoor media. Newspapers and magazines are print media. Radio communicates through sound, while television combines sound and moving images. Television can demonstrate use and attract several senses but is often costly and requires suitable audience access. Do not call it automatically cheap or universally the best channel.\n\nWorked example: a new phone launches at a high price, later falls in price and receives a competing budget model. The initial strategy may be skimming. A billboard identifies the brand; a demonstration advert shows how it works; a prize contest is sales promotion.\n\nA billboard reaches people near its display; its local visibility can be effective but geographically limited. No medium is universally most effective.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product mix is not only a list of advertisements.\n• A brand and a patent protect different interests.\n• An advertising objective differs from its chosen medium.\n• A familiar slogan can persuade without changing the physical product."},"new":{"title":"Pricing strategies, product mix and advertising media","body":"A pricing policy guides how a business sets and changes prices. It should consider costs, demand, competition, objectives and the customers being served. Price is one element of the marketing mix, alongside product, place and promotion. A policy that suits one product and season may be unsuitable for another.\n\nPrice skimming sets a relatively high initial price to obtain returns from buyers willing to pay more, commonly before wider competition or lower-priced versions develop. Penetration pricing uses a relatively low initial price to attract a large market quickly. Neither policy guarantees profit. Target-return pricing seeks a stated return on investment; bidding quotes a price for a particular contract.\n\nVariable pricing changes prices according to relevant conditions, such as season, demand or customer group, within legal and fair-dealing limits. Seasonal demand can make it relevant to warm clothing. Product-line pricing sets related prices across a range of products. A question should provide enough context before claiming that one strategy is universally best.\n\nA product mix is the full range of goods and services a firm offers. Branding gives an offer an identity and distinguishes it from competitors. A brand name can be protected as a trademark when legal requirements are met. A label may show the brand, ingredients or instructions; packaging is the container or wrapper. These functions overlap, but naming the branded word is different from naming the entire packet.\n\nPromotion includes advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations. Informative advertising explains features or availability. Persuasive advertising encourages preference. Competitive advertising stresses a particular brand in relation to rivals. Publicity refers to public attention or information, often through media coverage rather than purchased advertisement space.\n\nBillboards and posters are outdoor media. Newspapers and magazines are print media. Radio communicates through sound, while television combines sound and moving images. Television can demonstrate use and attract several senses but is often costly and requires suitable audience access. Do not call it automatically cheap or universally the best channel.\n\nWorked example: a new phone launches at a high price, later falls in price and receives a competing budget model. The initial strategy may be skimming. A billboard identifies the brand; a demonstration advert shows how it works; a prize contest is sales promotion.\n\nA billboard reaches people near its display; its local visibility can be effective but geographically limited. No medium is universally most effective.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product mix is not only a list of advertisements.\n• A brand and a patent protect different interests.\n• An advertising objective differs from its chosen medium.\n• A familiar slogan can persuade without changing the physical product."}},{"id":"networks-security-digital-information","old":{"title":"Computer networks, security and digital information","body":"A computer network links devices so they can communicate and share resources. The Internet is a worldwide network of networks. An intranet is a private organizational network using related technologies. An extranet extends authorized access to selected outside parties, such as suppliers. An intranet is not normally open to every member of the public.\n\nA modem enables communication over a suitable connection by converting signals as required by the communication system. A web browser retrieves and displays web content, including text, images and video. A web server supplies resources to browsers. HTTP is a communication protocol; HTML is a markup language used to structure web pages. Neither is simply the name of a physical storage disk.\n\nIn an email address such as ada@example.com, the @ sign separates the local username from the domain identifying the receiving mail system. It is not a password. Historic telegram charges commonly depended on the number of chargeable words. Communication satellites can relay long-distance signals, including international telephone traffic. Modern links may also use terrestrial cables and other networks; satellite is not the only possible route.\n\nSystem software manages the computer and supports its operation. Operating systems such as Windows coordinate hardware and applications. Utility programs perform support tasks such as backup, security or file maintenance. Application software performs user tasks, such as word processing or preparing accounts. Registers are hardware storage locations, not application packages.\n\nA hard copy is printed output. A soft copy is information held or displayed electronically. Main memory supports current processing, while cache is small, fast memory that keeps frequently needed data near the processor. Historical classifications may treat cache within primary storage, but cache is not identical to all main memory. Secondary drives retain files; they do not replace the CPU's instruction-processing role.\n\nEncryption transforms data into coded form; decryption reverses the appropriate coding with the required method or key. Malware is malicious software, including viruses. Antivirus tools detect and remove or quarantine threats. Debugging finds and corrects program errors; it should not be taught as the precise synonym for virus removal.\n\nWorked example: an Ibadan firm lets employees use an intranet and gives a supplier limited extranet access. A browser displays its ordering page, and security utilities help protect the computers. Printing an order produces a hard copy.\n\nA management information system supplies organized information to support managerial decisions; it is not merely a file-transfer protocol.\n\nCybercafés face operating costs.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A browser and a web server have different roles.\n• HTML is a markup language; HTTP is a protocol.\n• Removing a virus is not merely correcting an ordinary programming mistake.\n• Downloading information requires a working connection, not only an empty storage disk."},"new":{"title":"Computer networks, security and digital information","body":"A computer network links devices so they can communicate and share resources. The Internet is a worldwide network of networks. An intranet is a private organizational network using related technologies. An extranet extends authorized access to selected outside parties, such as suppliers. An intranet is not normally open to every member of the public.\n\nA modem enables communication over a suitable connection by converting signals as required by the communication system. A web browser retrieves and displays web content, including text, images and video. A web server supplies resources to browsers. HTTP is a communication protocol; HTML is a markup language used to structure web pages. Neither is simply the name of a physical storage disk.\n\nIn an email address such as ada@example.com, the @ sign separates the local username from the domain identifying the receiving mail system. It is not a password. Historic telegram charges commonly depended on the number of chargeable words. Communication satellites can relay long-distance signals, including international telephone traffic. Modern links may also use terrestrial cables and other networks; satellite is not the only possible route.\n\nSystem software manages the computer and supports its operation. Operating systems such as Windows coordinate hardware and applications. Utility programs perform support tasks such as backup, security or file maintenance. Application software performs user tasks, such as word processing or preparing accounts. Registers are hardware storage locations, not application packages.\n\nA hard copy is printed output. A soft copy is information held or displayed electronically. Main memory supports current processing, while cache is small, fast memory that keeps frequently needed data near the processor. Historical classifications may treat cache within primary storage, but cache is not identical to all main memory. Secondary drives retain files; they do not replace the CPU's instruction-processing role.\n\nEncryption transforms data into coded form; decryption reverses the appropriate coding with the required method or key. Malware is malicious software, including viruses. Antivirus tools detect and remove or quarantine threats. Debugging finds and corrects program errors; it should not be taught as the precise synonym for virus removal.\n\nWorked example: an Ibadan firm lets employees use an intranet and gives a supplier limited extranet access. A browser displays its ordering page, and security utilities help protect the computers. Printing an order produces a hard copy.\n\nA management information system supplies organized information to support managerial decisions; it is not merely a file-transfer protocol.\n\nCybercafés face operating costs.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A browser and a web server have different roles.\n• HTML is a markup language; HTTP is a protocol.\n• Removing a virus is not merely correcting an ordinary programming mistake.\n• Downloading information requires a working connection, not only an empty storage disk."}},{"id":"securities-trading-listing","old":{"title":"Capital-market instruments, listing and investment returns","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance. Its instruments include shares, stocks, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments such as Treasury bills and commercial bills. A broad question offering both stocks and debentures as capital-market instruments may have more than one correct option; the maturity and legal nature of each instrument matter.\n\nA bond is a borrowing instrument with stated terms. Its coupon is the contractual interest rate or payment basis. An ordinary share represents ownership and may provide dividends and capital gains, but neither is guaranteed. A warrant gives a right to subscribe for specified securities under stated terms; it is not itself an ordinary interest-bearing loan.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying and selling admitted securities. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. Investors supply funds and hold securities, while authorized market intermediaries conduct transactions under exchange and regulatory rules. A company being incorporated does not mean that anyone may trade on the exchange without authorization.\n\nThe primary market concerns new securities. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders under specified entitlement terms. The secondary market permits trading existing securities. Daily exchange lists report prices and trading information, helping investors follow market movements. The price at which one investor sells an existing share does not automatically become new cash received by the company.\n\nHistorical Nigerian questions distinguish the main or first-tier market from the Second-tier Securities Market. The latter offered less demanding admission arrangements for smaller indigenous enterprises, including shorter records and particular public-shareholding conditions. Different papers may ask about a percentage offered to the public, shareholder numbers or years of trading. Use the historical rule actually requested rather than assuming that “less restricted” means unregulated. Both operate within a regulatory framework.\n\nWorked example: a firm raises cash by issuing new shares. Later, a shareholder sells through a broker. The first event provides primary-market finance; the second provides liquidity in the secondary market. A published price list helps another investor observe the latest market quotation.\n\nSome historical classroom rules specify at least 100 shareholders; check the exact era and market segment.\n\nHistorical school accounts of the Second-tier market also specify at least 10% offered publicly alongside a minimum of 100 shareholders; these are not permanent current listing requirements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A share dividend differs from a bond coupon.\n• Treasury bills do not become long-term capital-market securities merely because government issues them.\n• The regulator and the exchange perform different functions.\n• Listing requirements and institutional names can change; old examination rules should not be presented as permanent current law.\n• A dealer's own-account trading differs from a broker's client agency."},"new":{"title":"Capital-market instruments, listing and investment returns","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance. Its instruments include shares, stocks, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments such as Treasury bills and commercial bills. A broad question offering both stocks and debentures as capital-market instruments may have more than one correct option; the maturity and legal nature of each instrument matter.\n\nA bond is a borrowing instrument with stated terms. Its coupon is the contractual interest rate or payment basis. An ordinary share represents ownership and may provide dividends and capital gains, but neither is guaranteed. A warrant gives a right to subscribe for specified securities under stated terms; it is not itself an ordinary interest-bearing loan.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying and selling admitted securities. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. Investors supply funds and hold securities, while authorized market intermediaries conduct transactions under exchange and regulatory rules. A company being incorporated does not mean that anyone may trade on the exchange without authorization.\n\nThe primary market concerns new securities. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders under specified entitlement terms. The secondary market permits trading existing securities. Daily exchange lists report prices and trading information, helping investors follow market movements. The price at which one investor sells an existing share does not automatically become new cash received by the company.\n\nHistorical Nigerian questions distinguish the main or first-tier market from the Second-tier Securities Market. The latter offered less demanding admission arrangements for smaller indigenous enterprises, including shorter records and particular public-shareholding conditions. Different papers may ask about a percentage offered to the public, shareholder numbers or years of trading. Use the historical rule actually requested rather than assuming that “less restricted” means unregulated. Both operate within a regulatory framework.\n\nWorked example: a firm raises cash by issuing new shares. Later, a shareholder sells through a broker. The first event provides primary-market finance; the second provides liquidity in the secondary market. A published price list helps another investor observe the latest market quotation.\n\nSome historical classroom rules specify at least 100 shareholders; check the exact era and market segment.\n\nHistorical school accounts of the Second-tier market also specify at least 10% offered publicly alongside a minimum of 100 shareholders; these are not permanent current listing requirements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A share dividend differs from a bond coupon.\n• Treasury bills do not become long-term capital-market securities merely because government issues them.\n• The regulator and the exchange perform different functions.\n• Listing requirements and institutional names can change; old examination rules should not be presented as permanent current law.\n• A dealer's own-account trading differs from a broker's client agency."}},{"id":"business-combinations-cooperation","old":{"title":"Business combinations, partnerships and restrictive arrangements","body":"Businesses may cooperate or combine to obtain finance, expertise, purchasing strength or wider markets. The precise arrangement determines ownership, control and legal obligations. A business combination is not automatically the same as a partnership, and cooperation does not always require the participating firms to surrender their separate identities.\n\nA partnership brings persons together to carry on business with a view to profit. Partners can combine complementary knowledge and skills so that one person's strength offsets another's weakness. An ordinary partnership commonly lacks the separate incorporated personality of a company. The partnership agreement should set out duties, profit sharing and arrangements for change or dissolution.\n\nA merger combines businesses under an agreed structure. A takeover gives one business control of another through the relevant transaction. A holding company controls subsidiary companies through sufficient voting interests. A trust, in historical industrial-organization language, places the affairs of participating businesses under centralized trustees or control. Do not confuse that commercial grouping with every personal trust in law.\n\nA cartel coordinates independent producers, often concerning prices, output or market shares. Participants may agree quotas, dividing a market while remaining separate businesses. OPEC illustrates cooperation among oil-producing countries, though the precise legal treatment differs from private firms' arrangements. Competition law may prohibit price-fixing and similar conduct; a classroom definition does not establish that every cooperative agreement is lawful.\n\nA ring is a historical term for a restrictive arrangement among traders or producers to control prices or terms, sometimes for a particular transaction or market. It is not the same as factoring, which concerns receivables finance. A consortium is a group of organizations cooperating on a project too large or demanding for one alone. A joint venture is a joint undertaking for a particular activity, with the exact duration and legal form determined by agreement.\n\nWorked example: three construction firms form a consortium to build a major bridge using their different equipment and expertise. If independent producers instead divide sales territories and fix prices, their arrangement is cartel-like rather than merely a bridge consortium. If one company buys control of another, that concerns acquisition and group ownership.\n\nForward integration combines a producer with a later distribution stage; backward integration moves toward its suppliers. A syndicate is a cooperative grouping for a common business purpose; distinguish its precise arrangement from a merger or project consortium.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A consortium need not erase each member's separate business.\n• A holding company and its subsidiary are different positions in a group.\n• A partnership combines people and resources, not only friendship.\n• A cartel quota is different from a government's import quota.\n• Check the actual arrangement before choosing a familiar label."},"new":{"title":"Business combinations, partnerships and restrictive arrangements","body":"Businesses may cooperate or combine to obtain finance, expertise, purchasing strength or wider markets. The precise arrangement determines ownership, control and legal obligations. A business combination is not automatically the same as a partnership, and cooperation does not always require the participating firms to surrender their separate identities.\n\nA partnership brings persons together to carry on business with a view to profit. Partners can combine complementary knowledge and skills so that one person's strength offsets another's weakness. An ordinary partnership commonly lacks the separate incorporated personality of a company. The partnership agreement should set out duties, profit sharing and arrangements for change or dissolution.\n\nA merger combines businesses under an agreed structure. A takeover gives one business control of another through the relevant transaction. A holding company controls subsidiary companies through sufficient voting interests. A trust, in historical industrial-organization language, places the affairs of participating businesses under centralized trustees or control. Do not confuse that commercial grouping with every personal trust in law.\n\nA cartel coordinates independent producers, often concerning prices, output or market shares. Participants may agree quotas, dividing a market while remaining separate businesses. OPEC illustrates cooperation among oil-producing countries, though the precise legal treatment differs from private firms' arrangements. Competition law may prohibit price-fixing and similar conduct; a classroom definition does not establish that every cooperative agreement is lawful.\n\nA ring is a historical term for a restrictive arrangement among traders or producers to control prices or terms, sometimes for a particular transaction or market. It is not the same as factoring, which concerns receivables finance. A consortium is a group of organizations cooperating on a project too large or demanding for one alone. A joint venture is a joint undertaking for a particular activity, with the exact duration and legal form determined by agreement.\n\nWorked example: three construction firms form a consortium to build a major bridge using their different equipment and expertise. If independent producers instead divide sales territories and fix prices, their arrangement is cartel-like rather than merely a bridge consortium. If one company buys control of another, that concerns acquisition and group ownership.\n\nForward integration combines a producer with a later distribution stage; backward integration moves toward its suppliers. A syndicate is a cooperative grouping for a common business purpose; distinguish its precise arrangement from a merger or project consortium.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A consortium need not erase each member's separate business.\n• A holding company and its subsidiary are different positions in a group.\n• A partnership combines people and resources, not only friendship.\n• A cartel quota is different from a government's import quota.\n• Check the actual arrangement before choosing a familiar label."}},{"id":"modern-retailing-channels","old":{"title":"Modern retailing, distribution channels and customer service","body":"Retailing is selling goods or services to final consumers for their own use. Wholesaling normally sells to retailers or other business users, commonly in larger quantities. A wholesaler connects producers with many retailers, while retailers provide convenient local access for consumers. The buyer's purpose helps identify the transaction; quantity alone is not always decisive.\n\nRetailers assemble a range of goods, break bulk into convenient quantities, explain products and may offer credit or after-sales service. They can report customers' preferences to suppliers. Wholesalers may also break bulk, hold stock, finance purchases and promote suppliers' goods. These shared functions do not make wholesaling and retailing identical.\n\nA multiple or chain store has branches under common ownership and control. A department store brings several product departments under one roof. A supermarket normally uses self-service, allowing customers to examine and select goods before payment. A vending machine accepts the required payment and dispenses the selected product automatically. An automated teller machine provides banking services and is not simply a shop vending snacks.\n\nModern retailing can combine branded products, attractive display, self-service, online or mail ordering and after-sales support. These are business practices rather than one universally fixed three-item list. A business may combine features of several store formats, but a chain is identified principally by common ownership across branches, not merely by having many departments inside one shop.\n\nManufacturers may sell directly to final consumers to retain the intermediary margin, learn about customers or control distribution. Eliminating a middleman does not eliminate the work of storage, delivery, finance and selling. The producer must perform those functions or pay another provider. Direct selling therefore does not automatically produce the lowest total cost in every case.\n\nAfter-sales service includes installation, repairs, maintenance, assistance and complaint handling after purchase. It can build loyalty and encourage repeat sales. Pre-sales service helps before the order, while the broader customer-service function covers the full relationship. Credit and hire purchase can widen demand but create collection and default risks for the seller.\n\nWorked example: a phone maker supplies a wholesaler, which supplies neighbourhood retailers. A retailer demonstrates a phone, sells it to a student and later helps with a warranty complaint. Buying, selling and servicing concern different stages of the same retail relationship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A chain is not defined simply by one building's many departments.\n• Self-service and after-sales service happen at different stages.\n• A wholesaler is not normally the manufacturer.\n• Removing an intermediary does not remove every distribution expense."},"new":{"title":"Modern retailing, distribution channels and customer service","body":"Retailing is selling goods or services to final consumers for their own use. Wholesaling normally sells to retailers or other business users, commonly in larger quantities. A wholesaler connects producers with many retailers, while retailers provide convenient local access for consumers. The buyer's purpose helps identify the transaction; quantity alone is not always decisive.\n\nRetailers assemble a range of goods, break bulk into convenient quantities, explain products and may offer credit or after-sales service. They can report customers' preferences to suppliers. Wholesalers may also break bulk, hold stock, finance purchases and promote suppliers' goods. These shared functions do not make wholesaling and retailing identical.\n\nA multiple or chain store has branches under common ownership and control. A department store brings several product departments under one roof. A supermarket normally uses self-service, allowing customers to examine and select goods before payment. A vending machine accepts the required payment and dispenses the selected product automatically. An automated teller machine provides banking services and is not simply a shop vending snacks.\n\nModern retailing can combine branded products, attractive display, self-service, online or mail ordering and after-sales support. These are business practices rather than one universally fixed three-item list. A business may combine features of several store formats, but a chain is identified principally by common ownership across branches, not merely by having many departments inside one shop.\n\nManufacturers may sell directly to final consumers to retain the intermediary margin, learn about customers or control distribution. Eliminating a middleman does not eliminate the work of storage, delivery, finance and selling. The producer must perform those functions or pay another provider. Direct selling therefore does not automatically produce the lowest total cost in every case.\n\nAfter-sales service includes installation, repairs, maintenance, assistance and complaint handling after purchase. It can build loyalty and encourage repeat sales. Pre-sales service helps before the order, while the broader customer-service function covers the full relationship. Credit and hire purchase can widen demand but create collection and default risks for the seller.\n\nWorked example: a phone maker supplies a wholesaler, which supplies neighbourhood retailers. A retailer demonstrates a phone, sells it to a student and later helps with a warranty complaint. Buying, selling and servicing concern different stages of the same retail relationship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A chain is not defined simply by one building's many departments.\n• Self-service and after-sales service happen at different stages.\n• A wholesaler is not normally the manufacturer.\n• Removing an intermediary does not remove every distribution expense."}},{"id":"production-labour-distinctions","old":{"title":"Labour, land and production classifications","body":"Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production. Its reward is wages or salaries. Skilled labour requires relevant education, training or acquired expertise. Unskilled labour requires comparatively little specialized training for the particular task. These are classifications of skill, not statements about a worker's intelligence or worth.\n\nManual labour emphasizes physical effort. Blue-collar work commonly means manual, industrial or craft work. White-collar work commonly concerns office, administrative or professional duties. A mechanic or electrician can be both blue-collar and highly skilled. Therefore physical effort alone does not prove that a worker is unskilled. When a source question offers both descriptions without further detail, its explanation must identify that overlap.\n\nLand comprises natural resources used in production, including soil, water, minerals and natural sites. It is relatively fixed in total supply and differs in quality from place to place. This difference is heterogeneity. Land is traditionally called a passive factor because labour and enterprise organize its productive use. Remuneration can motivate workers, while land itself does not become willing to work because it receives rent.\n\nPrimary production obtains resources from nature. Fishing, crabbing, farming, forestry and mineral extraction are examples. Secondary production changes materials into useful products or constructs works. Manufacturing includes processing and factory assembly; construction combines materials into buildings, roads and similar works. A bare phrase such as assembling products needs context to distinguish factory manufacturing from the classroom construction definition.\n\nTertiary production supplies services. Direct services satisfy a particular person's wants, such as a doctor treating a patient. Public services benefit society collectively. Commerce includes trade and its supporting activities; it is not the name for every occupation.\n\nWorked example: a worker in Rivers State catches crabs, another operates a fish-processing machine, and a retailer sells the packaged fish. Catching is primary production, processing is secondary, and retailing is commerce. The machine operator may perform physical work while also needing considerable training.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Blue-collar does not mean unskilled.\n• Land's heterogeneous nature means differing qualities, not an unlimited total supply.\n• A finished iron product is different from iron ore extracted from nature.\n• Read the actual activity before choosing its occupational category."},"new":{"title":"Labour, land and production classifications","body":"Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production. Its reward is wages or salaries. Skilled labour requires relevant education, training or acquired expertise. Unskilled labour requires comparatively little specialized training for the particular task. These are classifications of skill, not statements about a worker's intelligence or worth.\n\nManual labour emphasizes physical effort. Blue-collar work commonly means manual, industrial or craft work. White-collar work commonly concerns office, administrative or professional duties. A mechanic or electrician can be both blue-collar and highly skilled. Therefore physical effort alone does not prove that a worker is unskilled. When a source question offers both descriptions without further detail, its explanation must identify that overlap.\n\nLand comprises natural resources used in production, including soil, water, minerals and natural sites. It is relatively fixed in total supply and differs in quality from place to place. This difference is heterogeneity. Land is traditionally called a passive factor because labour and enterprise organize its productive use. Remuneration can motivate workers, while land itself does not become willing to work because it receives rent.\n\nPrimary production obtains resources from nature. Fishing, crabbing, farming, forestry and mineral extraction are examples. Secondary production changes materials into useful products or constructs works. Manufacturing includes processing and factory assembly; construction combines materials into buildings, roads and similar works. A bare phrase such as assembling products needs context to distinguish factory manufacturing from the classroom construction definition.\n\nTertiary production supplies services. Direct services satisfy a particular person's wants, such as a doctor treating a patient. Public services benefit society collectively. Commerce includes trade and its supporting activities; it is not the name for every occupation.\n\nWorked example: a worker in Rivers State catches crabs, another operates a fish-processing machine, and a retailer sells the packaged fish. Catching is primary production, processing is secondary, and retailing is commerce. The machine operator may perform physical work while also needing considerable training.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Blue-collar does not mean unskilled.\n• Land's heterogeneous nature means differing qualities, not an unlimited total supply.\n• A finished iron product is different from iron ore extracted from nature.\n• Read the actual activity before choosing its occupational category."}},{"id":"insurance-average-benefits-claims","old":{"title":"Average clause, assurance benefits and marine sacrifice","body":"Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, subject to the policy's conditions and limits. It does not permit the insured to profit from that loss. The sum insured is the agreed cover limit; the value at risk is the relevant value of the property. A premium buys cover and is not itself the compensation amount.\n\nAn average clause can reduce a property claim when the property is underinsured. The usual school formula is claim payable equals sum insured divided by value at risk, multiplied by the covered loss. This makes the insured bear the uninsured proportion. Check that the policy actually contains an average clause before applying the formula. Also distinguish partial loss from the entire value of the property.\n\nUtmost good faith, traditionally called uberrimae fidei, concerns honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Deliberately overstating a property's value can breach that duty. Indemnity still prevents a fraudulent profit: writing a high figure on an application does not guarantee an equally high claim payment.\n\nNon-indemnity life assurance pays an agreed benefit on the specified event rather than measuring a person's life as damaged merchandise. Whole-life cover concerns death; an endowment can pay on survival to maturity or earlier death under its conditions. Traditional classroom wording may call a life benefit a consolation payment, but it remains a contractual benefit, not necessarily an ex gratia gift. Ex gratia means payment without admitting a legal obligation.\n\nMarine hull cover concerns the vessel; cargo cover concerns the goods. Jettison is deliberate throwing of cargo overboard to lighten a vessel in danger. A qualifying intentional sacrifice for common safety can give rise to general average. Accidental partial damage does not automatically become general average merely because it occurs at sea.\n\nWorked example: goods worth ₦200,000 are insured for ₦100,000 under an average clause. A covered loss of ₦40,000 produces ₦100,000/₦200,000 × ₦40,000 = ₦20,000 before any other applicable adjustments. The uninsured half remains with the owner.\n\nThe traditional assurance distinction concerns certainty of an eventual event, especially death under whole-life cover; its timing remains uncertain. A printed option saying possibility does not mean certainty. A missing correct term must be explained.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sum insured, property value and actual loss are different figures.\n• A life-assurance benefit is not the resale value of a life.\n• Ex gratia and contractual life benefits are different.\n• Discarding cargo deliberately is jettison, not demurrage."},"new":{"title":"Average clause, assurance benefits and marine sacrifice","body":"Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, subject to the policy's conditions and limits. It does not permit the insured to profit from that loss. The sum insured is the agreed cover limit; the value at risk is the relevant value of the property. A premium buys cover and is not itself the compensation amount.\n\nAn average clause can reduce a property claim when the property is underinsured. The usual school formula is claim payable equals sum insured divided by value at risk, multiplied by the covered loss. This makes the insured bear the uninsured proportion. Check that the policy actually contains an average clause before applying the formula. Also distinguish partial loss from the entire value of the property.\n\nUtmost good faith, traditionally called uberrimae fidei, concerns honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Deliberately overstating a property's value can breach that duty. Indemnity still prevents a fraudulent profit: writing a high figure on an application does not guarantee an equally high claim payment.\n\nNon-indemnity life assurance pays an agreed benefit on the specified event rather than measuring a person's life as damaged merchandise. Whole-life cover concerns death; an endowment can pay on survival to maturity or earlier death under its conditions. Traditional classroom wording may call a life benefit a consolation payment, but it remains a contractual benefit, not necessarily an ex gratia gift. Ex gratia means payment without admitting a legal obligation.\n\nMarine hull cover concerns the vessel; cargo cover concerns the goods. Jettison is deliberate throwing of cargo overboard to lighten a vessel in danger. A qualifying intentional sacrifice for common safety can give rise to general average. Accidental partial damage does not automatically become general average merely because it occurs at sea.\n\nWorked example: goods worth ₦200,000 are insured for ₦100,000 under an average clause. A covered loss of ₦40,000 produces ₦100,000/₦200,000 × ₦40,000 = ₦20,000 before any other applicable adjustments. The uninsured half remains with the owner.\n\nThe traditional assurance distinction concerns certainty of an eventual event, especially death under whole-life cover; its timing remains uncertain. A printed option saying possibility does not mean certainty. A missing correct term must be explained.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sum insured, property value and actual loss are different figures.\n• A life-assurance benefit is not the resale value of a life.\n• Ex gratia and contractual life benefits are different.\n• Discarding cargo deliberately is jettison, not demurrage."}},{"id":"contracts-forms-remedies","old":{"title":"Forms of contract, discharge and the unpaid seller","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. A simple contract normally depends on its substantive requirements, including consideration where required, rather than a special sealed form. A specialty contract is traditionally executed as a deed under the relevant formal rules. A contract of record is an obligation formally recorded or acknowledged before a court, such as a recognizance in traditional classroom examples. These categories should not be confused merely because each has a written record.\n\nA valid contract has legal effect. A void contract has no legal effect as a contract from the outset under the relevant rule. A voidable contract remains effective unless the party entitled to avoid it lawfully does so. An unenforceable agreement may have substantive existence but cannot be enforced through the court in the circumstances. An unrelated third party cannot simply cancel another person's contract at will.\n\nDischarge means the ending of contractual obligations. Performance means carrying out what was promised. Agreement can end or replace an obligation by consent. Breach occurs when a party fails to perform as required; serious breach may entitle the other party to terminate and claim appropriate remedies. It is not successful performance merely because goods were delivered to somebody else.\n\nFrustration concerns an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different. Whether destruction of a particular hired car frustrates a contract depends on the agreement and fault; a party cannot rely on its own breach as automatic frustration.\n\nA lien is a right to retain possession of goods until a relevant debt is paid. It does not by itself mean ownership of the goods. A factor is a mercantile agent entrusted with goods and authority to sell under the agency arrangement. A broker usually brings parties together without ordinary possession of the goods. An agent's authority, possession and right to remuneration must be distinguished.\n\nWorked example: a dealer promises to supply a specific car to Ada but instead supplies it to another buyer on the due date. Ada has not received performance. This illustrates breach, subject to the contract and available remedies, rather than frustration.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A lien is retention of possession, not automatic ownership.\n• Court records and specialty deeds are different categories.\n• Void and voidable have different effects.\n• Delivering to the wrong buyer does not fulfil the original promise."},"new":{"title":"Forms of contract, discharge and the unpaid seller","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. A simple contract normally depends on its substantive requirements, including consideration where required, rather than a special sealed form. A specialty contract is traditionally executed as a deed under the relevant formal rules. A contract of record is an obligation formally recorded or acknowledged before a court, such as a recognizance in traditional classroom examples. These categories should not be confused merely because each has a written record.\n\nA valid contract has legal effect. A void contract has no legal effect as a contract from the outset under the relevant rule. A voidable contract remains effective unless the party entitled to avoid it lawfully does so. An unenforceable agreement may have substantive existence but cannot be enforced through the court in the circumstances. An unrelated third party cannot simply cancel another person's contract at will.\n\nDischarge means the ending of contractual obligations. Performance means carrying out what was promised. Agreement can end or replace an obligation by consent. Breach occurs when a party fails to perform as required; serious breach may entitle the other party to terminate and claim appropriate remedies. It is not successful performance merely because goods were delivered to somebody else.\n\nFrustration concerns an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different. Whether destruction of a particular hired car frustrates a contract depends on the agreement and fault; a party cannot rely on its own breach as automatic frustration.\n\nA lien is a right to retain possession of goods until a relevant debt is paid. It does not by itself mean ownership of the goods. A factor is a mercantile agent entrusted with goods and authority to sell under the agency arrangement. A broker usually brings parties together without ordinary possession of the goods. An agent's authority, possession and right to remuneration must be distinguished.\n\nWorked example: a dealer promises to supply a specific car to Ada but instead supplies it to another buyer on the due date. Ada has not received performance. This illustrates breach, subject to the contract and available remedies, rather than frustration.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A lien is retention of possession, not automatic ownership.\n• Court records and specialty deeds are different categories.\n• Void and voidable have different effects.\n• Delivering to the wrong buyer does not fulfil the original promise."}},{"id":"product-benefits-segmentation","old":{"title":"Product benefits, satisfaction and market segmentation","body":"A product is an offer of goods, services or associated benefits that can satisfy a customer's want. Customers buy the usefulness of the offer, not only its physical materials. The core benefit is the basic need or problem being addressed. For a bus journey, the core benefit is movement to the required destination. For a torch, it is usable light.\n\nThe actual or formal product gives that benefit a particular form. Its features may include design, quality, brand, packaging and other characteristics. An augmented product adds supporting benefits such as installation, warranty or after-sales assistance. These layers are related, but a warranty is not the basic reason light is useful in a dark room.\n\nUtility means want-satisfying ability. Manufacturing can create form utility; transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility; exchange creates possession utility. Physical and psychological satisfaction can accompany a product, but product mix is a firm's range of offerings, not the exact definition of that satisfaction. Where a printed question omits utility or benefit from its options, the omission needs an explicit explanation.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a market into identifiable groups with similar needs or characteristics. A business can then choose target segments and prepare a suitable offer. Common bases include age, income, location, lifestyle and buying behaviour. Segmentation is not merely changing the price, although different segments may receive different legally appropriate offers.\n\nProduct differentiation makes an offer meaningfully different from competing offers through features, design, service or brand image. Positioning concerns the place the offer occupies in the intended customer's mind. Skimming and penetration are pricing strategies, not alternative names for dividing customers into groups.\n\nA marketing orientation starts with identifying and satisfying customer needs while achieving business objectives. A product orientation stresses product characteristics and quality. Knowing customers' desires distinguishes the marketing concept from simply assuming that a technically excellent product will sell itself.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian shoe producer separates school pupils from office workers as customer groups. This is segmentation. Durable soles and an identifiable brand belong to the actual product; repair support adds an augmented benefit. The basic benefit remains suitable footwear.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Core benefit is not identical to brand name.\n• Product mix is a range, not a feeling of satisfaction.\n• Segmentation divides markets; differentiation distinguishes offers.\n• Market skimming sets an introductory price rather than creating customer groups."},"new":{"title":"Product benefits, satisfaction and market segmentation","body":"A product is an offer of goods, services or associated benefits that can satisfy a customer's want. Customers buy the usefulness of the offer, not only its physical materials. The core benefit is the basic need or problem being addressed. For a bus journey, the core benefit is movement to the required destination. For a torch, it is usable light.\n\nThe actual or formal product gives that benefit a particular form. Its features may include design, quality, brand, packaging and other characteristics. An augmented product adds supporting benefits such as installation, warranty or after-sales assistance. These layers are related, but a warranty is not the basic reason light is useful in a dark room.\n\nUtility means want-satisfying ability. Manufacturing can create form utility; transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility; exchange creates possession utility. Physical and psychological satisfaction can accompany a product, but product mix is a firm's range of offerings, not the exact definition of that satisfaction. Where a printed question omits utility or benefit from its options, the omission needs an explicit explanation.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a market into identifiable groups with similar needs or characteristics. A business can then choose target segments and prepare a suitable offer. Common bases include age, income, location, lifestyle and buying behaviour. Segmentation is not merely changing the price, although different segments may receive different legally appropriate offers.\n\nProduct differentiation makes an offer meaningfully different from competing offers through features, design, service or brand image. Positioning concerns the place the offer occupies in the intended customer's mind. Skimming and penetration are pricing strategies, not alternative names for dividing customers into groups.\n\nA marketing orientation starts with identifying and satisfying customer needs while achieving business objectives. A product orientation stresses product characteristics and quality. Knowing customers' desires distinguishes the marketing concept from simply assuming that a technically excellent product will sell itself.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian shoe producer separates school pupils from office workers as customer groups. This is segmentation. Durable soles and an identifiable brand belong to the actual product; repair support adds an augmented benefit. The basic benefit remains suitable footwear.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Core benefit is not identical to brand name.\n• Product mix is a range, not a feeling of satisfaction.\n• Segmentation divides markets; differentiation distinguishes offers.\n• Market skimming sets an introductory price rather than creating customer groups."}},{"id":"securities-dividends-settlement","old":{"title":"Dividends, market settlement and government securities","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. Its holder may receive dividends when lawfully declared and may gain or lose when the share price changes. A bond or debenture normally represents borrowing. Interest is a contractual borrowing cost, whereas an ordinary dividend is not guaranteed simply because a person owns shares.\n\nGilt-edged securities traditionally means government debt regarded as having high credit standing. The description does not mean that a security literally contains gold or cannot change in market value. An irredeemable bond or perpetual security has no ordinary fixed date for repayment of principal, while interest is payable according to its terms. Redeemable borrowing provides for repayment under stated conditions.\n\nCum dividend means that the buyer receives the entitlement to the dividend covered by the quotation. Ex dividend means that the share is sold without that entitlement, which remains with the eligible seller under the relevant record and settlement rules. Do not confuse entitlement to one dividend with transfer of every future ownership benefit.\n\nA broker acts for a client. A jobber or dealer traditionally trades on the dealer's own account. A contract note confirms details of a securities transaction carried out for a client, such as the security, quantity, price and charges. It is different from the transfer documentation used to register ownership or a delivery note for merchandise.\n\nOlder stock-exchange questions use contango and backwardation for settlement postponement charges. In that traditional classroom use, contango concerns a buyer postponing payment; backwardation concerns a seller postponing delivery of securities. Modern finance also uses these words for relationships between futures and spot prices. Identify the historical settlement context before applying a modern price-curve definition.\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission regulates the capital market under the applicable law. The exchange provides the organized market and its trading arrangements. The regulator is not the same organization as a commercial bank, deposit insurer or privatization bureau.\n\nWorked example: shares are bought ex dividend through a broker. The buyer obtains the shares, but not the current dividend excluded by that quotation. The broker supplies a contract note confirming the transaction; the note does not promise that the next market price will rise.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Ex dividend leaves the specified dividend with the eligible seller.\n• Perpetual borrowing still has contractual obligations.\n• A contract note is not an invitation to buy a new issue.\n• Historical settlement charges and modern futures terminology need separate explanations."},"new":{"title":"Dividends, market settlement and government securities","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. Its holder may receive dividends when lawfully declared and may gain or lose when the share price changes. A bond or debenture normally represents borrowing. Interest is a contractual borrowing cost, whereas an ordinary dividend is not guaranteed simply because a person owns shares.\n\nGilt-edged securities traditionally means government debt regarded as having high credit standing. The description does not mean that a security literally contains gold or cannot change in market value. An irredeemable bond or perpetual security has no ordinary fixed date for repayment of principal, while interest is payable according to its terms. Redeemable borrowing provides for repayment under stated conditions.\n\nCum dividend means that the buyer receives the entitlement to the dividend covered by the quotation. Ex dividend means that the share is sold without that entitlement, which remains with the eligible seller under the relevant record and settlement rules. Do not confuse entitlement to one dividend with transfer of every future ownership benefit.\n\nA broker acts for a client. A jobber or dealer traditionally trades on the dealer's own account. A contract note confirms details of a securities transaction carried out for a client, such as the security, quantity, price and charges. It is different from the transfer documentation used to register ownership or a delivery note for merchandise.\n\nOlder stock-exchange questions use contango and backwardation for settlement postponement charges. In that traditional classroom use, contango concerns a buyer postponing payment; backwardation concerns a seller postponing delivery of securities. Modern finance also uses these words for relationships between futures and spot prices. Identify the historical settlement context before applying a modern price-curve definition.\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission regulates the capital market under the applicable law. The exchange provides the organized market and its trading arrangements. The regulator is not the same organization as a commercial bank, deposit insurer or privatization bureau.\n\nWorked example: shares are bought ex dividend through a broker. The buyer obtains the shares, but not the current dividend excluded by that quotation. The broker supplies a contract note confirming the transaction; the note does not promise that the next market price will rise.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Ex dividend leaves the specified dividend with the eligible seller.\n• Perpetual borrowing still has contractual obligations.\n• A contract note is not an invitation to buy a new issue.\n• Historical settlement charges and modern futures terminology need separate explanations."}},{"id":"office-trade-records-adjustments","old":{"title":"Statements of account, quotations and correcting invoices","body":"Trade documents record offers, orders, charges, delivery and payment. Each has a particular purpose. A letter of inquiry asks for information. A quotation answers with prices and relevant terms. An order asks the supplier to provide specified goods. An invoice records the seller's charge for the transaction. Reading the sequence helps identify which party sends each document.\n\nA statement of account summarizes dealings between a supplier and customer for a stated period. It normally shows opening balance, invoices or other debits, payments, credits and closing balance. It helps the parties see how much is owing. It is not identical to a statement of affairs, which concerns financial position in a different accounting context.\n\nA debit note increases or advises a debit in the relevant party's account; classroom invoice-correction questions commonly use a seller's debit note for an undercharge. A credit note reduces the customer's charge, such as for accepted returns or an overcharge. Always identify who issues the note and whose records are being discussed. The label alone should not replace checking the direction of the adjustment.\n\nAn advice note tells a buyer that goods have been dispatched. A delivery note accompanies or records delivery and can be acknowledged by the recipient. It is not usually the same document as the invoice requesting payment. A consignment note concerns goods entrusted to a carrier. A bill of lading has specific maritime receipt, carriage and possible title functions; it is not the name for every road-delivery slip.\n\nA document needed before departure, customs clearance or collection should be selected from the specific stage and authority in the question. A ship's manifest lists cargo; a dock or warehouse warrant concerns stored goods. A bill of sight supplies an initial declaration where the importer cannot yet provide the full description required for a detailed entry.\n\nWorked example: a carton correctly priced at ₦2,000 was invoiced at ₦200. The customer has been undercharged by ₦1,800. A seller's debit note for ₦1,800 corrects the additional charge; a new ₦2,000 charge without cancelling the old entry could overstate the amount due.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An undercharge requires the difference, not the whole price again.\n• A statement of account summarizes transactions rather than replacing every invoice.\n• Advice of dispatch and proof of delivery serve different stages.\n• Check issuer, recipient and timing before choosing a document."},"new":{"title":"Statements of account, quotations and correcting invoices","body":"Trade documents record offers, orders, charges, delivery and payment. Each has a particular purpose. A letter of inquiry asks for information. A quotation answers with prices and relevant terms. An order asks the supplier to provide specified goods. An invoice records the seller's charge for the transaction. Reading the sequence helps identify which party sends each document.\n\nA statement of account summarizes dealings between a supplier and customer for a stated period. It normally shows opening balance, invoices or other debits, payments, credits and closing balance. It helps the parties see how much is owing. It is not identical to a statement of affairs, which concerns financial position in a different accounting context.\n\nA debit note increases or advises a debit in the relevant party's account; classroom invoice-correction questions commonly use a seller's debit note for an undercharge. A credit note reduces the customer's charge, such as for accepted returns or an overcharge. Always identify who issues the note and whose records are being discussed. The label alone should not replace checking the direction of the adjustment.\n\nAn advice note tells a buyer that goods have been dispatched. A delivery note accompanies or records delivery and can be acknowledged by the recipient. It is not usually the same document as the invoice requesting payment. A consignment note concerns goods entrusted to a carrier. A bill of lading has specific maritime receipt, carriage and possible title functions; it is not the name for every road-delivery slip.\n\nA document needed before departure, customs clearance or collection should be selected from the specific stage and authority in the question. A ship's manifest lists cargo; a dock or warehouse warrant concerns stored goods. A bill of sight supplies an initial declaration where the importer cannot yet provide the full description required for a detailed entry.\n\nWorked example: a carton correctly priced at ₦2,000 was invoiced at ₦200. The customer has been undercharged by ₦1,800. A seller's debit note for ₦1,800 corrects the additional charge; a new ₦2,000 charge without cancelling the old entry could overstate the amount due.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An undercharge requires the difference, not the whole price again.\n• A statement of account summarizes transactions rather than replacing every invoice.\n• Advice of dispatch and proof of delivery serve different stages.\n• Check issuer, recipient and timing before choosing a document."}},{"id":"data-retrieval-business-applications","old":{"title":"Stored data, retrieval and business information software","body":"Data are recorded facts that can be processed into useful information. A computer accepts input, processes instructions and data, stores material and produces output. Hardware is the physical equipment; software consists of programs. An input device sends data into the system, while an output device presents results. The processor's control functions do not make it a long-term store for all business records.\n\nSecondary storage keeps files for later use. Hard disks, solid-state drives, optical discs and older floppy disks are examples. A DVD is an optical storage medium. A floppy disk is removable magnetic storage used in older systems. Both a hard disk and a suitable removable disk can hold information that can later be retrieved, so an unspecified question about retrieving information may not distinguish them sufficiently.\n\nRetrieval means locating and obtaining stored information. Downloading normally means receiving data from a remote system into a local system. Uploading sends local data to a remote system. Data transfer is the wider idea and can occur between two computers in several directions. Copying a file is not decryption, which concerns reversing encryption under the relevant method or key.\n\nA database organizes related records and supports searches, updates and reports. A business may store customers, stock and transactions in related tables. A database application can provide information useful to management, but a management information system includes people, procedures, data and processing arrangements rather than one program alone.\n\nA spreadsheet organizes cells and performs calculations; a word processor prepares text documents. Design applications help produce graphics or technical drawings. Select the application by the task. Do not call a drawing package the usual database merely because the completed drawing contains information.\n\nAccess security includes suitable passwords, permissions and other authentication arrangements. An email address identifies an account but is not by itself proof of authority to view confidential records. Sharing a password weakens the protection even if the storage device itself works correctly.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos wholesaler stores customer records in a database, retrieves unpaid invoices for a report and downloads a bank statement through an authorized account. The hard drive stores files; the database manages records; the download describes the direction of transfer.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A storage medium and an input device have different roles.\n• Retrieval is wider than downloading.\n• DVD is not an Internet protocol.\n• Possessing an email address does not automatically grant access to another person's data."},"new":{"title":"Stored data, retrieval and business information software","body":"Data are recorded facts that can be processed into useful information. A computer accepts input, processes instructions and data, stores material and produces output. Hardware is the physical equipment; software consists of programs. An input device sends data into the system, while an output device presents results. The processor's control functions do not make it a long-term store for all business records.\n\nSecondary storage keeps files for later use. Hard disks, solid-state drives, optical discs and older floppy disks are examples. A DVD is an optical storage medium. A floppy disk is removable magnetic storage used in older systems. Both a hard disk and a suitable removable disk can hold information that can later be retrieved, so an unspecified question about retrieving information may not distinguish them sufficiently.\n\nRetrieval means locating and obtaining stored information. Downloading normally means receiving data from a remote system into a local system. Uploading sends local data to a remote system. Data transfer is the wider idea and can occur between two computers in several directions. Copying a file is not decryption, which concerns reversing encryption under the relevant method or key.\n\nA database organizes related records and supports searches, updates and reports. A business may store customers, stock and transactions in related tables. A database application can provide information useful to management, but a management information system includes people, procedures, data and processing arrangements rather than one program alone.\n\nA spreadsheet organizes cells and performs calculations; a word processor prepares text documents. Design applications help produce graphics or technical drawings. Select the application by the task. Do not call a drawing package the usual database merely because the completed drawing contains information.\n\nAccess security includes suitable passwords, permissions and other authentication arrangements. An email address identifies an account but is not by itself proof of authority to view confidential records. Sharing a password weakens the protection even if the storage device itself works correctly.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos wholesaler stores customer records in a database, retrieves unpaid invoices for a report and downloads a bank statement through an authorized account. The hard drive stores files; the database manages records; the download describes the direction of transfer.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A storage medium and an input device have different roles.\n• Retrieval is wider than downloading.\n• DVD is not an Internet protocol.\n• Possessing an email address does not automatically grant access to another person's data."}},{"id":"professional-consumer-regulation","old":{"title":"Professional integrity, consumer dialogue and public duties","body":"A professional body organizes people practising a recognized occupation. It can promote training, ethical standards and the profession's interests. A trade association represents members in a particular trade or industry. A chamber of commerce has a broader business membership. These bodies should not be confused with every statutory regulator simply because they all protect standards in some way.\n\nProfessional integrity means acting honestly and maintaining the duties expected of the occupation. A lawyer who defrauds a client damages trust in legal services. Discipline is intended to protect the public and uphold professional standards, not merely protect a wrongdoer from the client. The legal authority to investigate, discipline or remove a practitioner must be checked under the applicable professional law; membership of an association alone does not describe every legal step.\n\nIn Nigerian legal questions, references to the Nigerian Bar Association may use a simplified classroom description of professional discipline. Explain that legal disciplinary institutions and procedures determine formal sanctions. Do not teach that any association can remove a person's legal right to practise solely by an informal vote.\n\nThe Nigerian Communications Commission regulates the communications sector under its legal framework. Consumer dialogue, complaints channels and public engagement can help users understand services and bring problems to the regulator's attention. A consumers' parliament is intended to protect and inform consumers and obtain feedback, not simply increase a service provider's profit or give government a commercial dividend.\n\nConsumer protection includes safety, accurate information, fair treatment and redress. Regulators, businesses, professional bodies and consumer organizations have different responsibilities. Providing a discussion forum does not transfer every duty of service quality from the provider to the customer.\n\nSocial responsibility also concerns effects on the host community and the natural environment. Safe waste disposal, support for community facilities and responsible conduct can overlap in their benefits. Environmental responsibility is a specific aspect of wider social responsibility; it is not automatically false merely because a question offers the wider label.\n\nWorked example: a consumer reports repeated unexplained charges at a regulator's engagement meeting. The complaint concerns consumer protection and service accountability. It is not a share subscription or a tax payment to government.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An association and a statutory regulator need not have identical powers.\n• Protecting professional integrity does not mean concealing misconduct.\n• Consumer dialogue is not merely advertising for providers.\n• Ethical responsibility can overlap with specific legal duties without replacing them."},"new":{"title":"Professional integrity, consumer dialogue and public duties","body":"A professional body organizes people practising a recognized occupation. It can promote training, ethical standards and the profession's interests. A trade association represents members in a particular trade or industry. A chamber of commerce has a broader business membership. These bodies should not be confused with every statutory regulator simply because they all protect standards in some way.\n\nProfessional integrity means acting honestly and maintaining the duties expected of the occupation. A lawyer who defrauds a client damages trust in legal services. Discipline is intended to protect the public and uphold professional standards, not merely protect a wrongdoer from the client. The legal authority to investigate, discipline or remove a practitioner must be checked under the applicable professional law; membership of an association alone does not describe every legal step.\n\nIn Nigerian legal questions, references to the Nigerian Bar Association may use a simplified classroom description of professional discipline. Explain that legal disciplinary institutions and procedures determine formal sanctions. Do not teach that any association can remove a person's legal right to practise solely by an informal vote.\n\nThe Nigerian Communications Commission regulates the communications sector under its legal framework. Consumer dialogue, complaints channels and public engagement can help users understand services and bring problems to the regulator's attention. A consumers' parliament is intended to protect and inform consumers and obtain feedback, not simply increase a service provider's profit or give government a commercial dividend.\n\nConsumer protection includes safety, accurate information, fair treatment and redress. Regulators, businesses, professional bodies and consumer organizations have different responsibilities. Providing a discussion forum does not transfer every duty of service quality from the provider to the customer.\n\nSocial responsibility also concerns effects on the host community and the natural environment. Safe waste disposal, support for community facilities and responsible conduct can overlap in their benefits. Environmental responsibility is a specific aspect of wider social responsibility; it is not automatically false merely because a question offers the wider label.\n\nWorked example: a consumer reports repeated unexplained charges at a regulator's engagement meeting. The complaint concerns consumer protection and service accountability. It is not a share subscription or a tax payment to government.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An association and a statutory regulator need not have identical powers.\n• Protecting professional integrity does not mean concealing misconduct.\n• Consumer dialogue is not merely advertising for providers.\n• Ethical responsibility can overlap with specific legal duties without replacing them."}},{"id":"money-acceptability-token-value","old":{"title":"Money, legal acceptance and token value","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. General acceptability distinguishes money from an object that only one seller happens to desire. A medium of exchange passes between buyers and sellers. A unit of account or measure of value expresses prices in a common standard. A store of value carries purchasing power forward, while a standard of deferred payment states obligations due later.\n\nThese functions are related but different. A ₦5,000 price label uses the naira as a measure of value. Paying that amount uses money as a medium of exchange. Keeping money for a later purchase uses its store-of-value function, although inflation can reduce its purchasing power. Describing money simply as a means of settlement emphasizes the payment function rather than one physical material.\n\nUseful qualities include acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility, recognizability and reasonable stability of value. Relative scarcity means supply is not unlimited compared with demand. It does not mean that every rare object is money. Homogeneity means units of the same denomination and issue have the required uniform quality, not that every denomination has the same value.\n\nLegal tender has legal status for settling relevant debts under applicable conditions. Legal backing can support confidence, but market acceptance, institutional trust and reliable payment arrangements also matter. A governor's signature alone is not a complete economic explanation of why money is accepted. Forgery remains possible even where law prohibits it; security design and verification are also needed.\n\nToken money has a face value greater than the value of its material content. Commodity money derives substantial value from the commodity itself. A banknote's usefulness as money is not measured simply by the resale price of its paper. Different historical systems use different forms, so avoid assuming that every monetary instrument is a metal coin.\n\nWorked example: a coin accepted for ₦100 contains metal worth less than ₦100. It illustrates token money. A trader compares prices in naira, then receives payment by an approved banking channel. The currency unit and the payment mechanism are connected but different ideas.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Scarcity alone does not establish money.\n• Divisibility is a desirable quality; indivisibility is not.\n• Face value differs from metal value.\n• Legal acceptance does not make counterfeiting physically impossible."},"new":{"title":"Money, legal acceptance and token value","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. General acceptability distinguishes money from an object that only one seller happens to desire. A medium of exchange passes between buyers and sellers. A unit of account or measure of value expresses prices in a common standard. A store of value carries purchasing power forward, while a standard of deferred payment states obligations due later.\n\nThese functions are related but different. A ₦5,000 price label uses the naira as a measure of value. Paying that amount uses money as a medium of exchange. Keeping money for a later purchase uses its store-of-value function, although inflation can reduce its purchasing power. Describing money simply as a means of settlement emphasizes the payment function rather than one physical material.\n\nUseful qualities include acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility, recognizability and reasonable stability of value. Relative scarcity means supply is not unlimited compared with demand. It does not mean that every rare object is money. Homogeneity means units of the same denomination and issue have the required uniform quality, not that every denomination has the same value.\n\nLegal tender has legal status for settling relevant debts under applicable conditions. Legal backing can support confidence, but market acceptance, institutional trust and reliable payment arrangements also matter. A governor's signature alone is not a complete economic explanation of why money is accepted. Forgery remains possible even where law prohibits it; security design and verification are also needed.\n\nToken money has a face value greater than the value of its material content. Commodity money derives substantial value from the commodity itself. A banknote's usefulness as money is not measured simply by the resale price of its paper. Different historical systems use different forms, so avoid assuming that every monetary instrument is a metal coin.\n\nWorked example: a coin accepted for ₦100 contains metal worth less than ₦100. It illustrates token money. A trader compares prices in naira, then receives payment by an approved banking channel. The currency unit and the payment mechanism are connected but different ideas.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Scarcity alone does not establish money.\n• Divisibility is a desirable quality; indivisibility is not.\n• Face value differs from metal value.\n• Legal acceptance does not make counterfeiting physically impossible."}},{"id":"company-documents-share-rights","old":{"title":"Company constitutions, share offers and shareholder rights","body":"A company is a legal person distinct from its shareholders once properly incorporated. Its constitutional documents explain its framework and internal arrangements under the law applying at the time. Historical Nigerian Commerce questions commonly distinguish the memorandum of association from the articles of association. Read the era of the question rather than assuming every older document requirement remains unchanged.\n\nThe memorandum traditionally states matters such as the company's name, objects, registered-office framework, liability and capital provisions. The articles concern internal management and the rights and procedures relevant to members and officers. A question about detailed shareholder rights is therefore usually testing articles rather than an objects clause. Neither document is simply a price list for goods sold by the company.\n\nA prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation under the applicable rules. It can describe the securities offered and other material details. An invoice demands payment for a commercial transaction; an indent orders goods. They are not substitutes for an investment prospectus merely because each is a written business document.\n\nAuthorized or nominal capital is the historic maximum share capital permitted under the relevant constitutional framework. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares issued or offered and allotted under the terminology used. Called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. A part payment is not automatically the same thing as the amount called; a subscriber can owe an unpaid call.\n\nThe portion not issued is unissued capital. Reserved or reserve capital has a narrower traditional meaning associated with capital not callable except under specified winding-up conditions. Some papers use that term loosely, and the explanation must distinguish their intended calculation from the precise category.\n\nOrdinary shareholders own the company subject to its legal structure. Debenture holders are creditors. In a winding up, creditor and shareholder priorities depend on security, statutory rules and class rights. It is unsafe to claim that shareholders must always receive payment before every lender.\n\nWorked example: a company has historic authorized capital of ₦1 million, issues ₦800,000, calls ₦600,000 and receives ₦500,000. The four figures answer different questions. Cash received is ₦500,000, while the amount demanded is ₦600,000.\n\nIts separate legal personality permits a company to sue and be sued in its own name.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A memorandum's broad framework differs from internal articles.\n• A prospectus invites investment, not delivery of merchandise.\n• Called-up and paid-up amounts need not match.\n• Historical capital terminology needs its stated legal context."},"new":{"title":"Company constitutions, share offers and shareholder rights","body":"A company is a legal person distinct from its shareholders once properly incorporated. Its constitutional documents explain its framework and internal arrangements under the law applying at the time. Historical Nigerian Commerce questions commonly distinguish the memorandum of association from the articles of association. Read the era of the question rather than assuming every older document requirement remains unchanged.\n\nThe memorandum traditionally states matters such as the company's name, objects, registered-office framework, liability and capital provisions. The articles concern internal management and the rights and procedures relevant to members and officers. A question about detailed shareholder rights is therefore usually testing articles rather than an objects clause. Neither document is simply a price list for goods sold by the company.\n\nA prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation under the applicable rules. It can describe the securities offered and other material details. An invoice demands payment for a commercial transaction; an indent orders goods. They are not substitutes for an investment prospectus merely because each is a written business document.\n\nAuthorized or nominal capital is the historic maximum share capital permitted under the relevant constitutional framework. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares issued or offered and allotted under the terminology used. Called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. A part payment is not automatically the same thing as the amount called; a subscriber can owe an unpaid call.\n\nThe portion not issued is unissued capital. Reserved or reserve capital has a narrower traditional meaning associated with capital not callable except under specified winding-up conditions. Some papers use that term loosely, and the explanation must distinguish their intended calculation from the precise category.\n\nOrdinary shareholders own the company subject to its legal structure. Debenture holders are creditors. In a winding up, creditor and shareholder priorities depend on security, statutory rules and class rights. It is unsafe to claim that shareholders must always receive payment before every lender.\n\nWorked example: a company has historic authorized capital of ₦1 million, issues ₦800,000, calls ₦600,000 and receives ₦500,000. The four figures answer different questions. Cash received is ₦500,000, while the amount demanded is ₦600,000.\n\nIts separate legal personality permits a company to sue and be sued in its own name.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A memorandum's broad framework differs from internal articles.\n• A prospectus invites investment, not delivery of merchandise.\n• Called-up and paid-up amounts need not match.\n• Historical capital terminology needs its stated legal context."}},{"id":"postal-delivery-services","old":{"title":"Postal services, proof of delivery and travellers’ mail","body":"Postal services collect, carry and deliver letters and other permitted items. Different services meet different needs. A sender should consider urgency, value, destination, proof of posting and delivery arrangements. A postal order transfers a specified value under its terms; it is not the service used to carry an urgent bundle of legal documents.\n\nOrdinary mail provides the standard collection and delivery arrangement. Registered mail adds recorded handling and other safeguards under the postal operator's conditions. It can provide evidence of posting and delivery or collection. Recorded delivery emphasizes an acknowledgment or record of delivery under the relevant service rules. Exact names and guarantees differ by operator and period, so read the historical question carefully.\n\nA certificate of posting establishes that an item was handed in for posting. It does not by itself prove that the addressee received the contents. Express services give priority or expedited handling. A courier provides a dedicated document or parcel delivery service, often with tracking and clear delivery arrangements. Speed alone does not establish the extent of insurance or compensation if an item is lost.\n\nPoste restante means mail held at a designated post office for collection by the addressee. It suits a traveller who has no fixed delivery address in the town. The recipient must follow the operator's identification and collection rules. A private mail bag or post-office box is a different continuing arrangement for receiving mail, not another name for every traveller's collection service.\n\nA parcel post service carries permitted parcels rather than defining every letter service. Surface and air refer to transport arrangements; registration or express handling concerns a service feature. An item can therefore be registered and carried by air without the words describing competing categories.\n\nWorked example: a visitor to Kano gives correspondents a suitable poste restante address and collects letters from the designated post office. A law firm sending an urgent signed agreement instead chooses a suitable tracked courier. A receipt proving posting helps the sender but does not itself prove delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Poste restante concerns collection without a fixed local address.\n• A certificate of posting is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n• Courier service and a postal order perform different tasks.\n• Urgency, transport method and evidence of delivery are separate considerations."},"new":{"title":"Postal services, proof of delivery and travellers’ mail","body":"Postal services collect, carry and deliver letters and other permitted items. Different services meet different needs. A sender should consider urgency, value, destination, proof of posting and delivery arrangements. A postal order transfers a specified value under its terms; it is not the service used to carry an urgent bundle of legal documents.\n\nOrdinary mail provides the standard collection and delivery arrangement. Registered mail adds recorded handling and other safeguards under the postal operator's conditions. It can provide evidence of posting and delivery or collection. Recorded delivery emphasizes an acknowledgment or record of delivery under the relevant service rules. Exact names and guarantees differ by operator and period, so read the historical question carefully.\n\nA certificate of posting establishes that an item was handed in for posting. It does not by itself prove that the addressee received the contents. Express services give priority or expedited handling. A courier provides a dedicated document or parcel delivery service, often with tracking and clear delivery arrangements. Speed alone does not establish the extent of insurance or compensation if an item is lost.\n\nPoste restante means mail held at a designated post office for collection by the addressee. It suits a traveller who has no fixed delivery address in the town. The recipient must follow the operator's identification and collection rules. A private mail bag or post-office box is a different continuing arrangement for receiving mail, not another name for every traveller's collection service.\n\nA parcel post service carries permitted parcels rather than defining every letter service. Surface and air refer to transport arrangements; registration or express handling concerns a service feature. An item can therefore be registered and carried by air without the words describing competing categories.\n\nWorked example: a visitor to Kano gives correspondents a suitable poste restante address and collects letters from the designated post office. A law firm sending an urgent signed agreement instead chooses a suitable tracked courier. A receipt proving posting helps the sender but does not itself prove delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Poste restante concerns collection without a fixed local address.\n• A certificate of posting is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n• Courier service and a postal order perform different tasks.\n• Urgency, transport method and evidence of delivery are separate considerations."}},{"id":"communication-forms-media","old":{"title":"Forms of communication and choosing business media","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. Its success depends on the intended meaning being understood, not merely on a message leaving a machine. Business communication supports orders, instructions, payments, reports, complaints and coordination among people who may be in different places.\n\nVerbal communication uses words. It can be oral, such as speech in a meeting, or written, such as an email or letter. Non-verbal communication uses other signals, including gestures, facial expressions, diagrams or warning lights. A written message is still verbal in this classification because it uses words. Do not assume that verbal always means spoken when the options contrast verbal with non-verbal.\n\nA different classification divides messages into oral and written forms. Both are useful classroom categories, but they do not exclude every non-verbal sign. A question asking for the two main forms needs to identify the intended classification. Where the options supply both pairs, explain the level being classified instead of teaching that diagrams and gestures do not communicate.\n\nCommunication can reduce the expense and risk of travel by allowing instructions and information to move without every participant moving. It connects suppliers and customers and helps coordinate the delivery of goods. These are direct business benefits; communication does not by itself guarantee wealth or replace the actual transport of physical goods.\n\nThe medium should suit the task. Speech permits quick clarification; written messages can provide a lasting record. Radio reaches listeners through sound, while television combines sound and images. Print advertising ordinarily requires reading for its textual content, though pictures may also convey meaning. Confidential instructions and public advertising require different choices of audience and channel.\n\nWorked example: a Jos wholesaler emails a stock order and telephones to clarify the delivery time. Both messages are verbal: one is written and the other oral. A warehouse warning light is non-verbal. The exchange can save a journey while the goods themselves still need transport.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Written words can be verbal communication.\n• An email and a fax are channels or technologies, not the only two forms of communication.\n• A broad communication benefit does not remove the need for physical delivery.\n• Select the classification and medium from the precise question."},"new":{"title":"Forms of communication and choosing business media","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. Its success depends on the intended meaning being understood, not merely on a message leaving a machine. Business communication supports orders, instructions, payments, reports, complaints and coordination among people who may be in different places.\n\nVerbal communication uses words. It can be oral, such as speech in a meeting, or written, such as an email or letter. Non-verbal communication uses other signals, including gestures, facial expressions, diagrams or warning lights. A written message is still verbal in this classification because it uses words. Do not assume that verbal always means spoken when the options contrast verbal with non-verbal.\n\nA different classification divides messages into oral and written forms. Both are useful classroom categories, but they do not exclude every non-verbal sign. A question asking for the two main forms needs to identify the intended classification. Where the options supply both pairs, explain the level being classified instead of teaching that diagrams and gestures do not communicate.\n\nCommunication can reduce the expense and risk of travel by allowing instructions and information to move without every participant moving. It connects suppliers and customers and helps coordinate the delivery of goods. These are direct business benefits; communication does not by itself guarantee wealth or replace the actual transport of physical goods.\n\nThe medium should suit the task. Speech permits quick clarification; written messages can provide a lasting record. Radio reaches listeners through sound, while television combines sound and images. Print advertising ordinarily requires reading for its textual content, though pictures may also convey meaning. Confidential instructions and public advertising require different choices of audience and channel.\n\nWorked example: a Jos wholesaler emails a stock order and telephones to clarify the delivery time. Both messages are verbal: one is written and the other oral. A warehouse warning light is non-verbal. The exchange can save a journey while the goods themselves still need transport.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Written words can be verbal communication.\n• An email and a fax are channels or technologies, not the only two forms of communication.\n• A broad communication benefit does not remove the need for physical delivery.\n• Select the classification and medium from the precise question."}}];
